1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Praejecta or Praiecta was a niece to Byzantine emperor Justinian I (r. 527–565) by blood and of his empress Theodora by marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [hide]  1 Biography 2 References 2.1 Citations 2.2 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Praejecta was a daughter of Vigilantia and Dulcidio (or Dulcissimus), respectively the sister and brother-in-law of Emperor Justinian. She was also a sister of the later Byzantine emperor Justin II (r. 565–578) and the patricius M
2 CONC arcellus.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was initially married to the patricius Areobindus, a senator of noble birth.[2] In 545, as the situation in Africa had gotten out of control following the death of Solomon and his replacement by his incompetent nephew Sergius,
2 CONC  Areobindus, although having no prior military experience, was dispatched there with a small force.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Areobindus was placed as joint commander with Sergius, but the two generals did not agree, with predictably disastrous results for the Byzantine imperial effort: the imperial forces were severely defeated at Thacia, although they 
2 CONC managed to kill the rebel Stotzas. Following this, Sergius was relieved and Areobindus was installed in his stead.[4][5] Soon, however, (March 546) he was murdered in a military mutiny led by Guntharic, the dux Numidiae. Praejecta
2 CONC  and her sister-in-law had been sent to safety to a fortified monastery in Carthage, but when Guntharic took over the city, they were removed from there. Guntharic, who probably intended to marry Praejecta, kept her under house ar
2 CONC rest but treated her well.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Guntharic's assassination by the Armenian Artabanes in May and the restoration of Byzantine imperial rule, Praejecta rewarded him with a large sum of money and became engaged to him. Once she was back at Constantinople, Arta
2 CONC banes followed, but Empress Theodora discovered that he was already married and forbade the union.[5][7] Artabanes was enraged by this affair, and it contributed to his involvement in the failed conspiracy to overthrow Emperor Jus
2 CONC tinian in late 548.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Praejecta was instead married off to John, son of Pompeius and Anastasia, a marriage which took place sometime in 546–548.[9] Pompeius was a son of Secundinus, consul in 511 and Caesaria. His mother was a sister of a previous Byza
2 CONC ntine emperor, Anastasius I (r. 491–518). Pompeius and his brother Hypatius were executed in 532 for their role in the Nika riots.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Praejecta
