1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT "of Athens"; Empress of Rome. one of the last pagans who taught rhetoric at
2 CONT Athens.2  Also called Athena‹s.She married Theodosius II, imperator, son of
2 CONT Flavius Arcadius Augustus, imperator and Aelia Eudoxia des Francs, on 7 June
2 CONT 421; The marriage was arrainged by Theodosius' sister, Plechtrude.4,2 She gave
2 CONT the Jews permission to pray on Temple Mount on holy days other than the Ninth
2 CONT of Av in 438.1 She made her first pilgrimage to Jerusalem; on the way she
2 CONT stopped at Antioch and made a speech with a quotation from Homer that greatly
2 CONT delighted the citizens-so much so that they set up a golden statue in her, in
2 CONT 438.2 She dedicated a small shrine to "St. Stephen" outside the northern gate
2 CONT of Jerusalem on 15 May 439 in Palestine.1 She fell into disgrace through an
2 CONT unjust suspicion of infidelity with Paulinos, the "Master of the Offices";
2 CONT Paulinos was murdered and Eudocia banished in 441.2 She returned to Jerusalem
2 CONT to remain in 442.2 She became, for a time, an ardent Monophysite between 442
2 CONT and 453.2 She permitted Temple reconstruction, but the effort failed in 443 in
2 CONT Jerusalem, Palestine.1 She quarrelled with her sister-in-law, Pulcheria, and
2 CONT was banished to Jerusalem where she became the ruler in 444 in Palestine.1 She
2 CONT built many new churches and hospices in and around Jerusalem, and together
2 CONT with bishop Juvenal of Jerusalem, supported the monophysite Christians
2 CONT (represented in Jerusalem today by Coptic, Syrian Jacobites, Armenian, and
2 CONT Ethiopian Christians) after 444 in Palestine.1 She returned to the Catholic
2 CONT faith after Pope Leo of Rome wrote to convert her in 453.2 She consulted the
2 CONT Syrian ascetic Simeon Stylites, after the death of her husband, over the
2 CONT disunity of Christendom, and referred to the Armenian monastic leader,
2 CONT Euthymius, who convinced her on the Orthodox theology, and therefore the
2 CONT Orthodox patriarch Anastasius became archbishop of Jerusalem in 457 in
2 CONT Palestine.1 She died in 461 in Jerusalem.3,5 Empress Aelia Eudokia of Athens
2 CONT was buried in the church of St. Stephen, Jerusalem. She was buried in the
2 CONT church of St. Stephen, built by her outside the northern gate.2
