1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Roman Emperor; came from Naissus (modern Nis, Yugos.) in the province of
2 CONT Moesia.1 He was one of the most important magistri militum of Honorius after
2 CONT the death of Stilicho in 408.2 He helped the western Roman emperor Flavius
2 CONT Honorius, overthrow the usurping emperor Constantine (Flavius Claudius
2 CONT Constantinus) in 411 in Arelate (Arles), (France).1 Magister militum ("master
2 CONT of the soldiers") in 411.  He and Galla Placidia the elder, augusta became
2 CONT involved -- on the losing side -- in the controversy over the election of a
2 CONT new bishop of Rome in 419. He was Catholic. He was so described: a fierce
2 CONT warrior in public, but he was far more relaxed in private and at banquets.2 He
2 CONT married the sister of the Western Emperor Honorius, and served as co-Emperor
2 CONT with him for 7 months until his death in 421. He was appointed coemperor of
2 CONT the West by Honorius, with the title augustus, on 8 February 421.1 Augustus in
2 CONT Western Roman Empire, between 8 February 421 and 2 September 421.
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~cousin/html/p170.htm#i10622
1 NOTE Ruler of the Roman empire in 0411.
1 NOTE EMPORER OF THE WEST 421
1 NOTE Flavius Constantius (d. 2 September 421), whose name is traditionally anglicized as Constantius III, was a late Roman general, politician, and emperor. He was the power behind the throne for much of the 410s, and in 421 briefly be
2 CONC came co-emperor of the Western Empire with Honorius.
1 NOTE Constantius III (died 421), Western Roman emperor (421). A general in the 
2 CONT service of the Western emperor Honorius (384-423), Constantius became 
2 CONT virtual ruler of the western provinces in 414, when he forced the 
2 CONT Visigoths out of Gaul into Spain. In 417 he married Honorius's sister, 
2 CONT Galla Placidia. The next year he recalled the Visigoths from Spain and 
2 CONT established a kingdom for them in southern Gaul under their ruler Wallia 
2 CONT (reigned 415-19). During the last year of his life, Constantius was 
2 CONT officially recognized by Honorius as co-emperor.
