2 CONT Flavius Ardabur Aspar (c. 400 – 471) was an Alan patrician and magister militum ("Master of soldiers") of the Eastern Roman Empire. Aspar's family exerted a great influence on the Eastern Roman Emperors for half a century, from th
2 CONC e 420s to his death in 471, over Theodosius II, Marcian and Leo I, who, in the end, had him killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of the magister Ardaburius[1], Aspar played a crucial role in his father's expedition in 424 to defeat the western usurper, Joannes of Ravenna, and to install Galla Placidia and her son, Valentinian III, in his place. He also 
2 CONC helped to negotiate a peace treaty with Geiseric after the Vandal invasion of Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aspar attained the consulship in 434 after campaigning in Africa.[2] However, Aspar could not become emperor because of his Arian religion. Instead, he played the role of kingmaker with his subordinate, Marcian, who became emperor
2 CONC  by marrying Theodosius II's sister Pulcheria.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 January 457 Marcian died, and the political and military characters of the Eastern court took eleven days to chose a successor. Despite the presence of a strong candidate to the purple, the magister militum and Marcian's son
2 CONC -in-law Anthemius, the choice was quite different. Aspar, who in this occasion was probably offered the throne by the Senate but refused,[3] could have chosen his own son Ardabur, but instead selected an obscure tribune of one of 
2 CONC his military units, Leo I.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 470, in an episode of the struggle for power between Aspar and the Isaurian general Zeno, Aspar persuaded the emperor to appoint his second son, Julius Patricius, as caesar and give him in marriage his daughter Leontia. However
2 CONC , as for the clergy and people of Constantinople an Arian was not eligible to become an emperor, at the news of the appointment riots broke out in the city hippodrome, led by the head of the Sleepless Monks, Marcellus: Aspar and L
2 CONC eo had to promise to the bishops that Patricius would convert to Orthodoxy before becoming emperor, and only after the conversion he would have married Leontia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 471 an imperial conspiracy caused the death of Aspar and of his elder son Ardabur: it is possible that Patricius died on this occasion, although some sources report that he recovered from his wounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aspar was the teacher of Theodoric the Great, who later became king of the Ostrogoths. Aspar had another son, Ermanaric, with the sister of Theodoric Strabo.[5] Aspar's wife was an Ostrogoth, as the Ostrogoth King Theodoric was he
2 CONC r nephew.[2] A cistern attributed to him still exists today in Istanbul.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavius Ardabur Aspar
2 CONT
2 CONT Born:  abt. 405    Died:  471 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife/Partner:         (missing) 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Child:         Godisthea 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Flavius Ardabur of the Alans 
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
