2 CONC ark on the Empire.
2 CONT Sources and Bibliography There are a number of sources that treat the age of Arcadius. The historians Zosimus (New History), Socrates (Ecclesiastical History) and Sozomon (Ecclesiastical History) offer the most complete accounts. 
2 CONC Additionally, Bishop Synesius of Cyrene in letters and other works (On the Kingship and On Providence) specifically addresses a number of the problems and issues going on in Constantinople and the imperial court at that time. The 
2 CONC letters and homilies of John Chrysostom are also of enormous value and tell us more about the social history of the capital than any other source. Minor accounts include the Chronicon Paschale and the Chronicle of Marcellinus Come
2 CONC s. Several important saints' lives, including the Life of Porphyry, provide valuable information about events outside the capital. Finally, for the military and political organization of the Roman Empire, the Notitia Dignitatum of
2 CONC fers a static look at the early fifth century.
2 CONT Cameron, Alan, and Long, Jacqueline (1993) Barbarians and Politics at the Court of Arcadius (Berkeley).
2 CONT Demougeot, Emilienne (1951), De l'unité‚ … la division de l'empire romain, 395-410: Essai sur la government impérial (Paris).
2 CONT
2 CONT Holum, Kenneth (1982), Theodosian Empresses (Berkeley).
2 CONT Jones, A.H.M. et al. (1970), The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire, vol. 1 (Cambridge).
2 CONT Liebeschuetz, J.H.W.G. (1991), Barbarians and Bishops. Army, Church, and State in the Age of Arcadius and Chrysostom (Oxford).
2 CONT Martindale, J.R. (1980), The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire, vol. 2 (Cambridge).
2 CONT Seeck, Otto (1896), "Arcadius," in RE, v. 2 (Berlin), 1137-53.
2 CONT Van Ommeslaeghe, F. (1979) "Jean Chrysostome en conflit avec l'impératrice Eudoxie," Analecta Bollandiana 97, 131-59.
