2 CONT (On Ataulf's inlaws): Brother and sister, parents not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ALARIC (-Bruttium, southern Italy end 410).  
2 CONT
2 CONT First mentioned in 391 when he moved southwards into Greece, he suffered several military setbacks but succeeded in resettling the Goths in Macedonia[18].  He was elected in 395 as ALARIC King of the Visigoths.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He entered the western part of the Roman Empire in 401, and by the end of the year had attacked Venetia and was threatening Milan.  Alaric was defeated by the Romans in 402 at Pollenza, and in 403 at Verona, after which the Goths 
2 CONC abandoned Italy and settled in Dalmatia and Pannonia[19].  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric returned to Italy in 408, the Goths' attacks culminating in the sack of Rome in Aug 410[20].  Procopius records that Alaric died from illness[21].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name(s) of King Alaric's wife or wives is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric had children, including a daughter who married Theoderic I King of the Visigoths.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alaric's sister: m ([410]) as his --- wife, ATAULF, son of --- (-murdered Barcelona 416).  He succeeded his brother-in-law in 410 as ATAULF King of the Visigoths. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT (On Ataulf/Adolphus' birth family) Two brothers, parents not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  ATAULF, son of --- (-murdered Barcelona [Aug/Sep] 416).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Ataulfo" as "eius [Alaricus rex Vesegotharum] consanguineo" but does not specify the precise relationship[23].  He is first mentioned in 408 in relation to a military campaign in upper Pannonia[24].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his brother-in-law in 410 as ATAULF King of the Visigoths.  Procopius records that Ataulf succeeded on the death of Alaric[25].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that the Goths left Italy during the reign of “Ataulphus” and occupied “Gallias, ac postea Hispanias”, adding that Ataulf reigned for six years[26].  He led his people out of Italy and esta
2 CONC blished his court at Narbonne[27].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He crossed the Pyrenees into Spain from the Visigoth base in Toulouse with a small army, charged by the enfeebled Roman State in Hispania with expelling the Vandal invaders from the south and the Suevi from the north west[28].  Th
2 CONC ey occupied Barcelona in 414[29].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was assassinated by Dubius or Eberwolf in revenge for the earlier killing of Sarus, the brother of Ataulf's successor Sigeric[30].  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Ataulfus” was murdered at Barcelona “per quemdam 
2 CONC Gothum…inter familiars fabulas” in 416[31].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.  The name of Ataulf's first wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([410]) --- of the Visigoths, sister of ALARIC King of the Visigoths, daughter of ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m thirdly (Narbonne 1 Jan 414) as her first husband, GALLA PLACIDIA, daughter of Emperor THEODOSIUS I & his second wife Galla ([388/early May 394][32]-27 Nov 450).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Placidiam" as the daughter of Emperor Theodosius & his second wife, recording in a later passage that she was captured by "Halaricus rex Vesegotharum" when he attacked Rome and later married his successor "Atauulfo
2 CONC "[33].  The Chronicle of Marcellinus also records that "Placidia Honorii principis sorore" was abducted by "Halaricus" and later married "Athaulfo propinquo suo"[34].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Captured by Alaric King of the Visigoths during the sack of Rome in Aug 409, she passed to Ataulf on his accession as king[35].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Ataulfus” married “Placidiam” at Narbonne in 414[36].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first husband married her after failing to establish an alliance with Emperor Honorius[37].  
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of the peace negotiated by King Walia with the Romans in 416, Galla Placidia was returned to her brother Honorius in early 416[38].  The Chronicon Albeldense names “Ballia” as successor of “Sigericus”, adding that he made 
2 CONC peace with Emperor Honorius and returned his sister Placidia to him[39].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married secondly (1 Jan 417) Flavius Constantius, who succeeded in 421 as Emperor CONSTANTIUS III.  Iordanes records that Placidia was created "Augustam" and her son Valentinian "Cæsar" to lead the opposition to Iohannes who i
2 CONC nvaded the western empire[40].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records the death in 450 of “Valentiniani Imperatoris mater Placidia…apud Romam”[41].  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ataulf & his first wife had [six ] (this is a mistranslation of Latin into Greek; "Adaulphi ei priore conjuge lebieros vi " which means "Ataulf had children with his former wife"  The translator read "vi" as VI. We do not kno
2 CONC w how many children he had.
2 CONT
2 CONT )         the children (-murdered Barcelona [Aug/Sep] 415).  Sigesar, Bishop of the Goths, tried in vain to protect these children after their father's death[43]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ataulf & his third wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         THEODOSIUS (Barcelona end 414-Barcelona before Aug 415). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         brother .  Designated by King Ataulf as his successor, but displaced by Sigeric[44]. 
2 CONT
