1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richomer (Flavius Richomeres among the Romans), who died in 393, was a Frankish officer in the service of the Roman Empire.  He was a Comes Domesticorum (Head of the Domestici or Imperial Bodyguard), Magister Militum (Master of th
2 CONC e Militia) and Consul of Rome in 384.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Eugen Ewig (German historic Institute and Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres), he could be the son of Teutomer, who was a Frankish officer in the service of Emperor Julian the Apostate [1].  Karl Ferdinand We
2 CONC rner agrees with this opinion [2], but Christian Settipani is more reserved, deducing that it is difficult to conclude as long as the tribal origin of Teutomer remains unknown [3].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 378, Emperor Gratien sends him East at the head of an army to help Co-empereur Valens fight the Goths, but the Romans are defeated at the Battle of Adrianople on 9 August 378.  While Valens is killed in the battle, Richomer sur
2 CONC vived the rout and remained in the East, where he was second to Theodosius I, the new Caesar of the East.  Again at the head of a Frankish and Roman army, he is ordered to march against his nephew Arbogast (possibly son of Bauto) 
2 CONC to quell his rebellion. Theodosius names him Master of the Militia for the East in 383, and then Consul of Rome in 384 [4],[5].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He not only pursued a military career, but was very interested in the arts and letters, and corresponded with the rhetorician Libanios and the theologian St. Augustine [6],[7].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 388, Theodosius sent him to the West to fight the usurper Magnus Maximus, and he defeats him at the Battle of the Save (River), and forces his surrender at Aquilea, after which he executes him. After the assassination of one of
2 CONC  the co-Emperors Valentinien II (15 May 392), Arbogast places Eugenius on the throne and Theodosius sends Richomer to fight them, but he dies shortly after his departure, leaving it to Stilicho, the Vandal general, to defeat Eugen
2 CONC ius and Arbogast at the Battle of Frigidus (present Slovenia) on 6 September 394 [8],[3].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Posterity
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married Ascylla, who gave birth to Théodomir, future king of the Franks [9],[10],[11] and possibly an ancestor of the Merovingian dynasty, according to the later source, the Chonicle of Fredegaire (written about 660, 2-1/2 
2 CONC centuries later).  The existence of a Ricimer (or Richomer), a Roman patrician in 457-472, led Helmut Castritius (Germany) to proposed that a daughter of Richomer had married Wallia, King of the Visigoths and grandmother of the Pa
2 CONC trician Ricimer [12],[13].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes et références
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.↑ Eugen Ewig, Spätantikes und fränkishes Gallien, 1976-1979 (Werner 1984, p. 297).
2 CONT *2.↑ Werner 1984, p. 296-297.
2 CONT *3.↑ Settipani 1996, p. 28
2 CONT *4.↑ Werner 1984, p. 297-298.
2 CONT *5.↑ Rouche 1996, p. 73.
2 CONT *6.↑ Riché et Périn 1996, p. 288, notice « Richomer ».
2 CONT *7.↑ Werner 1984, p. 298-299.
2 CONT *8.↑ Werner 1984, p. 299-300.
2 CONT *9.↑ Grégoire de Tours, Histoire des Francs, Livre 2, chapitre 9.
2 CONT *10.↑ Kurth 1896, p. 152.
