2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1For Constantine II's name, see: ILS, 712-13, 721-2, 724; AE 1960, # 06; he is called Flavius Constantinus on one inscription: ILS 714. Constantine II's early life is discussed by Otto Seeck, RE 4, s.v. "Constantinus (3)," col. 10
2 CONC 26. 21f. For a listing of the sources that treat the parentage of Constantine II, see T. D. Barnes, The New Empire of Diocletian and Constantine (Cambridge, MA., 1981), 44. The authors of PLRE wrongly claim that Constantine II was
2 CONC  a bastard of Constantine I (A. H. M. Jones, J. R. Martindale, and J. Morris, The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire [Cambridge, 1971], s. v. "Fl. Claudius Constantinus 3," 1. 223); this contention is based upon an argument f
2 CONC rom silence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2The surviving books of Ammianus Marcellinus, which present our best political source for the fourth century, begin their coverage with the Fall of 353. For sources for Constantine II, see the short references in Origo Constantini
2 CONC  Imperatoris (=Anonymus Valesianus) , Aurelius Victor, Eusebius, Eutropius, Zosimus, Orosius and Zonaras. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3For a discussion of the dating of the birth and Caesarship of Constantine II, see Michael DiMaio, Jörn Zeuge, and Jane Bethune, "The Proelium Cibalense et Proelium Campi Ardiensis : The First Civil War of Constantine I and Licini
2 CONC us I," AncW 21 (1990): 89ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4For the chronology and sources for Constantine I's expeditions against the Sarmatians, see ibid , 84. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5For Constantine II's nominal command of Gaul, see further Seeck, RE 4, col. 1026. 56 ff. For the death of Crispus, see Patrick Guthrie, "The Execution of Crispus," Phoenix 20 (1966): 325-331 and, more recently, Hans Pohlsander, "
2 CONC Crispus Caesar: Brilliant Career and Tragic End," Historia 33 (1984): 76-106. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6For his victory over the Alamanni, see ILS 724; CIL 3.7000; Barnes, New Empire, 84; idem , A thanasius and Constantius: Theology and Politics in the Constantinian Empire (Cambridge, MA., 1993): 310-11, nn. 4-5. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7For a discussion of the Gothic war and sources that treat it, see DiMaio, Zonaras' Account of the Neo-Flavian Emperors: A Commentary (PhD diss., University of Missouri-Columbia, 1977): 194 ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8For his marriage, see Euseb. VC , 4. 49; for his residences in Gaul, see Athanasius, Apol. c. Arian. 87.7 (Opitz, ed., 2.1.166); for a listing of Constantine II's residences and movements before 337, see Barnes, New Empire , 84-5
2 CONC ; for the same information after the death of Constantine I, see idem ., Athanasius and Constantius , 218. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9For these events, see X. Lucien-Brun, "Constance II et le massacre des princes," Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé ser. 4 (1973): 585-602; Joe W. Leedom, "Constantius II: Three Revisions," Byzantion 48 (1978): 133-36. More
2 CONC  recently, see Michael DiMaio and Duane W.-H. Arnold, " Per Vim, Per Caedem, Per Bellum : A Study of Murder and Ecclesiastical Politics in the Year 337 A.D.," Byzantion 62 (1992): 198 ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10For a listing of the sources which discuss the division of the empire among the sons of Constantine and problems with their interpretation, see Michael DiMaio, "Smoke in the Wind: Zonaras' use of Philostorgius, Zosimus, John of 
2 CONC Antioch, and John of Rhodes in his Narrative on the Neo-Flavian Emperors," Byzantion 58 (1988): 236 ff. For more general discussion, see Barnes, Athanasius and Constantius , 19ff. 
2 CONT
