2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1Constantius II's full name: ILS , 705, 724, 731-33, 737, 739, 8808; such variations as Flavius Constantius ( Ibid. , 730, 734) or Constantius ( Ibid. , 708, 710, 729, 736, 738, 740) appear on inscriptions.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a listing of the sources that treat the parentage of Constantius II, see T.D. Barnes, New Empire of Diocletian and Constantine , (Cambridge, 1981), 45, A.H.M. Jones, J.R. Martindale, and J. Morris, The Prosopography of the Lat
2 CONC er Roman Empire , (Cambridge, 1971), s.v. "Fl Iul. Constantius 8," 1.226-227, Michael DiMaio, Zonaras' Account of the Neo-Flavian Emperors: A Commentary , (Ph.D diss., University of Missouri-Columbia, 1977), 281, n.18.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Barnes ( New Empire , 85) and the authors of the PLRE (1.226) date the Caesarship to 8 November 324 based on literary evidence, epigraphic evidence ( AE , 1937, #119: DiMaio, Zonaras , 360, n.14), more immediate than the 
2 CONC literary evidence, presents a date of 13 November 324.
2 CONT
2 CONT For Constantius' early military commands and first marriage: Ibid. , 359-60.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of Constantius' role in Constantine's funeral, see idem. , "Zonaras, Julian, and Philostorgios on the Death of Constantine I," GOTR , 26 (1981) 118ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of the purge of 337 and the sources that treat these matters, see X. Lucien-Brun, "Constance II et le massacre des princes," Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé ser. 4 (1973): 585-602; Joe W. Leedom, "Constan
2 CONC tius II: Three Revisions," Byzantion 48 (1978): 132-145, and Michael DiMaio and Duane Arnold, " Per Vim, Per Caedem, Per Bellum : A Study of Murder and Ecclesiastical Politics in the Year 337 A.D.," Byzantion , 62(1992), 158ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2For a discussion of the succession of the sons of Constantine to the imperial throne, see ibid ., 198ff; for a listing of the sources which discuss the apportionment of the empire among the sons of Constantine and problems with t
2 CONC heir interpretation, see Michael DiMaio, "Smoke in the Wind: Zonaras' Use of Philostorgius, Zosimus, John of Antioch, and John of Rhodes in his Narrative on the Neo-Flavian Emperors," Byzantion , 58 (1988), 336ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3For a complete listing of Constantius' residences during his reign, his movements, and the sources that treat them, see Barnes ( New Empire , 85ff; Athanasius and Constantius , 218ff); Kienast does much the same thing in briefer 
2 CONC compass (Dietmar Kienast, Römische Kaisertabelle , [Darmstadt, 1990], 309ff); Seeck's treatment of Constantius' reign remains the classic (O. Seeck, RE 4, s.v. "Constantius (4)," col. 1044ff, although Barnes' Athanasius and Consta
2 CONC ntius may soon supplant it.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Ammianus Marcellinus 21.16.15; Libanius Or. 18. 206-207.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5The revolts of Magnentius and Vetranio are discussed by Barnes, Athanasius and Constantius , 101ff, Wm. Ensslin, RE 14, s.v. "Magnentius (1)," col.445.8ff, and O. Seeck, RE 8.2, s.v. col. 1838ff
2 CONT
2 CONT For a reconstruction of the Battle of Mursa and the sources that treat it, see DiMaio, Byzantion , 58 (1988), 245ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6For a discussion of Gallus' fall from grace and the sources that treat the event, see ibid , 232ff, and idem. , "The Antiochene Connection: Zonaras, Ammianus Marcellinus, and John of Antioch on the Reigns of the Emperors Constant
2 CONC ius II and Julian," Byzantion , 50 (1980), 170ff. See also R. N. Mooney, "Gallus Caesar's Last Journey," Classical Philology 53 (1958): 175-177. For a disputed plot of the usurper Magnentius to kill Gallus, see Robert Frakes, "Amm
2 CONC ianus Marcellinus and Zonaras on a Late Roman Assassination plot," Historia 46 (1997): 121-128.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7For a listing of sources on Eusebia and her influence on Constantius, see A.H.M. Jones, J.R. Martindale, and J. Morris, PLRE ,s.v. "Eusebia" 1.300-01.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8For a discussion of the rise of Julian and the events leading up to the death of Constantius, see R.C. Blockley, "Constantius Gallus and Julian as Caesars of Constantius II," Latomus , 21 (1972), 445ff; Ilse Müller-Seidel, "Die U
2 CONC surpation Julians des Abtrünnigen im Lichte seiner Germanenpolitik," HZ , 180 (1955). 227ff; and DiMaio, Zonaras , 329ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a listing of sources on Faustina, see A.H.M. Jones, J.R. Martindale, and J. Morris, PLRE ,s.v. "Faustina" 1.326.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9Extant Books XIV through XXI of Ammianus' Res Gestae treat the reign of Constantius. At least his lost Book XIII did also; see further, Robert Frakes, "Cross-References to the Lost Books of Ammianus Marcellinus," Phoenix 49 (1995
2 CONC ): 232-246.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10For a discussion of Constantius' character, see DiMaio and Arnold, Byzantion , 62 (1992), 168ff. For a summary of his virtues and vices, see Ammianus 21.16.1-9
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnes' Athanasius and Constantius is the locus classicus for any discussion of Constantius' involvement with the Christian faith.
2 CONT
