2 TITL Spurious Pedigree -- Table 818: Constantine the Great to Charlemagne
1 NOTE Constantine I., Augustus, (Flavius Valerius Aurelius Constantius), born265 (272?), in Naissus in Moesia. He died May, 336 or 337, buried in thechurch of the Holy Apostles in Constantinople. Constantine received onlya meager educat
2 CONC ion. He took up soldiering early, and proved his valor inthe wars against Egypt and Persia. He was of British birth and education,and is known as the first Christian Emperor. He fought with his father inthe Boulogne campaign and s
2 CONC hared in a British campaign. The Gallic army,deeply loyal to the humane Constantius, came to love his handsome, brave,and energetic son; and when the father died at York in 306, the troopsproclaimed Constantine not merely as "Caes
2 CONC ar" but as Augustus - emperor.He accepted the lesser title, excusing himself on the grounds that hislife would be unsafe without an army at his back. ConsequentlyConstantine fought successfully against the invading Franks. Later, 
2 CONC witha British army he set out to put down the persecution of Christiansforever. The greatest of all Roman Emperors, he annexed Britain to theRoman Empire and founded Constantinople. In the year 321 he decreed thatthe Christian Sun
2 CONC day be truly observed as a day of rest. In 325 heassembled the Council of Nicea in Bithynia, Asia Minor, which he attendedin person. This Council formulated the Nicene Creed. The following edictof Constantine sets forth the standa
2 CONC rds of his life: "We call God towitness, the Savior of all men, that in assuming the government we areinfluenced solely by these two considerations - the uniting of the empirein one faith, and the restoration of peace to a world r
2 CONC ent in pieces bythe insanity of religious persecution." By his first wife (1) Minervinahe was father of the following son:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Flavius Valerius Crispus Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (2) Fausta, sister of his step-mother, Theodora. Fausta andTheodora and their brother Maxentius were children of Maximinus, RomanEmperor (286-305). One writer, Brewer, said he was a giant, eight feet,six inches tall! Hi
2 CONC s son Maxentius, Emperor (310-311), married Valeria,daughter of Galerius, Emperor (310-311), and his wife, Valeria, who wasdaughter of Diocletian, Emperor (284-305). Fausta and Constantine theGreat had three sons and one daughter 
2 CONC as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Constantine II.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Constantius II
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Constants I.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Helen, wife of Julian the Apostate.
2 CONT
2 CONT See details of the life of Constantine I. in Durant, pp. 653-664.
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT Constantine the Great, Roman Emperor, Kings of Denmark and Sweden, toGeoffrey Plantaganet
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~pmcbride/james/f005.htm#I310X1
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: Wurts, "Magna Charta Sureties," Vol I., pp. 163-164
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: Gordon, C. D., "The Age of Attila" (1960)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Constantine I., Augustus, the Great, son of Constantius I, Caesar(293-305), Augustus (305-306), and his wife, Helena, born 265, died 336or 337. He was of British birth and education, and he is known as thefirst Christian ruler 
2 CONC of the Roman Empire. With a British army he set outto put down the persecution of Christians forever. The greatest of allRoman Emperors, he annexed Britain to the Roman Empire and foundedConstantinople. In 325 he assembled the Cou
2 CONC ncil of Nicea, which heattended in person, in Nicea in Bithynia, Asia Minor, which formulatedthe Nicene Creed. According to Dr. Anderson (Wurts, "Magna Charta"), hemarried Fausta and had three sons and a daughter as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Constantine II. was given the West (Britain, Gaul, and Spain) by hisfather. He ruled from 337 to 340.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Constantius II See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Constans I., was given the South (North Africa, Italy, Illyrium, andThrace including Rome and Constantinople). He ruled from 337 to 350.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Helen, wife of Julian the Apostate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two nephews (Gallus and Julian) were given Armenia, Macedonia, andGreece. Constantine also had another son, Crispus, who died in 326.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Constantius II, second son of Constantine the Great, died in 360. Hewas given the East (Asia Minor, Syria, and Egypt. He as the sole heir by353. He married Fausta.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Constantius III, Emperor in 421, married in 417 as her second husbandand against her will, Galla Placida, daughter of Theodosius I.,Theodosius the Great, Emperor (379-395) and his wife, Galla. Galla, themother, was the daughter
2 CONC  of Valentinian I., Emperor (364-375), and hiswife, Justina. Galla Placida married in 414 (1) Ataulph, Visigothic King(410-415). Theodosius I. was the son of Theodosius and his wife,Thermanis. They were the first of the House of T
2 CONC heodosius. He died earlyin 395, the last ruler of a united Roman Empire, as great in extent asthat left by Augustus. He died in 421. She died about 450, after beingexiled after the death of Constantius III. They had a son and daug
2 CONC hter.See John Fines, "Who's Who in the Middle Ages", (1970), pp. 175-177, fordetails of Galla Placida. She was a half-sister of Honorius, the Emperorunder whom Britain was finally lost to Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Valentinian III was clothed with imperial robes in Ravenna in 425. Hedied in 455. He fought against and bribed Attila, the Hun. He married asher first husband Licinia Eudoxia, daughter of Theodosius II., Emperor ofthe east (408
2 CONC -450) and his wife, Eudocia, who died in 454. Vanentinianand Eudoxia had two daughters as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Eudoxia. See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Placida, married Anicius Olybrius, Emperor (472).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Eudoxia (Eudocia) married Huneric, Vandal King (477-484), son ofGaiseric (Genseric), King of the Vandals (428-477), father of otherVandal kings, who died in 480 in Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Hilderic, King of the Vandals (523-530).
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Hilda, wife of Frode VII., who died in 548.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Halfdan of Lethra, King of Denmark. He had two sons as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ivar Vidfadma. See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hraeric of Lethra, married (1) Auda of Roeskilda. They had a son,Halfdan (Sigfrid) of Jutland. See elsewhere for a continuation of thislineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Ivar Vidfadma, King of Denmark and Sweden in 660.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Roric Slingeband, King of Denmark and Sweden in 700.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. Harald Hildetand, King of Denmark and Sweden in 725.
2 CONT
2 CONT 12. Sigurd Ring, living in 750.
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. Rayner Lodbrok, King of Denmark and Sweden, who died in 794, havingmarried Aslanga.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14. Sigurd Snodoye, King of Denmark and Sweden, died in 830.
2 CONT
2 CONT 15. Horda Knut, King of Denmark, died in 850.
2 CONT
2 CONT 16. Frotho, King of Denmark, died in 875.
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. Gorm Enske, married Sida and died in 890.
2 CONT
2 CONT 18. Harold Parcus, King of Denmark, married Elgiva, daughter of EthelredI, King of England, a brother of King Alfred the Great. They had thefollowing children:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Gorm del Gammel. See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Ethelwald
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Ethelhelm
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 19. Gorm del Gammel, King of Denmark, died in 931. He married Thyra.
2 CONT
2 CONT 20. Harald I Blaatand, Bluetooth, King of Denmark, born circa 910, diedin 981. He had at least two children of record as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Svend I (known in England as Sweyn) Forkbeard, born in 965, died inGainsborough, England, who was King of England in the autumn of 1013 andKing of Denmark about 985. He was the leader of several unsuccessfulinvasions of Scotlan
2 CONC d. He had two sons: Harald II Svendsen, King ofDenmark on February 3, 1014; and Knud (Cnut or Canute) I., the Great,King of England and Denmark in 1019, born circa 995-1000, died inShaftsbury, co. Dorset, England, also King of Nor
2 CONC way 1028, Lord of NorseColonies, Overload of the Scots, King of Dublin (?).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Gunnora of Denmark. See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 21. Lady Gunnora of Denmark, married Richard I, 3rd Duke of Normandy, sonof William Longsword, and his wife, Espriota. They had several childrenas follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Richard II, 4th Duke of Normandy. He had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Adeliza, married Rainald I., Count of Burgundy. They had a son,William I., Count of Burgundy, father of Rainald II., Count of Burgundyand Guy, Archbishop of Vienne, and later Pope Calixtus II.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Robert, Duke of Normandy, father of William the Conqueror, King ofEngland, and grandfather of Henry I, King of England. See elsewhere forthe continuation of this lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Geoffrey
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Emma of Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Robert of Evereux, the Archbishop. See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Godfrey (Geoffrey)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. William
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Mauger
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Hawisa.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 22. Robert of Evereux, the Archbishop, died in 1087.
2 CONT
2 CONT 23. Richard of Evereux, Count of Evereux, died in 1067.
2 CONT
2 CONT 24. Agnes Evereux, married Simon de Montfort.
2 CONT
2 CONT 25. Bertrade Montfort, married Fulk IV, the Surly, Count of Anjou, bornin 1043, died in 1109.
2 CONT
2 CONT 26. Fulk V. the Younger of Anjou, 9th Count of Anjou, 1109-1129, whoabdicated his position in Anjou and became King of Jerusalem, 1131-1143.He married Ermengarde., daughter of Helias, Count of Maine, who died in1110.
2 CONT
2 CONT 27. Geoffrey IV, The Fair, Plantaganet, 10th Count of Anjou, 1129-1151,etc. married Matilda (Maud the English Empress), daughter of King HenryI. of England and his wife, Matilda of Scotland, daughter of MalcolmIII., King of Scotla
2 CONC nd.
