1 NOTE Antoninus Pius
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT Antoninus Pius 
2 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 11 July 138 – 7 March 161 
2 CONT Full name Titus Aurelius Fulvus Boionius Arrius Antoninus (from birth to adoption by Hadrian);
2 CONT Titus Aelius Caesar Antoninus (from adoption to accession); Caesar Titus Aelius Hadrianus Antoninus Augustus Pius
2 CONT (as emperor) 
2 CONT Born 19 September 86(86-09-19) 
2 CONT Birthplace near Lanuvium 
2 CONT Died 7 March 161 (aged 74) 
2 CONT Place of death Lorium 
2 CONT Buried Hadrian's Mausoleum 
2 CONT Predecessor Hadrian 
2 CONT Successor Marcus Aurelius &
2 CONT Lucius Verus 
2 CONT Wife Faustina 
2 CONT Offspring Faustina the Younger, one other daughter and two sons, all died before 138 (natural); Marcus Aurelius
2 CONT and Lucius Verus (adoptive) 
2 CONT Dynasty Antonine 
2 CONT Father Titus Aurelius Fulvus (natural);
2 CONT Hadrian (adoptive, from 25 February 138) 
2 CONT Mother Arria Fadilla 
2 CONT Roman imperial dynasties
2 CONT Antonine Dynasty Antoninus Pius 
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT Natural - Faustina the Younger, also one other daughter and two sons, all died before 138 
2 CONT Adoptive - Marcus Aurelius and Lucius Verus 
2 CONT Marcus Aurelius with Lucius Verus 
2 CONT Marcus Aurelius alone 
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT Natural - 13, including Commodus and Lucilla 
2 CONT Commodus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Titus Aurelius Fulvus Boionius Arrius Antoninus (19 September, 86–7 March 161), generally known in English as Antoninus Pius was Roman emperor from 138 to 161. He was the fourth of the Five Good Emperors and a member of the Aureli
2 CONC i. He did not possess the sobriquet "Pius" until after his accession to the throne. Almost certainly, he earned the name "Pius" because he compelled the Senate to deify Hadrian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT 1 Early life 
2 CONT 1.1 Childhood and family 
2 CONT 1.2 Marriage and children 
2 CONT 1.3 Favour with Hadrian 
2 CONT 2 Emperor 
2 CONT 3 Historiography 
2 CONT 3.1 In later scholarship 
2 CONT 4 References 
2 CONT 5 Notes 
2 CONT 6 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Childhood and family
2 CONT He was the son and only child of Titus Aurelius Fulvus, consul in 89 whose family came from Nemausus (modern Nîmes) and was born near Lanuvium and his mother was Arria Fadilla. Antoninus’ father and paternal grandfather died when 
2 CONC he was young and he was raised by Titus Arrius Antoninus, his maternal grandfather, a man of integrity and culture and a friend of Pliny the Younger. His mother married to Publius Julius Lupus (a man of consular rank), Suffect Con
2 CONC sul in 98, and bore him a daughter called Julia Fadilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT As a private citizen between 110–115, he married Annia Galeria Faustina the Elder. They had a very happy marriage. She was the daughter of consul Marcus Annius Verus and Rupilia Faustina (a half-sister to Roman Empress Vibia Sabin
2 CONC a). Faustina was a beautiful woman, renowned for her wisdom. She spent her whole life caring for the poor and assisting the most disadvantaged Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Faustina bore Antoninus four children, two sons and two daughters. They were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Aurelius Fulvius Antoninus (died before 138); his sepulchral inscription has been found at the Mausoleum of Hadrian in Rome. 
2 CONT Marcus Galerius Aurelius Antoninus (died before 138); his sepulchral inscription has been found at the Mausoleum of Hadrian in Rome. His name appears on a Greek Imperial coin. 
2 CONT Aurelia Fadilla (died in 135); she married Lucius Lamia Silvanus, consul 145. She appeared to have no children with her husband and her sepulchral inscription has been found in Italy. 
2 CONT Annia Galeria Faustina Minor or Faustina the Younger (between 125-130-175), a future Roman Empress, married her maternal cousin, future Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius. 
2 CONT When Faustina died in 141, he was in complete mourning and did the following in memory of his wife:
2 CONT
2 CONT Deified her as a goddess. 
2 CONT Had a temple built in the Roman Forum in her name, with priestesses in the temple. 
2 CONT Had various coins with her portrait struck in her honor. These coins were scripted ‘DIVA FAUSTINA’ and were elaborately decorated. 
2 CONT He created a charity which he founded and called it Puellae Faustinianae or Girls of Faustina, which assisted orphaned girls. 
2 CONT Created a new alimenta (see Grain supply to the city of Rome). 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Favour with Hadrian
2 CONT Having filled with more than usual success the offices of quaestor and praetor, he obtained the consulship in 120; he was next appointed by the Emperor Hadrian as one of the four proconsuls to administer Italia, then greatly incre
2 CONC ased his reputation by his conduct as proconsul of Asia. He acquired much favor with the Emperor Hadrian, who adopted him as his son and successor on 25 February, 138, after the death of his first adopted son Lucius Aelius, on the
2 CONC  condition that he himself would adopt Marcus Annius Verus, the son of his wife's brother, and Lucius, son of Aelius Verus, who afterwards became the emperors 
