2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Faustina the Younger=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Annia Galeria Faustina Minor (Minor is Latin for the Younger), Faustina Minor or Faustina the Younger (born probably 21 September c. 130 , died in winter of 175 or spring of 176) was a daughter of Roman Emperor Antoninus Pius and 
2 CONC Roman Empress Faustina the Elder. She was a Roman Empress and wife to her maternal cousin Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius. Though Roman sources give a generally negative view of her character, she was held in high esteem by soldiers
2 CONC  and her own husband and was given divine honours after her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ''Early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Faustina, named after her mother, was her parents' fourth and youngest child and their second daughter; she was also their only child to survive to adulthood. She was born and raised in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her great uncle, the emperor Hadrian, had arranged with her father for Faustina to marry Lucius Verus. On 25 February 138, she and Verus were betrothed. Verus’ father was Hadrian’s first adopted son and his intended heir; however,
2 CONC  when Verus’ father died, Hadrian chose Faustina’s father to be his second adopted son, and eventually, he became Hadrian’s successor. Faustina’s father ended the engagement between his daughter and Verus and arranged for Faustina
2 CONC 's betrothal to her maternal cousin, Marcus Aurelius; Aurelius was also adopted by her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Imperial heiress''
2 CONT
2 CONT In April or May 145, Faustina and Marcus Aurelius were married, as had been planned since 138. Since Aurelius was, by adoption, Antoninus Pius' son, under Roman law he was marrying his sister; Antoninus would have had to formally 
2 CONC release one or the other from his paternal authority (his patria potestas) for the ceremony to take place. Little is specifically known of the ceremony, but it is said to have been "noteworthy". Coins were issued with the heads of
2 CONC  the couple, and Antoninus, as Pontifex Maximus, would have officiated. Marcus makes no apparent reference to the marriage in his surviving letters, and only sparing references to Faustina. Faustina was given the title of Augusta 
2 CONC on 1 December 147 after the birth of her first child, Domitia Faustina.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Reign as empress''
2 CONT
2 CONT When Antoninus died on 7 March 161, Marcus and Lucius Verus ascended to the throne and became co-rulers. Faustina then became empress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, not much has survived from the Roman sources regarding Faustina's life, but what is available does not give a good report. Cassius Dio and the Augustan History accuse Faustina of ordering deaths by poison and execut
2 CONC ion; she has also been accused of instigating the revolt of Avidius Cassius against her husband. The Augustan History mentions adultery with sailors, gladiators, and men of rank; however, Faustina and Aurelius seem to have been ve
2 CONC ry close and mutually devoted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Faustina accompanied her husband on various military campaigns and enjoyed the love and reverence of Roman soldiers. Aurelius gave her the title of Mater Castrorum or ‘Mother of the Camp’. She attempted to make her home out of an 
2 CONC army camp. Between 170–175, she was in the north, and in 175, she accompanied Aurelius to the east.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Revolt of Avidius Cassius and death''
2 CONT
2 CONT That same year, 175, Aurelius's general Avidius Cassius was proclaimed Roman emperor after the erroneous news of Marcus's death; the sources indicate Cassius was encouraged by Marcus's wife Faustina, who was concerned about her hu
2 CONC sband's failing health, believing him to be on the verge of death, and felt the need for Cassius to act as a protector in this event, since her son Commodus, aged 13, was still young. She also wanted someone who would act as a cou
2 CONC nter-weight to the claims of Tiberius Claudius Pompeianus, who was in a strong position to take the office of Princeps in the event of Marcus’s death. The evidence, including Marcus's own Meditations, supports the idea that Marcus
2 CONC  was indeed quite ill, but by the time Marcus recovered, Cassius was already fully acclaimed by the Egyptian legions of II Traiana Fortis and XXII Deiotariana.
2 CONT
2 CONT "After a dream of empire lasting three months and six days", Cassius was murdered by a centurion; his head was sent to Marcus Aurelius, who refused to see it and ordered it buried. Egypt recognized Marcus as emperor again by 28 Ju
2 CONC ly 175.
2 CONT
2 CONT As would be expected, the events of Cassius' rebellion took their toll on Faustina, who died in the winter of 175, after an accident, at the military camp in Halala (a city in the Taurus Mountains in Cappadocia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Aurelius grieved much for his wife and buried her in the Mausoleum of Hadrian in Rome. She was deified: her statue was placed in the Temple of Venus in Rome and a temple was dedicated to her in her honor. Halala’s name was changed
2 CONC  to Faustinopolis and Aurelius opened charity schools for orphan girls called Puellae Faustinianae or 'Girls of Faustina'. The Baths of Faustina in Miletus are named after her.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT In their thirty years of marriage, Faustina bore Marcus Aurelius thirteen children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Annia Aurelia Galeria Faustina (147–after 165)
2 CONT # Gemellus Lucillae (died around 150), twin brother of Lucilla
2 CONT # Annia Aurelia Galeria Lucilla (148/50–182), twin sister of Gemellus, married her father's co-ruler Lucius Verus
2 CONT # Titus Aelius Antoninus (born after 150, died before 7 March 161)
2 CONT # Titus Aelius Aurelius (born after 150, died before 7 March 161)
2 CONT # Hadrianus (152–157)
2 CONT # Domitia Faustina (born after 150, died before 7 March 161)
2 CONT # Annia Aurelia Fadilla (159–after 211)
2 CONT # Annia Cornificia Faustina Minor (160–after 211)
2 CONT # Titus Aurelius Fulvus Antoninus (161–165), twin brother of Commodus
2 CONT # Lucius Aurelius Commodus Antoninus (Commodus) (161–192), twin brother of Titus Aurelius Fulvus Antoninus, later emperor
2 CONT # Marcus Annius Verus Caesar (162–169)
2 CONT # Vibia Aurelia Sabina (170–died before 217)
2 CONT
