2 CONT =Lucius Antistius Burrus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Antistius Burrus Adventus (died 188) of the gens Antistia was a Roman Senator that lived in the 2nd century. He was one of the sons-in-law of Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius and Roman Empress Faustina the Younger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burrus originally came from an African Roman Senatorial family from Thibilis, a town near Hippo Regius in the Africa Province. Although Burrus was born and raised in Thibilis, his family was not of very ancient lineage. He was the
2 CONC  son of Quintus Antistius Adventus Aquilinus Postumus and Novia Crispina. Novia Crispina is known from an honorific inscription dedicated to her, when her husband served as Roman Governor in Arabia Petraea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Antistius Adventus (born around mid-120s), during the rule of the Nerva–Antonine dynasty, served as a successful Military Tribune, Legatus, Quaestor, Public Construction Official and Governor in various provinces throughou
2 CONC t the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before the death of the Emperor Marcus Aurelius, Burrus was betrothed to and married the Emperor’s youngest daughter Vibia Aurelia Sabina. After Burrus married Aurelia Sabina, they returned and settled in Thibilis. When M
2 CONC arcus Aurelius died in 180, Aurelia Sabina’s brother Commodus succeeded her father as Roman Emperor. In 181, Burrus served as an ordinary consul.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 188, Antistius Burrus was involved in a conspiracy against Commodus. When this conspiracy was uncovered, Antistius Burrus was put to death. After his death, Aurelia Sabina later remarried and it appears they had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Antistius_Burrus
