1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domitia_Corbula
2 CONT
2 CONT Domitia was a Roman noble woman who lived in the first century. She was the eldest daughter to Roman Consul and General Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo and Cassia Longina. Her younger sister was Domitia Longina, a future Roman Empress who
2 CONC  would marry the future Roman Emperor Domitian. Her paternal aunt was Roman Empress Milonia Caesonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domitia was born sometime towards the middle of the first century at an unknown date. By 63, she married the Roman Senator Lucius '''Annius Vinicianus''', who was a son to the Roman Senator Gaius Annius Pollio.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vinicianus, in 63 acted as a military commander under his father-in-law in the Euphrates. In 66, there was a conspiracy that involved Vinicianus and his father-in-law to overthrow the Roman Emperor Nero. Vinicianus refused to spea
2 CONC k nor prove his innocence to the Emperor and committed suicide in 67.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roman Historian Suetonius makes a reference to their son in the account of Domitian which is mentioned in clause 12. The year that Domitia died is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://romanatoz.blogspot.com/2011/12/annius-vinicianus.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Annius Vinicianus, is said by the historian Tacitus (1) to be 26 years old in 63.  When not yet of senatorial age, he served as deputy-commander of the fifth legion in Tiridates, Armenia.   He served with Domitius Corbulo in Armen
2 CONC ia and was married to Corbulo’s daughter. In 66, he died as a result of a failed plot to replace Nero with his father-in-law. His exact relationship to the first Annius Vinicianus is curious, although he may have been his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Annius Vinicianus the elder, was a leading figure in the plots against the imperial house during the reigns of Gaius Caligula (37–41 C.E.) and Claudius (41–54 C.E.). In 32 C.E., he was listed as a member of a treasonous group of p
2 CONC oliticians but escaped trial and condemnation when Tiberius set aside certain cases for personal review. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nine years later he became one of the organizers in a plot to kill Caligula. After the emperor’s death, Vinicianus called for Valerius Asiaticus to withdraw from seeking the throne, hoping to avert a massacre of the senate by the 
2 CONC praetorian guard, which had just proclaimed Claudius emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediately unhappy with Claudius, Vinicianus joined a conspiracy to elevate Scribonianus Camillus, governor of Illyricum, to the throne in 42. When the attempt failed utterly , he was one of those who followed Scribonianus’s exam
2 CONC ple—and killed himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=UoY3AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA413&dq=Vivianus+Annius&hl=en&sa=X&ei=TNOKUbGuHsaTiQKBnYHoCw&ved=0CGcQ6AEwCQ#v=onepage&q=Vivianus%20Annius&f=false
2 CONT
