1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was Corrector Apuliae et Calabriae, Proconsul, and Praefectus Urbis Romae (351). The Clodii Celsini continued to practice the traditional religions of antiquity in the face of Christian hegemony through at least
2 CONC  the 4th century, when Clodius Celsinus Adelphius converted.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Clodius Celsinus Adelphius=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Clodius Celsinus Adelphius or Adelfius (fl. 333–351) was a politician of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to the poet Faltonia Betitia Proba, and they had two sons, Quintus Clodius Hermogenianus Olybrius (consul in 379) and Faltonius Probus Alypius. His wife converted to Christianity after 353, and later Celsinus probab
2 CONC ly converted too; he probably dedicated a column ad altare majus S. Anastasiae, near the main altar of the church of Sant'Anastasia, or that was his and his wife's funerary inscription.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 333, Adelphius was corrector of Apulia et Calabria, with the see of his office at Beneventum, where he was a patron too. In 351 he was proconsul of an unknown province, probably Africa, and he was already married to Proba. 
2 CONC From 7 June to 18 December 351 he is attested as praefectus urbi of Rome, under the usurper Magnentius. In this period he was accused by some Dorus of conspiring against Magnentius; it is probable that this accusation was true, as
2 CONC  shown by the fact that Proba wrote a poem celebrating Emperor Constantius II's victory over the usurper.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clodius_Celsinus_Adelphius
