1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Nonius_Asprenas_(consul_36_BC) Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lucius Nonius Asprenas''' was a Roman politician and general who fought with Julius Caesar and was elected consul suffectus in 36 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT A Novus homo of the late republic, and originally hailing from Picenum,[1] Asprenas was elected to the office of praetor by 47 BC.[2] Although having no obvious connections or political ties to Julius Caesar[3] he held a proconsul
2 CONC ar command under Caesar in Africa during the civil war, holding the town of Thapsus with two legions in 46 BC.[4] The following year he followed Caesar to Hispania, where he was given a command over the cavalry, possibly as a lega
2 CONC te.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the early years of the Second Triumvirate Asprenas was largely overlooked for military command, but eventually he was given a role in Caesar Octavianus’s war against Sextus Pompeius.[6] He was rewarded for his services with
2 CONC  his election as suffect consul in 36 BC.[7] In 31 BC, Asprenas was elected as one of the Septemviri Epulones.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had at least one son, Lucius Nonius Asprenas, who was the father of the consul suffectus of AD 6. He also had a daughter, Nonia Polla, who married Lucius Volusius Saturninus.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * T. Robert S. Broughton, The Magistrates of the Roman Republic, Vol II (1952).
2 CONT * Syme, Ronald, The Roman Revolution (1939)
