1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Vipsania Julia Agrippina''', Roman noblewoman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia the Younger (19 BC - c. AD 29) (Classical Latin: IVLIA•MINOR) or Julilla (little Julia), Vipsania Julia Agrippina, Iulilla, Julia, Augustus' granddaughter, or Julia Caesaris Minor, was a Roman noblewoman of the Julio-Claudia
2 CONC n dynasty. She was the first daughter and second child of [http://www.geni.com/people/Marcus-Vipsanius-Agrippa/6000000000701119762 Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Julia-Caesaris-Major/6000000007234813810 
2 CONC Julia the Elder]. Along with her sister Agrippina the Elder, Julia was raised and educated by her maternal grandfather Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Augustus-Roman-Emperor/6000000001336717255 Augustus] and her maternal step-
2 CONC grandmother [http://www.geni.com/people/Livia-Drusilla-Julia-Augusta/6000000000424886603 Livia Drusilla].
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia the Younger was the elder granddaughter of the Emperor Augustus, sister-in-law, stepdaughter and daughter-in-law of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Tiberius-Roman-Emperor/6000000008122648505 Tiberius], maternal aunt 
2 CONC of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Caligula-Emperor-of-the-Roman-Empire/6000000006101240586 Caligula] and Empress [http://www.geni.com/people/Julia-Agrippina-Minor-of-Rome/4994272588560101934 Agrippina the Younger], second
2 CONC  cousin of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-Emperor/6000000002119140027 Claudius], and maternal great-aunt of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Nero-Roman-Emperor/6000000000424698537 Nero].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT About 5 BC or 6 BC, Augustus arranged her to marry [http://www.geni.com/people/Lucius-%C3%86milius-Paullus/6000000000976947983 Lucius Aemilius Paullus]. Paullus had a family relation to her as her first half-cousin, as both had [h
2 CONC ttp://www.geni.com/people/Scribonia/6000000000700912476 Scribonia] as grandmother: Julia's mother was a daughter of Scribonia by Emperor Augustus; Paullus' mother, [http://www.geni.com/people/Cornelia-Scipio/6000000004089117513 Co
2 CONC rnelia Scipio], was a daughter of Scribonia resulting from her earlier marriage to [http://www.geni.com/people/Publius-Cornelius-Scipio-Salvito/6000000000700882993 Publius Cornelius Scipio Salvito].
2 CONT
2 CONT Paullus and Julia had a daughter, [http://www.geni.com/people/Aemilia-Lepida/6000000002106239618 Aemilia Lepida] and (possibly) a son, Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (although the latter may also have been the son to Marcus Aemilius Lepi
2 CONC dus (consul 6). According to Suetonius, she built a large pretentious country house. Augustus disliked large overdone houses and had it demolished.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 8, according to ancient historians, Julia was exiled for having an affair with Decimus Junius Silanus, a Roman Senator. She was sent to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isole_Tremiti Trimerus], a small Italian island, where she ga
2 CONC ve birth to a child. Augustus rejected the infant and ordered it to be exposed, or left on a mountainside to die. Silanus went into voluntary exile, but returned under Tiberius' reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime between 1 and 14, her husband Paullus was executed as a conspirator in a revolt. Modern historians theorize that Julia's exile was not actually for adultery but for involvement in Paullus' revolt. Livia Drusilla plotted a
2 CONC gainst her stepdaughter's family and ruined them, according to some. This led to open compassion for the fallen family. In 29 AD, Julia died on the same island where she had been sent in exile twenty years earlier. Due to the adul
2 CONC tery that Julia committed, Augustus stated in his will that she would never be buried in Rome. She was survived by daughter, (possibly) a son, and by several grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Unusual naming variants===
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia the Younger was not a Julia Caesaris by birth: being the daughter of Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa made her a Vipsania Agrippina by birth, although there are no contemporary sources that show that that name was ever used for her.
2 CONC  She came to belong to the household of the Julio-Claudian dynasty as she was raised and instructed by her maternal grandfather Augustus. Further, Augustus adopted Tiberius as his son (and heir), and while Tiberius was remarried t
2 CONC o Julia the Elder. Augustus became something of a paternal grandfather to Julia the Elder's children, too, including Julia the Younger. A formal adoption "in the family of the Caesars" among the offspring of Agrippa and Julia the 
2 CONC Elder is however only recorded regarding Vipsania Julia's brothers Gaius — hence Gaius Caesar — and Lucius — hence Lucius Caesar. Her younnger sister Agrippina the Elder and youngest full brother, Agrippa Postumus, were named afte
2 CONC r their natural father. Likewise, her eldest half-sisters, Vipsania Agrippina and Vipsania Marcella, were named after their father. Her younger half-brother, unnamed in contemporary sources, was later sometimes dubbed "Tiberillus,
2 CONC " after his father Tiberius, and died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT * English Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_the_Younger Julia the Younger]
2 CONT
2 CONT * English Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julio-Claudian_family_tree Julio-Claudian family tree]
2 CONT
2 CONT * ancestry.com: [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jdp-fam&id=I22748 Vipsania of Rome]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Julia the Younger=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia the Younger (Classical Latin: IVLIA•MINOR) or Julilla (little Julia), Vipsania Julia Agrippina, Iulilla, Julia, Augustus' granddaughter, or Julia Caesaris Minor (19 BC-c. AD 29), was a Roman noblewoman of the Julio-Claudian 
2 CONC dynasty. She was the first daughter and second child of Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa and Julia the Elder. Along with her sister Agrippina the Elder, Julia was raised and educated by her maternal grandfather Augustus and her maternal s
2 CONC tep-grandmother Livia Drusilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia the Younger was the elder granddaughter of the Emperor Augustus, sister-in-law, stepdaughter and daughter-in-law of the Emperor Tiberius, maternal aunt of the Emperor Caligula and Empress Agrippina the Younger, second cousin
2 CONC  of the Emperor Claudius, and maternal great-aunt of the Emperor Nero.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT About 5 BC or 6 BC, Augustus arranged her to marry Lucius Aemilius Paullus. Paullus had a family relation to her as her first half-cousin, as both had Scribonia as grandmother: Julia's mother was a daughter of Scribonia by Augustu
2 CONC s; Paullus' mother, Cornelia Scipio, was a daughter of Scribonia resulting from her earlier marriage to Publius Cornelius Scipio Salvito.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paullus and Julia had a daughter, Aemilia Lepida and possibly a son, Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (although the latter may also have been the son to Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (consul 6). According to Suetonius, she built a large pretenti
2 CONC ous country house. Augustus disliked large overdone houses and had it demolished.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 8, according to ancient historians, Julia was exiled for having an affair with Decimus Junius Silanus, a Roman Senator. She was sent to Trimerus, a small Italian island, where she gave birth to a child. Augustus rejected the in
2 CONC fant and ordered it to be exposed, or left on a mountainside to die. Silanus went into voluntary exile, but returned under Tiberius' reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime between 1 and 14, her husband Paullus was executed as a conspirator in a revolt. Modern historians theorize that Julia's exile was not actually for adultery but for involvement in Paullus' revolt. Livia plotted against he
2 CONC r stepdaughter's family and ruined them, according to some. This led to open compassion for the fallen family. In 29 AD, Julia died on the same island where she had been sent in exile twenty years earlier. Due to the adultery that
2 CONC  Julia committed, Augustus stated in his will that she would never be buried in Rome. She was survived by a daughter, possibly a son, and by several grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Unusual naming variants'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia the Younger was not a Julia Caesaris by birth: being the daughter of Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa made her a Vipsania Agrippina by birth, although there are no contemporary sources that show that that name was ever used for her.
2 CONC  She came to belong to the household of the Julio-Claudian dynasty as she was raised and instructed by her maternal grandfather Augustus. Further, Augustus adopted Tiberius as his son (and heir), and while Tiberius was remarried t
2 CONC o Julia the Elder. Augustus became something of a paternal grandfather to Julia the Elder's children, too, including Julia the Younger. A formal adoption "in the family of the Caesars" among the offspring of Agrippa and Julia the 
2 CONC Elder is however only recorded regarding Vipsania Julia's brothers Gaius — hence Gaius Caesar — and Lucius — hence Lucius Caesar. Her younger sister Agrippina the Elder and youngest full brother, Agrippa Postumus, were named after
2 CONC  their natural father only until Agrippa Postumus was adopted by Augustus as Marcus Julius Caesar Agrippa Postumus. Likewise, her eldest half-sisters, Vipsania Agrippina and Vipsania Marcella, were named after their father. Her yo
2 CONC unger half-brother, unnamed in contemporary sources, was later sometimes dubbed "Tiberillus," after his father Tiberius, and died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
