2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Anmärkning: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anteckningar från http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompey
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompejus Magnus , även kallad Pompey /? p ? mpi / eller Pompejus den store  ( Klassisk latin förkortning CN · POMPEIVS · CN · F · SEX ° n ° MAGNVS ) ( 29 september 106 f.Kr.? 29 september 48 f.Kr.), var en militär och polit
2 CONC isk ledare för den romerska republiken. Han kom från en förmögen italiensk provinsiell bakgrund, och etablerade sig i raden av romerska adeln genom ett framgångsrikt ledarskap i flera kampanjer. Sulla upp honom tillnamnet Magnus (
2 CONC  den stora) och han har vunnit sex segrar .
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompejus gick hans rival Marcus Licinius Crassus och hans allierade och svärfar Julius Caesar i den militärpolitiska allians som kallas det första triumviratet . Efter död Crassus och Julia , Pompejus fru och Caesars dotter , Pomp
2 CONC ejus och Caesar gällande ledning av den romerska staten i ett inbördeskrig. Pompejus ställde sig på Optimater , den konservativa och aristokratiska delen av den romerska senaten . När Caesar besegrade honom i slaget vid Farsalos h
2 CONC an sökt skydd i Egypten där han mördades . Hans karriär och nederlag är betydande i Rom efterföljande omvandling från republiken till principatet och Empire .
2 CONT
2 CONT Uppväxt och politisk debut
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompeius far, Gnaeus Pompejus Strabo , var en förmögen landade italienska provinsiella från Picenum , en av de " nya män ", vars inflytande skulle allt mer dominera romersk politik under sen republiken. Pompejus Strabo besteg trad
2 CONC itionella Cursus honorum , blir kvestor med 104 BC , praetor i 92 f.Kr. och konsul 89 f.Kr. , och fått rykte om girighet , politisk dubbelspel och militär hänsynslöshet . Han stödde Sullas traditionalist Optimater mot popularist a
2 CONC llmänna Marius i första Marian- Sullan krig. Han dog under Sullan kampanjen mot italienarna i 87 f.Kr. , antingen som en olycka av en pandemi pest eller träffad av blixten , eller möjligen bådadera. I Plutarkos vet var hans kropp 
2 CONC släpades från båren av pöbeln .  Hans nitton -årige son Pompey ärvt sina egendomar , hans politiska sympatier och lojalitet hans legioner .
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompejus den store i medelåldern. Marmorbyst i Ny Carlsberg Glyptotek , Köpenhamn, Danmark
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompejus hade tjänstgjort två år i sin fars befallning , och var med i den slutliga rättsakter Marsic sociala kriget mot italienarna. Han återvände till Rom och åtalades för förskingring av plundra : hans förlovning att domaren do
2 CONC tter Antistia säkrat ett snabbt frikännande.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Pompey=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus (Classical Latin: [ˈgnae̯.ʊs pɔmˈpɛj.jʊs ˈmaŋ.nʊs]; 29 September 106 BC – 29 September 48 BC), usually known in English as Pompey /ˈpɒmpiː/ or Pompey the Great, was a military and political leader of the lat
2 CONC e Roman Republic. He came from a wealthy Italian provincial background, and his father had been the first to establish the family among the Roman nobility. Pompey's immense success as a general while still very young enabled him t
2 CONC o advance directly to his first consulship without meeting the normal requirements for office. His success as a military commander in Sulla's Second Civil War resulted in Sulla bestowing the nickname Magnus, "the Great", upon him.
2 CONC  He was consul three times and celebrated three triumphs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In mid-60 BC, Pompey joined Marcus Licinius Crassus and Gaius Julius Caesar in the unofficial military-political alliance known as the First Triumvirate, which Pompey's marriage to Caesar's daughter Julia helped secure. After the 
2 CONC deaths of Julia and Crassus, Pompey sided with the optimates, the conservative faction of the Roman Senate. Pompey and Caesar then contended for the leadership of the Roman state, leading to a civil war. When Pompey was defeated a
2 CONC t the Battle of Pharsalus, he sought refuge in Egypt, where he was assassinated. His career and defeat are significant in Rome's subsequent transformation from Republic to Principate and Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life and political debut'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Pompey's family first gained the position of Consul in 141 BC. Pompey's father, Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo, was a wealthy landed Italian provincial from Picenum, one of the novi homines (new men). Pompeius Strabo ascended the traditio
2 CONC nal cursus honorum, becoming quaestor in 104 BC, praetor in 92 BC and consul in 89 BC, and acquired a reputation for greed, political double-dealing and military ruthlessness. He supported Sulla's traditionalist optimates against 
2 CONC the popularist general Marius in the first Marian-Sullan war.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died during the Marian siege against Rome in 87 BC, either as a casualty of pandemic plague, or struck by lightning, or possibly both. In Plutarch's account, his body was dragged from its bier by the mob. His twenty-year-old so
2 CONC n Pompey inherited his estates, his political leanings and the loyalty of his legions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey had served two years under his father's command, and had participated in the final acts of the Marsic Social War against the Italians. He returned to Rome and was prosecuted for misappropriation of plunder: his betrothal to
2 CONC  the judge's daughter, Antistia, secured a rapid acquittal.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the next few years, the Marians had possession of Italy.[6] When Sulla returned from campaigning against Mithridates in 83 BC, Pompey raised three Picenean legions to support him against the Marian regime of Gnaeus Papirius Ca
2 CONC rbo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sulla and his allies displaced the Marians in Italy and Rome: Sulla, now Dictator of Rome, was impressed by the young Pompey's self-confident performance. He addressed him as imperator and offered him his stepdaughter, Aemilia Sca
2 CONC ura, in marriage. Aemilia – already married and pregnant – divorced her husband and Pompey divorced Antistia. Though Aemilia died in childbirth soon after, the marriage confirmed Pompey's loyalty and greatly boosted his career.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sicily and Africa'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT With the war in Italy over, Sulla sent Pompey against the Marians in Sicily and Africa. In 82 BC, Pompey secured Sicily, guaranteeing Rome's grain supply. He executed Gnaeus Papirius Carbo and his supporters out of hand, which may
2 CONC  have led to his dubbing as the adulescens carnifex (adolescent butcher). In 81 BC, he moved on to the Roman province of Africa, where he defeated Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and the Numidian king Hiarbas, after a hard-fought batt
2 CONC le.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this string of victories, Pompey was proclaimed Imperator by his troops on the field in Africa; once back in Rome, he was given an enthusiastic popular reception and hailed by Sulla as Magnus (the Great) – probably in recogn
2 CONC ition of Pompey's undoubted victories and popularity. However, it seems that Sulla was reluctant to honor him. The young general was still officially a mere privatus (private citizen) who had held no offices in the cursus honorum.
2 CONC  The title may have been meant to cut Pompey down to size; he himself used it only later in his career.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Pompey demanded a triumph for his African victories, Sulla refused; it would have been an unprecedented, even illegal, honour for a young privatus – by law, Pompey would have had to disband his legions. Pompey refused, and pr
2 CONC esented himself expectantly at the gates of Rome, upon which Sulla gave in and granted his request. However, Sulla had his own triumph first, then allowed Metellus Pius his triumph, relegating Pompey to an extra-legal third place 
2 CONC in a quick succession of triumphs.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the day the triumph was to take place, Pompey attempted to upstage both his seniors in a triumphal chariot towed by an elephant, representing his exotic African conquests. The elephant would not fit through the city gate. Some 
2 CONC hasty replanning was needed, much to the embarrassment of Pompey and amusement of those present. His refusal to give in to his troops' near-mutinous demands for cash is believed by historians to have impressed his mentor and Rome'
2 CONC s conservatives, ultimately leading to his rise in Rome's military and political hierarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Quintus Sertorius and Spartacus'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Pompey's career seems to have been driven by desire for military glory and disregard for traditional political constraints. In a very common political move at the time, Pompey married Sulla's step-daughter Mucia Tertia. However, i
2 CONC n the consular elections of 78 BC, he supported Lepidus against Sulla's wishes, causing Sulla to remove Pompey from his will. In 78 BC, Sulla died; when Lepidus revolted, Pompey suppressed him on behalf of the Senate. He subsequen
2 CONC tly asked for proconsular imperium in Hispania to deal with the populares' general Quintus Sertorius, who had held out for the past three years against Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius, one of Sulla's most able generals.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roman aristocracy turned him down, as they were beginning to fear the young, popular and successful general. Pompey resorted to his tried and tested persuasion; he refused to disband his legions until his request was granted. 
2 CONC The senate acceded, reluctantly granted him the title of proconsul and powers equal to those of Metellus, and sent him to Hispania.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his way to Spain, Pompey spent one year subduing rebellious tribes in southern Gaul and organizing the province.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey remained in Hispania from 76 – 71 BC; he was, for a long time, unable to bring the war to an end due to Sertorius' guerrilla tactics. Though he was never able to decisively beat Sertorius (and he nearly met disaster at the 
2 CONC battle of Sucro), he won several campaigns against Sertorius' junior officers and gradually took the advantage over his enemy in a war of attrition. Sertorius was significantly weakened, and by 74 BC, Metellus and Pompey were winn
2 CONC ing city after city. In 72 BC, the Sertorians controlled little more than Lusitania and many soldiers were deserting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, Pompey managed to crush the populares when Sertorius was murdered by his own officer, Marcus Perperna Vento, who was decisively defeated in 72 BC by the young general, at their first battle. By early 71 BC, the whole of H
2 CONC ispania was subdued. Pompey showed a talent for efficient organisation and fair administration in the conquered province; this extended his patronage throughout Hispania and into southern Gaul. Some time in 71 BC, he set off for I
2 CONC taly, along with his army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Crassus was facing Spartacus to end Rome's Third Servile War. Crassus defeated Spartacus, but in his march towards Rome, Pompey encountered the remnants of Spartacus' army; he captured five thousand of them and claimed 
2 CONC the credit for finishing the revolt, which infuriated Crassus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Back in Rome, Pompey was wildly popular. On December 31, 71 BC, he was given a triumph for his victories in Hispania – like his first, it was granted extralegally. To his admirers, he was the most brilliant general of the age, evi
2 CONC dently favoured by the gods and a possible champion of the people's rights. He had successfully faced down Sulla and his Senate; he or his influence might restore the traditional plebeian rights and privileges lost under Sulla's d
2 CONC ictatorship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to this, Pompey was allowed to bypass another ancient Roman tradition; at only 35 years of age and while not even a senator, he was elected Consul by an overwhelming majority vote, and served in 70 BC with Crassus as partner. 
2 CONC Pompey's meteoric rise to the consulship was unprecedented; his tactics offended the traditionalist nobility whose values he claimed to share and defend. He had left them no option but to allow his consulship.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Campaign against the pirates'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Two years after his consulship, Pompey was offered command of a naval task force to deal with piracy in the Mediterranean Sea. The conservative faction of the Senate remained suspicious and wary of him; this seemed yet another ill
2 CONC egal or at least extraordinary appointment. Pompey's supporters for this command – including Caesar – were in the minority, but support was whipped up through his nomination by the Tribune of the Plebs Aulus Gabinius who proposed 
2 CONC a Lex Gabinia; Pompey should have control over the sea and the coasts for 50 miles inland. This would set him above every military leader in the East – it was passed despite vehement opposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Rome's historians, pirates had freely plundered the coastal cities of Greece, Asia and Italy itself. The extent and nature of their threat is questionable; anything that threatened Rome's grain supply was cause for pa
2 CONC nic, and the Romans tended to exaggerate any such threats. Roman public opinion and Pompey's supporters may have exaggerated the solution. Various settlements, peoples and city-states around the Mediterranean had coexisted several
2 CONC  centuries and most had operated small fleets for war, or trade in commodities, including slaves. Their alliances might be loose and temporary or more-or-less permanent; some regarded themselves as nations.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Rome's increasing hegemony, the independent maritime economies of the Mediterranean would have been further marginalised; an increasing number would have resorted to piracy. As long as they met Rome's increasing requirement f
2 CONC or slaves, left her allies and territories untouched and offered her enemies no support, they were tolerated. Some were subsidised. But fear of piracy was potent – and these same pirates, it was later alleged, had assisted Sertori
2 CONC us.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the end of that winter, the preparations were complete. Pompey allocated one of thirteen areas to each of his legates, and sent out their fleets. In forty days, the western Mediterranean was cleared.[26] Dio reported communicat
2 CONC ion was restored between Hispania, Africa, and Italy; and that Pompey then attended to the largest of these alliances, centered on the coast of "Rough Cilicia". After "defeating" its fleet, he induced its surrender with promises o
2 CONC f pardon, and settled many of its people at Soli, which was henceforward called Pompeiopolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT De Souza (2002) finds that Pompey had officially returned the Cilicians to their own cities, which were ideal bases for piracy and not – as Dio would have it – for the dignified reformation of pirates as farmers. Pompey's entire c
2 CONC ampaign is therefore in question; its description as "war" is hyperbole – some form of treaty or payoff is likely, with Pompey as chief negotiator. This was standard practice, but undignified and seldom acknowledged; Rome's genera
2 CONC ls were supposed to wage and win wars. A decade on, in the 50s BC, the Cilicians and pirates in general remained a nuisance to Rome's sea trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rome, however, Pompey was hero; once again, he had guaranteed the grain supply. According to Plutarch, by the end of the summer of 66 BC, his forces had swept the Mediterranean clear of opposition. Pompey was hailed as the firs
2 CONC t man in Rome, Primus inter pares (the first among equals). Cicero could not resist a panegyric:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''"Pompey made his preparations for the war at the end of the winter, entered upon it at the commencement of spring, and finished it in the middle of the summer."''
2 CONT
2 CONT The expedience of his campaign probably guaranteed Pompey his next and even more impressive command, this time in Rome's long-running war against Mithridates. By the 40s BC, Cicero could comment less favourably on the pirate campa
2 CONC ign, and especially the funded "resettlement" at Soli/Pompeiopolis; "we give immunity to pirates and make our allies pay tribute."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pompey in the East'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Pompey spent the rest of that year and the beginning of the next visiting the cities of Cilicia and Pamphylia, and providing for the government of newly conquered territories. In his absence from Rome (66 BC), he was nominated to 
2 CONC succeed Lucius Licinius Lucullus as commander in the Third Mithridatic War against Mithridates VI of Pontus in the East. Pompey's command was proposed by the tribune Gaius Manilius, supported by Caesar and justified by Cicero in p
2 CONC ro Lege Manilia. His brother-in-law Quintus Metellus Celer served underneath him at this time and followed him in his exploits in the East. Like the Gabinian law, it was opposed by the aristocracy, but was carried nonetheless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucullus, a plebeian noble, was incensed at the prospect of his replacement by a "new man" such as Pompey. The outgoing commander and his replacements traded insults. Lucullus called Pompey a "vulture" who fed from the work of oth
2 CONC ers. Lucullus was referring not merely to Pompey's new command against Mithridates, but also his claim to have finished the war against Spartacus.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Pompey's approach, Mithridates strategically withdrew his forces. However, Pompey managed to besiege his camp, but could not prevent his enemy from breaking the encirclement and retreating further east. But, afterward, near Arm
2 CONC enia, Pompey managed to surprise the Pontic army by a daring nocturnal attack and all but destroyed it, leaving the king with no choice but to flee in disarray. Tigranes the Great refused him refuge, so he made his way to his own 
2 CONC dominions in the Cimmerian Bosporus. Pompey secured a treaty with Tigranes, and in 65 BC set out in pursuit of Mithridates, but met resistance from the Caucasian Iberians and Albanians. The Romans won a succession of decisive vict
2 CONC ories over these people on the Abas and the Cyrus rivers and at Seusamora, destroying their forces. Pompey then advanced to Phasis in Colchis and liaised with his legate Servilius, admiral of his Euxine fleet, before decisively de
2 CONC feating Mithridates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey then retraced his steps, wintered at Pontus, and made it into a Roman province. In 64 BC, he marched into Syria, deposed its king, Antiochus XIII Asiaticus, and reconstituted this, too, as a Roman province. In 63 BC, he mov
2 CONC ed south, and established Roman supremacy in Phoenicia and Coele-Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Judea, Pompey intervened in the civil war between Hyrcanus II, who supported the Pharisee faction and Aristobulus II, who supported the Sadducees. The armies of Pompey and Hyrcanus II laid siege to Jerusalem. After three months
2 CONC , the city fell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''"Of the Jews there fell twelve thousand, but of the Romans very few.... and no small enormities were committed about the temple itself, which, in former ages, had been inaccessible, and seen by none; for Pompey went into it, and
2 CONC  not a few of those that were with him also, and saw all that which it was unlawful for any other men to see but only for the high priests. There were in that temple the golden table, the holy candlestick, and the pouring vessels,
2 CONC  and a great quantity of spices; and besides these there were among the treasures two thousand talents of sacred money: yet did Pompey touch nothing of all this, on account of his regard to religion; and in this point also he acte
2 CONC d in a manner that was worthy of his virtue. The next day he gave order to those that had the charge of the temple to cleanse it, and to bring what offerings the law required to God; and restored the high priesthood to Hyrcanus, b
2 CONC oth because he had been useful to him in other respects, and because he hindered the Jews in the country from giving Aristobulus any assistance in his war against him."'' (Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews, book 14, chapter 4; tr.
2 CONC  by William Whiston, available at Project Gutenberg.)
2 CONT
2 CONT During the war in Judea, Pompey heard of Mithridates' suicide; his army had deserted him for his son Pharnaces. In all, Pompey had annexed four new provinces to the Republic: Bithynia et Pontus, Syria, Cilicia, and Crete. Rome's A
2 CONC sian protectorates now extended as far east as the Black Sea and the Caucasus. Pompey's military victories, political settlements and annexations in Asia created Rome's new frontier on the east.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Return to Rome, and third triumph'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT News of Pompey's victories in the east – and probably of his divine honours there – reached Rome before he did. He had cult at Delos and was "saviour" in Samos and Mytelene. Plutarch quotes a wall-graffito in Athens, referring it 
2 CONC to Pompey: "The more you know you're a man, the more you become a god". In Greece, these honours were standard fare for benefactors. In Rome, they would have seemed dangerously monarchic.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Pompey's absence, his old supporter Cicero had risen to the consulship. His old enemy and colleague Crassus supported Caesar. In the Senate and behind its scenes, Pompey was probably equally admired, feared and excluded; on the
2 CONC  streets he was as popular as ever. His eastern victories earned him his third triumph. On his 45th birthday, in 61 BC, he rode the triumphal chariot, a magnificent god-king, but one of Republican form, ritualistically reminded of
2 CONC  his impermanence and mortality. Even so, he was accompanied by a gigantic portrait head of himself, studded with pearls.
2 CONT
2 CONT His third triumph exceeded all others; an unprecedented two days were scheduled for its procession and games (ludi). Spoils, prisoners, army and banners depicting battle scenes wended the triumphal route between the Campus Martius
2 CONC  and the Capitoline temple of Jupiter. To conclude, he gave an immense triumphal banquet and money to the people of Rome, and promised them a new theatre. Plutarch claimed this triumph represented Pompey's – and therefore Rome's –
2 CONC  domination over the entire world, an achievement to outshine even Alexander's.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime, Pompey promised his retiring veterans public lands to farm, then dismissed his armies. It was a reassuringly traditional gesture, but the Senate remained suspicious. They debated and delayed his eastern political 
2 CONC settlements[45] and the promised gifts of public land. From now on, Pompey seems to have toed a cautious line between his enthusiastic popular supporters and the conservatives who seemed so reluctant to acknowledge his solid achie
2 CONC vements. It would lead him into unexpected political alliances.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Caesar and the First Triumvirate'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Although Pompey and Crassus distrusted each other, Crassus' tax farming clients were being rebuffed at the same time Pompey's veterans were being ignored, and by 61 BC, their grievances had pushed them both into an alliance with C
2 CONC aesar, six years younger than Pompey, returning from service in Hispania and ready to seek the consulship for 59 BC. Their political alliance, known subsequently as the First Triumvirate, operated to the benefit of each. Pompey an
2 CONC d Crassus would make Caesar Consul, and Caesar would use his consular power to promote their claims.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caesar's consulship of 59 BC brought Pompey land for his veterans, confirmation of his Asian political settlements and a new wife. She was Caesar's daughter, Julia; Pompey was said to be besotted by her. In the same year, Clodius 
2 CONC renounced his patrician status, was adopted into a plebeian gens and was elected a Tribune of the plebs. At the end of his consulship, Caesar secured proconsular command in Gaul. Pompey was given the governorship of Hispania Ulter
2 CONC ior, but remained in Rome to oversee the grain supply as curator annonae.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his preoccupation with his new wife, Pompey handled the grain issue well. His political acumen was less sure. When Clodius turned on him in turn, Pompey defended himself by supporting Cicero's recall from exile (57 BC). On
2 CONC ce back in Rome, Cicero stepped back into his role as Pompey's defender and Clodius' antagonist, but Pompey himself retreated to his lovely young wife and his theatre plans; such behaviour was not expected of the once dazzling you
2 CONC ng general.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey might equally have been obsessed, exhausted and frustrated. His own party had not forgiven him for allowing Cicero's expulsion. Some tried to persuade him that Crassus was plotting his assassination. Meanwhile, Caesar seeme
2 CONC d set on outstripping both his colleagues in generalship and popularity.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 56 BC, the bonds between the three men were fraying. Caesar called first Crassus, then Pompey, to a secret meeting, the Lucca Conference, in the northern Italian town of Lucca to rethink their joint strategy. They agreed that P
2 CONC ompey and Crassus would again stand for the consulship in 55 BC. Once elected, they would extend Caesar's command in Gaul by five years. At the end of their joint consular year, Crassus would have the influential and lucrative gov
2 CONC ernorship of Syria, and use this as a base to conquer Parthia. Pompey would keep Hispania in absentia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 55 BC, Pompey and Crassus were elected as consuls, against a background of bribery, civil unrest and electioneering violence. Pompey's new theatre was inaugurated in the same year. It was Rome's first permanent theatre, a gigan
2 CONC tic, architecturally daring, self-contained complex on the Campus Martius, complete with shops, multi-service buildings, gardens and a temple to Venus Victrix. The latter connected its donor to Aeneas, a son of Venus and ancestor 
2 CONC of Rome itself. In its portico, the statuary, paintings and personal wealth of foreign kings could be admired at leisure. Pompey's triumph lived on. His theatre made an ideal meeting place for his supporters.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From confrontation to war'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 54 BC, Julia, Caesar's only child and Pompey's wife, died in childbirth along with her baby. Pompey and Caesar shared their grief and condolences, but Julia's death broke their family bonds. The following year, Crassus, his son
2 CONC  Publius and most of his army were annihilated by the Parthians at Carrhae. Caesar, not Pompey, was now Rome's great new general and the fragile balance of power between them was under threat. Public anxiety spilled over: rumours 
2 CONC circulated that Pompey would be offered dictatorship for the sake of law and order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caesar sought a second matrimonial alliance with Pompey, offering his grandniece Octavia (the sister of the future emperor Augustus). This time, though, Pompey refused. In 52 BC, he married Cornelia Metella, the very young widow o
2 CONC f Crassus's son Publius, and the daughter of Caecilius Metellus Scipio, one of Caesar’s greatest enemies. Pompey was drifting back toward the optimates. It can be presumed that they thought him the lesser of two evils.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, Clodius was murdered. When his supporters burned down the Senate House in retaliation, the Senate appealed to Pompey. He reacted with ruthless efficiency. Cicero, defending the accused murderer Titus Annius Milo,
2 CONC  was so shaken by a Forum seething with armed soldiers, he was unable to complete his defense.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once order was restored, the Senate and Cato avoided granting Pompey dictatorship – it recalled Sulla and his bloody proscriptions. Instead they made him sole Consul; this gave him sweeping, but limited, powers. A Dictator could n
2 CONC ot be lawfully punished for measures taken during his office. As sole Consul, Pompey would be answerable for his actions once out of office.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Caesar was fighting against Vercingetorix in Gaul, Pompey proceeded with a legislative agenda for Rome. Its details suggested covert alliance with Caesar's enemies: among his various legal and military reforms was a law allo
2 CONC wing retrospective prosecution for electoral bribery. Caesar's allies correctly interpreted this as a threat to Caesar once his imperium ended. Pompey also prohibited Caesar from standing for the consulship in absentia, though thi
2 CONC s had been permitted under past laws.
2 CONT
2 CONT This seemed to put paid to Caesar's plans after his term in Gaul expired. Finally, in 51 BC, Pompey was more forthright; Caesar would not be permitted to stand for Consul unless he relinquished his armies. This would, of course, l
2 CONC eave Caesar defenseless before his enemies. As Cicero sadly noted, Pompey had been diminished by age, uncertainty, his fear of Caesar and the strain of being the chosen tool of a quarreling oligarchy of optimates. The coming confl
2 CONC ict seemed inevitable.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Civil war and assassination'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT In the beginning, Pompey claimed he could defeat Caesar and raise armies merely by stamping his foot on the soil of Italy, but by the spring of 49 BC, with Caesar crossing the Rubicon and his invading legions sweeping down the pen
2 CONC insula, Pompey ordered the abandonment of Rome. His legions retreated south towards Brundisium, where Pompey intended to find renewed strength by waging war against Caesar in the east. By using his strategic resources in the East,
2 CONC  as well as his naval strength to defeat Caesar's forces. In the process, neither Pompey nor the Senate thought of taking the vast treasury with them, probably thinking Caesar would not dare take it for himself. It was left conven
2 CONC iently in the Temple of Saturn when Caesar and his forces entered Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barely eluding Caesar in Brundisium, Pompey crossed over into Epirus, where, during Caesar's Spanish campaign, Pompey had gathered a large force in Macedonia, comprising nine legions reinforced by contingents from the Roman allies
2 CONC  in the east.[52] His fleet, recruited from the maritime cities in the east, controlled the Adriatic. Nevertheless, Caesar managed to cross over into Epirus in November 49 BC, and proceeded to capture Apollonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey managed to arrive in time to save Dyrrhachium, and he then attempted to wait Caesar out during the siege of Dyrrhachium, scoring a victory. Yet, by failing to pursue at the critical moment of Caesar's defeat, Pompey threw a
2 CONC way the chance to destroy Caesar's much smaller army. As Caesar himself said, "Today the enemy would have won, if they had a commander who was a winner" (Plutarch, 65E).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Suetonius, it was at this point that Caesar said that "that man (Pompey) does not know how to win a war." With Caesar on their backs, the conservatives led by Pompey fled to Greece. Caesar and Pompey had their final s
2 CONC howdown at the Battle of Pharsalus in 48 BC. The fighting was bitter for both sides, and although Pompey was expected to win, due to advantage in numbers, the brilliant tactics and the superior fighting abilities of Caesar's veter
2 CONC ans led to a victory for Caesar. Pompey met his wife Cornelia and his son Sextus Pompeius on the island of Mytilene. He then wondered where to go next. The decision of running to one of the eastern kingdoms was overruled in favour
2 CONC  of Egypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his arrival in Egypt, Pompey's fate was decided by the counselors of the young king Ptolemy XIII. While Pompey waited offshore, they argued the cost of offering him refuge with Caesar already en route to Egypt; the king's eu
2 CONC nuch Pothinus won out. In the final dramatic passages of his biography, Plutarch had Cornelia watch anxiously from the trireme as Pompey left in a small boat with a few sullen, silent comrades, and headed for what appeared to be a
2 CONC  welcoming party on the Egyptian shore at Pelusium. As Pompey rose to disembark, he was stabbed to death by his betrayers, Achillas, Septimius and Salvius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plutarch has him meet his fate with great dignity, on his 60th birthday. His body remained on the shoreline, to be cremated by his loyal freeman Philip on the rotten planks of a fishing boat. His head and seal were presented to Ca
2 CONC esar, who, according to Plutarch, mourned this insult to the greatness of his former ally and son-in-law, and punished his assassins and their Egyptian co-conspirators, putting both Achillas and Pothinus to death. Pompey's ashes w
2 CONC ere eventually returned to Cornelia, who carried them to his country house near Alba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassius Dio describes Caesar's reactions with skepticism, and considers Pompey's own political misjudgments, rather than treachery, as instrumental in his downfall. In Appian's account of the civil war, Caesar has Pompey's severed
2 CONC  head interred in Alexandria, in ground reserved for a new temple to the goddess Nemesis, whose divine functions included the punishment of hubris. For Pliny, the humiliation of Pompey's end is anticipated by the vaunting pride of
2 CONC  his oversized portrait-head, studded entirely with pearls, and carried in procession during his greatest Triumph. Suetonius, however, states of Caesar "He even restored to their position the statues of Lucius Sulla and of Pompey 
2 CONC which had been broken up by the common people." 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Generalship'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Pompey's military glory was second to none for a few decades. Yet, his skills were occasionally criticized by some of his contemporaries. Sertorius or Lucullus, for instance, were especially critical. Pompey's tactics were usually
2 CONC  efficient, albeit not particularly innovative or imaginative. They could prove insufficient against greater tacticians. However, Pharsalus was his only decisive defeat. At times, he was reluctant to risk an open battle. While not
2 CONC  hugely charismatic, Pompey could display tremendous bravery and fighting skills on the battlefield, something which provided inspiration to his men. While being a superb commander, Pompey also earned a reputation for stealing oth
2 CONC er generals' victories.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other hand, Pompey is usually considered an outstanding strategist and organizer, who could win campaigns without displaying genius on the battlefield, but simply by constantly outmaneuvering his opponents and gradually pus
2 CONC hing them into a desperate situation. Pompey was a great forward planner, and had tremendous organizational skill, which allowed him to devise grand strategies and operate effectively with large armies. During his campaigns in the
2 CONC  east, he acted like a sledgehammer, relentlessly pursuing his enemies, and choosing the ground for his battles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Above all, he was often able to adapt to his enemies. On many occasions, he acted very swiftly and decisively, as he did during his campaigns in Sicily and Africa, or against the Cilician pirates. During the Sertorian war, on the 
2 CONC other hand, Pompey was beaten several times by Sertorius. Therefore, he decided to resort to a war of attrition, in which he would avoid open battles against his chief opponent but instead try to gradually regain the strategic adv
2 CONC antage by capturing his fortresses and cities and defeating his junior officers. In some instances, Sertorius showed up and forced Pompey to abandon a siege, only to see him strike somewhere else.[64] This strategy was not spectac
2 CONC ular but it led to constant territorial gains and did much to demoralize the Sertorian forces. By 72 BC, the year of his assassination, Sertorius was already in a desperate situation and his troops were deserting. Against Perpenna
2 CONC , a tactician far inferior to his former commander in chief, Pompey decided to revert to a more aggressive strategy and he scored a decisive victory that effectively ended the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Against Caesar too, his strategy was sound. During the campaign of Greece, he managed to regain the initiative, join his forces to that of Metellus Scipio (something that Caesar wanted to avoid) and trap his enemy. His strategic p
2 CONC osition was hence much better than that of Caesar and could have starved his army to death. However, he was finally compelled to fight an open battle by his allies and his conventional tactics proved no match to that of Caesar and
2 CONC  his better-trained troops.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Later portrayals and reputation'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT For the historians of his own and later Roman periods, Pompey fit the trope of the great man who achieved extraordinary triumphs through his own efforts, yet fell from power and was, in the end, murdered through treachery.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a hero of the Republic, who seemed once to hold the Roman world in his palm, only to be brought low by Caesar. Pompey was idealized as a tragic hero almost immediately after Pharsalus and his murder. Plutarch portrayed him 
2 CONC as a Roman Alexander the Great, pure of heart and mind, destroyed by the cynical ambitions of those around him. This portrayal of him survived into the Renaissance and Baroque periods, for example in Corneille's play The Death of 
2 CONC Pompey (1642). In spite of his war against Caesar, Pompey was still widely celebrated during the imperial period, as the conqueror of the orient. At Augustus' funeral procession, pictures of him were carried as he was still widely
2 CONC  considered as the great conqueror of the Orient. As a triumphator, he also had numerous statues in Rome, one of which was on the forum of Augustus. Though the imperial power did not honor him as much as his archenemy, who was con
2 CONC sidered a god, his reputation among many aristocrats and historians was equal or even superior to that of Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pompey has appeared as a character in several modern novels, plays, motion pictures, and other media.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Theater, film and television''
2 CONT * A theatrical portrayal was John Masefield's play The Tragedy of Pompey the Great (1910).
2 CONT * In the opening scene of the 1961 film King of Kings, he is played by actor Conrado San Martín.
2 CONT * In the television series Xena: Warrior Princess, he is portrayed by actor Jeremy Callaghan.
2 CONT * Chris Noth portrays Pompey in the 2002 miniseries Julius Caesar.
2 CONT * He appears as a major character in the first season of the HBO series Rome, in which he is portrayed by Kenneth Cranham.
2 CONT * In 2006 he was played by John Shrapnel in the BBC docu-drama Ancient Rome: The Rise and Fall of an Empire.
2 CONT * In the television series Spartacus: War of the Damned, he is portrayed by actor Joel Tobeck.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Literature''
2 CONT
2 CONT * In Colleen McCullough's Masters of Rome series of historical novels, Pompey's youthful exploits are depicted in Fortune's Favorites, the formation of the First Triumvirate and his marriage to Julia is a large part of Caesar's Wo
2 CONC men and his loss of Julia, the dissolution of the First Triumvirate, his later political career, the civil war between him and Caesar and his eventual defeat and his betrayal and murder in Egypt are all told in Caesar.
2 CONT * In comics, he appears as Julius Caesar's foe throughout the The Adventures of Alix series.
2 CONT * Pompey is a recurring character in the Roma Sub Rosa series of novels by Steven Saylor, portraying his role in the Civil War with Caesar. His final appearance is in Saylor's novel The Judgment of Caesar, graphically depicting hi
2 CONC s murder by Ptolemy in Egypt.
2 CONT * Pompey also appears frequently in the SPQR series by John Maddox Roberts, narrated by Senator Decius Metellus, a fictional nephew of Caecilius Metellus Pius. Decius despises Pompey as a glory-seeker and credit-grabber, while ack
2 CONC nowledging that he is a political dunce who was eventually swept up into the optimates feud with Caesar.
2 CONT * Pompey is a major recurring character in Robert Harris's trilogy of the life of Cicero, (Imperium, Lustrum and Dictator), in which Pompey is portrayed as bombastic and dim-witted, though fearsome.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriages and offspring'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT # First wife, Antistia
2 CONT # Second wife, Aemilia Scaura (Sulla's stepdaughter)
2 CONT # Third wife, Mucia Tertia (whom he divorced for adultery, according to Cicero's letters)
2 CONT # Offspring of marriage between Mucia and Pompey Magnus (1. Gnaeus Pompeius, executed in 45 BC, after the Battle of Munda, 2. Pompeia Magna, married to Faustus Cornelius Sulla; ancestor of Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus (Claudia Antonia's
2 CONC  first husband), 3. Sextus Pompey, who would rebel in Sicily against Augustus)
2 CONT # Fourth wife Julia (daughter of Caesar)
2 CONT # Fifth wife, Cornelia Metella (daughter of Metellus Scipio)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chronology of Pompey's life and career'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * 106 BC September 29– Born in Picenum
2 CONT * 83 BC– Aligns with Sulla, after his return from the Mithridatic War against King Mithridates IV of Pontus;
2 CONT * 83 BC- Pompey raises a legion and cavalry in hopes of joining Sulla
2 CONT * 82 BC- Marriage to Aemilia Scaura at the beheast of Sulla, Aemilia is already pregnant and eventually dies during childbirth
2 CONT * 82–81 BC– Defeats Gaius Marius's allies in Sicily (Autumn of 82 BC) and Africa after his victory in Sicily
2 CONT * 81 BC– Returns to Rome and celebrates First triumph
2 CONT * 80 BC- Pompey marries Mucia of the Mucii Scaevolae Family
2 CONT * 79 BC- Pompey supports the election of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, Lepidus openly revolts against the senate a few months later, Pompey suppresses the rebellion with an army raised from Picenum and puts down the rebellion killing t
2 CONC he senior legate Marcus Junius Brutus, father of Brutus who assassinated Julius Caesar
2 CONT * 76–71 BC– Campaign in Hispania against Sertorius
2 CONT * 71 BC– Returns to Italy and participates in the suppression of a slave rebellion led by Spartacus; Second triumph
2 CONT * 70 BC– First consulship (with M. Licinius Crassus)
2 CONT * 67 BC– Defeats the pirates and goes to Asia province
2 CONT * 66–61 BC– Defeats King Mithridates of Pontus; end of the Third Mithridatic War
2 CONT * 64–63 BC– Pompey's March through Syria, the Levant, and Judea
2 CONT * 61 BC September 29– Third triumph
2 CONT * 59 BC April– The first triumvirate is constituted; Pompey allies to Julius Caesar and Licinius Crassus; marriage to Julia (daughter of Julius Caesar)
2 CONT * 58–55 BC– Governs Hispania Ulterior by proxy, construction of Pompey's Theater
2 CONT * 55 BC– Second consulship (with M. Licinius Crassus), Dedication of the Theatre of Pompey
2 CONT * 54 BC– Julia dies; the first triumvirate ends
2 CONT * 52 BC– Serves as sole consul for intercalary month,[70] third ordinary consulship with Metellus Scipio for the rest of the year; marriage to Cornelia Metella
2 CONT * 51 BC– Forbids Caesar (in Gaul) to stand for consulship in absentia
2 CONT * 50 BC– Falls dangerously ill with fever in Campania, but is saved 'by public prayers'
2 CONT * 49 BC– Caesar crosses the Rubicon River and invades Italy; Pompey retreats to Greece with the conservatives
2 CONT * 48 BC– Caesar defeats Pompey's army near Pharsalus, Greece. Pompey retreats to Egypt and is killed at Pelusium.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompey
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://es.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piceno
2 CONT
2 CONT Regio V Picenum is the fifth Roman region in the Augustan era. Denomination
2 CONT
2 CONT As there was no official name at the time of the Augustinian regions, the region is currently named Regio V Picenum , using the name of those who inhabited the region prior to the Roman conquest, the Picenos . Regio V also include
2 CONC d the territory of pretuzos (ager Pretutianus) Territory
2 CONT
2 CONT It corresponded to the territory of the current Marche , south of the Esino River, of the current Province of Teramo and part of the current province of Pescara in the Abruzzos , comprising approximately the lands between the Esin
2 CONC o River to the north, the Adriatic to the east, the Apennines to the west and the Saline river to the south.
2 CONT
2 CONT A description of the territory of Regio V is present in Naturalis Historia de Plinio el Viejo (Plinio il Vecchio, Naturalis Historia , Book III, paragraph 110-111) [ 1 ] where the cities that were in the region are named:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Fifth region Piceni est, quondam uberrimae multinational. C C C L X ¯ Picentium in fidem pR venere. orti sunt to Sabinis vote vere sacro. faint ab Aterno amne, ubi nunc ager Hadrianus et Hadria cologne to mari V I ¯ p. flumen 
2 CONC Vomanum, ager Praetutianus Palmensisque, item Castrum Novum, flumen Batinum, Truentum cum amne, quod solum Liburnorum in Italia relicum est, flumina Albula, Tessuinum, Helvinum, quo finitur Praetutiana regio et Picentium incipit.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cupra oppidum, Castellum Firmanorum, and super id colony Asculum, Piceni nobilissima. intus Novana; in ora Cluana, Potentia, Numana a Siculis condita, ab iisdem cologne Ancona, average adposita Cunero in ipso flectentis orae c
2 CONC ubito, to Gargano C L X X X I I I ¯. intus Auximates, Beregrani, Cingulani, Cuprenses cognomine Montani, Falerienses, Pausulani, Planinenses, Ricinenses, Septempedani, Tolentinates, Traienses, Urbesalvia Pollentini.
2 CONT     Plinio il Vecchio, Naturalis Historia , III, 112 
2 CONT
2 CONT The region became part of Roman territory in the third century BC. C.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was the birthplace of the Roman senator and consul Cneo Pompeyo Estrabón , father of Pompeyo Magno . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT     Nereo Alfieri , Region V dell'Italia augustea nella "Naturalis Historia" , in Plinio il Vecchio sotto il profilo storico e letterario , II, Como 1982 , pp. 199-219 (in Italian)
2 CONT     Nereo Alfieri, Scritti di topografia antica sulle Marche , by Gianfranco Paci, Editrice Tipigraf, 2000 , ISBN 88-87994-09-9 (in Italian)
2 CONT     Mario Luni, Archeologia nelle Marche , 2003 , ISBN 88-392-0744-9 (in Italian) 
