2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Flavia Titiana from Promptuarii Iconum Insigniorum Flavia Titiana was a Roman empress, wife of emperor Pertinax, who ruled briefly in 193 (known as "Year of the Five Emperors").
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavia Titiana was the daughter of a Senator, Titus Flavius Claudius Sulpicianus, and sister of Titus Flavius Titianus (b. ca 165), Consul suffectus ca 200 who married to Postumia Varia (b. ca 175). Her maternal grandfather was Ti
2 CONC tus Flavius Titianus (b. ca 95), who was Praefectus of Aegyptus between 126 and 133 and was believed to be a third son of Titus Flavius Clemens and wife Flavia Domitilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Publius Helvius Pertinax, a rich self made man who had made a successful military and civil career and bore two children, a boy called Publius Helvius Pertinax and an unknown daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pertinax was proclaimed emperor after the murder of Commodus on January 1, 193. While the new princeps was offering the customary sacrifice on the Capitoline Hill, the Roman Senate gave Flavia Titiana the honorary title of Augusta
2 CONC . After the murder of Pertinax by the praetorians on March 28 neither Flavia nor her children were hurt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The highly unreliable Historia Augusta claims that Flavia Titiana "carried on an amour quite openly with a man who sang to the lyre" but Pertinax was not concerned.[1
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavia_Titiana
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Flavia Titiana=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavia Titiana was a Roman empress, wife of emperor Pertinax, who ruled briefly in 193 (known as "Year of the Five Emperors").
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavia Titiana was the daughter of a Senator, Titus Flavius Claudius Sulpicianus, and sister of Titus Flavius Titianus (b. ca 165), Consul suffectus ca 200 who married Postumia Varia (b. ca 175). Her maternal grandfather was Titus
2 CONC  Flavius Titianus (b. ca 95), who was Praefectus of Aegyptus between 126 and 133 and was believed to be a third son of Titus Flavius Clemens and wife Flavia Domitilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Publius Helvius Pertinax, a rich self-made man who had a successful military and civil career. She bore two children, a boy called Publius Helvius Pertinax and an unknown daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pertinax was proclaimed emperor after the murder of Commodus on January 1, 193. While the new princeps was offering the customary sacrifice on the Capitoline Hill, the Roman Senate gave Flavia Titiana the honorary title of Augusta
2 CONC . After the murder of Pertinax by the Praetorian Guard on March 28, neither Flavia nor her children were hurt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The highly unreliable Historia Augusta claims that Flavia Titiana "carried on an amour quite openly with a man who sang to the lyre", but Pertinax was not concerned.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavia_Titiana
