2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Marcus Atius=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Atius Balbus (105-51 BC) was a 1st-century Roman who served as a praetor in 62 BC and became governor of Sardinia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbus was born and raised in Aricia into a political family and was the son and heir of the elder Marcus Atius Balbus (148-87 BC). His mother was Pompeia the sister to consul Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo, father of Pompey Magnus, a mem
2 CONC ber of the so-called, unofficial, First Triumvirate with Julius Caesar and Marcus Licinius Crassus.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of the elder Balbus came from a Roman senatorial family of plebs status from Aricia (modern Ariccia, Italy). "Balbus" in Latin means stammer.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT During the consulship of Julius Caesar in 59 BC, Balbus was appointed along with Pompey to a board of commissioners under a Julian Law to divide estates in Campania among the commoners. Cicero stated that Pompey would say as a jok
2 CONC e about Balbus, that he was not a person of any importance.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personal life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Julia Minor, second eldest sister of the dictator Gaius Julius Caesar. Julia bore him three daughters and they were:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Atia Balba Prima was the mother of Quintus Pedius who served as a general and consul.
2 CONT * Atia Balba Caesonia became the mother of Octavia Minor, who was the fourth wife of triumvir Mark Antony, and of the first Emperor Augustus.
2 CONT * Atia Balba Tertia was the mother of Lucius Pinarius, a political supporter of Mark Antony.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Atius
