2 CONC tive record of NPR's programming is the audio.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Quintus Tullius Cicero=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Tullius Cicero (/ˈsɪsᵻroʊ/; Classical Latin: [ˈkɪkɛroː]; 102 BC – 43 BC) was the younger brother of the celebrated orator, philosopher and statesman Marcus Tullius Cicero. He was born into a family of the equestrian order,
2 CONC  as the son of a wealthy landowner in Arpinum, some 100 kilometres south-east of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Cicero's well-to-do father arranged for him to be educated with his brother in Rome, Athens and probably Rhodes in 79-77 BC. He married about 70 BC Pomponia (sister of his brother's friend Atticus), a dominant woman of strong pers
2 CONC onality. He divorced her after a long disharmonious marriage with much bickering between the spouses in late 45 BC. His brother, Marcus, tried several times to reconcile the spouses, but to no avail. The couple had a son born in 6
2 CONC 6 BC named Quintus Tullius Cicero after his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus was Aedile in 66 BC, Praetor in 62 BC, and Propraetor of the Province of Asia for three years 61-59 BC.[5] Under Caesar during the Gallic Wars, he was Legatus (accompanying Caesar on his second expedition to Britain in 54 
2 CONC BC and surviving a Nervian siege of his camp during Ambiorix's revolt), and under his brother in Cilicia in 51 BC. During the civil wars he supported the Pompeian faction, obtaining the pardon of Caesar later.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Second Triumvirate when the Roman Republic was again in civil war, Quintus, his son, and his famous brother, were all proscribed. He fled from Tusculum with his brother. Later Quintus went home to bring back money for t
2 CONC ravelling expenses. His son, Quintus minor, hid his father, and did not reveal the hiding place although he was tortured. When Quintus heard this, he gave himself up to try and save his son; however, both father and son, and his f
2 CONC amous brother, were all killed in 43 BC, as proscribed persons.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personality and relationship with brother Marcus'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus is depicted by Caesar as a brave soldier and an inspiring military leader. At a critical moment in the Gallic Wars he rallied his legion and retrieved an apparently hopeless position. Caesar commended him for this with the
2 CONC  words Ciceronem pro eius merito legionemque collaudat (He praised Cicero and his men very highly, as they deserved) (Bello Gallico 5.52). However, later the legate was purportedly responsible for a near-disaster in Gaul but does 
2 CONC not receive condemnation from Caesar as a result. (Bello Gallico 6.36)
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus had an impulsive temperament and had fits of cruelty during military operations, a behaviour frowned on by Romans of that time. The Roman (and Stoic) ideal was to control one’s emotions even in battle. Quintus Cicero also 
2 CONC liked old-fashioned and harsh punishments, like putting a person convicted of patricide into a sack and throwing him out in the sea (the felon was severely scourged then sewn into a stout leather bag with a dog, a snake, a rooster
2 CONC , and a monkey, and the bag was thrown into a river).[8] This punishment he meted out during his propraetorship of Asia.[9] (For the Romans, both patricide and matricide were one of the worst crimes.) His brother confesses in one 
2 CONC of his letters to his friend Titus Pomponius Atticus (written in 51 BC while he was Proconsul of Cilicia and had taken Quintus as legatus with him) that he dares not leave Quintus alone as he is afraid of what kind of sudden ideas
2 CONC  he might have.[10] On the positive side, Quintus was utterly honest, even as a governor of a province, in which situation many Romans shamelessly amassed private property for themselves. He was also a well-educated man, reading G
2 CONC reek tragedies - and writing some tragedies himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT The relationship between the brothers was mostly affectionate, except for a period of serious disagreement during Caesar’s dictatorship 49-44 BC.[11] The many letters from Marcus ad Quintum fratrem show how deep and affectionate t
2 CONC he brothers’ relationship was, though Marcus Cicero often played the role of the "older and more experienced" lecturing to his brother what was the right thing to do. Quintus might also feel at times, that the self-centred Marcus 
2 CONC thought only how his brother might hinder or help Marcus’ own career on the Cursus honorum.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Authorship'''
2 CONT As an author he wrote during the Gallic wars four tragedies in Greek style. Three of them were titled Troas, Erigones, and Electra, but all are lost. He also wrote several poems on the second expedition of Caesar to Britannia, thr
2 CONC ee epistles to Tiro (extant) and a fourth one to his brother. The long letter Commentariolum Petitionis (Little handbook on electioneering) has also survived, although its validity has been much questioned. It is in any case a val
2 CONC uable guide to political behaviour in Cicero’s time.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
