1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sporus''' was a young boy whom the Roman Emperor Nero favored, had castrated, and married.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Origins of the name
2 CONT * 2 Life
2 CONT * 2.1 Marriage to Nero
2 CONT * 2.2 After Nero and death
2 CONT * 3 In fiction
2 CONT * 4 Bibliography
2 CONT * 5 See also
2 CONT * 6 Notes
2 CONT * 7 References
2 CONT
2 CONT =Origins of the name=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporus derives from the ancient Greek word σπορά spora, meaning "seed, sowing," related to σπόρος sporos, "sowing," and σπείρειν speirein, "to sow." In all references about this story, he is always called Sporus, a male name, when
2 CONC  the female would be Spora.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Roman naming conventions, he would gain the nomen and praenomen of his former master retaining his former name as a cognomen. In that case, assuming that it was Nero who freed him (which is not clear), his full na
2 CONC me would be Nero Claudius Sporus (after Nero Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus).
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about Sporus's background except that he was a young man to whom Nero took a liking. He was a Puer delicatus, who were sometimes castrated in an effort to preserve their youthful qualities. The puer delicatus gener
2 CONC ally was a child-slave chosen by his master for his beauty as a "boy toy." Despite this, Cassius Dio called him a freedman.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriage to Nero==
2 CONT
2 CONT Nero's wife, Poppaea Sabina, was killed by Nero in 65. In the beginning of 66, he married Statilia Messalina. Later that year or in 67 he married Sporus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nero had Sporus's testicles removed and during their marriage, Nero had Sporus appear in public as his wife wearing the regalia that was customary for Roman empresses. He then took Sporus to Greece and back to Rome, making Calvia 
2 CONC Crispinilla serve as "mistress of wardrobe" to Sporus, ''epitropeia ten peri estheta''. Nero had earlier married another freedman, Pythagoras, who played the role of Nero's husband, as Sporus played the role of Nero's wife. In add
2 CONC ition to other forms of address, Sporus was termed "Lady", "Empress", and "Mistress". Suetonius quotes one Roman who lived around this time who remarked that the world would have been better off if Nero's father Gnaeus Domitius Ah
2 CONC enobarbus had married someone more like the castrated boy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suetonius places his account of the Nero–Sporus relationship in his reports of Nero's sexual aberrations, between his raping a vestal virgin and committing incest with his mother. Some think that Nero used his marriage to Sporus t
2 CONC o assuage the feelings of guilt he felt for kicking his pregnant wife Sabina to death. Dion Cassius, in a more detailed account, writes that Sporus bore an uncanny resemblance to Sabina and that Nero called Sporus by her name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly before Nero's death, during the Calends festival, Sporus presented Nero with a ring with a gemstone depicting the Rape of Proserpina, in which the ruler of the underworld forces a young girl to become his bride. It was at 
2 CONC the time considered one of the many bad omens of Nero's fall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporus was one of the four companions on the emperor's last journey in June 68, along with Epaphroditos, Neophytus and Phaon. It was to him, and not to his wife Messalina, that Nero turned as he began the ritual lamentations befor
2 CONC e taking his own life.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==After Nero and death==
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after Nero's death, Sporus was taken to the care of the Praetorian prefect Nymphidius Sabinus, who took part in the final conspiracy against Nero and persuaded the Praetorian Guard to desert him but when he attempted to have 
2 CONC himself declared emperor, he was killed by his own soldiers. Nymphidius treated Sporus as though they were married, and called him "Poppaea".
2 CONT
2 CONT After Nymphidius' death, Sporus would, in the year 69, become involved with Otho, who was also killed by his enemies. He died later that year: Vitellius planned for Sporus to play the title role of the Rape of Persephone (the same
2 CONC  theme of the ring Sporus gave Nero at the Calendas), for the viewing enjoyment of the crowds during one of the gladiatorial combats. Sporus then committed suicide to avoid the humiliating show. He was probably under 20 years old 
2 CONC at the time of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT =In fiction=
2 CONT
2 CONT * In 1735, Alexander Pope wrote a satirical poem that mocked the courtier Lord Hervey, who had been accused of homosexuality a few years earlier. He scoffs at using so strong weapon as satire upon a weak and effeminate target like
2 CONC  Sporus, "that mere white curd of ass's milk", and in a famous line Pope poses the rhetorical question: "Who breaks a butterfly upon a wheel?"
2 CONT * Sporus is the main character in the 2001 historical novel ''Moi, Sporus, prêtre et putain'' (English: ''I, Sporus, priest and whore'') by Cristina Rodríguez.
2 CONT
