2 CONT
2 CONT '''Furia''' [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livia_Medullina#cite_note-1 a] '''Livia Medullina Camilla''' (ca. 6 BC-ca 10 AD) was the '''second fiancee''' of the future [https://www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-Emperor/600000000
2 CONC 2119140027 '''Emperor Claudius'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livia_Medullina Livia Medullina], wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Born:''' Furia Livia Medullina Camilla circa 6 BC
2 CONT '''Died:'''circa 10 AD (aged 15)
2 CONT '''Known for:''' '''Fiancee of''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-Emperor/6000000002119140027 '''Claudius''']
2 CONT '''Parent:''' [https://www.geni.com/family-tree/index/6000000194120568896 '''Marcus Furius Camillus'''] (father)
2 CONT '''Relatives:''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Lucius-Arruntius-Camillus-Scribonianus/6000000194120493829 '''Lucius Arruntius Camillus Scribonianus''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livia_Medullina#Biography Biography]==
2 CONT
2 CONT Medullina was the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Marcus-Furius-Camillus-Roman-senator-and-consul/6000000194120568896 '''Marcus Furius Camillus'''] consul in AD 8, who was a close friend of the emperor Tiberius and Livia 
2 CONC Scriboniana, the daughter of Marcus Livius Drusus Libo (born Lucius Scribonius Libo), the adopted brother of the empress Livia. As a child, Medullina may have been called by the names '''Furia''' and '''Camilla''' (possibly along 
2 CONC with her other names), but as an adult she seems to have been referred to mainly as "Livia Medullina". Her nomen gentilicium Furia is not actually attested but can be assumed to have been used at some point due to Roman naming con
2 CONC ventions for women at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Medullina's brother was [https://www.geni.com/people/Lucius/6000000194120493829 '''Lucius Arruntius Camillus Scribonianus'''], who had been adopted by [https://www.geni.com/people/Lucius-Arruntius/6000000194122442821 '''Lucius Arr
2 CONC untius''']. He was consul in AD 32, as the colleague of [https://www.geni.com/people/Consul-32-Gnaeus-Domitius-Ahenobarbus/6000000007037369475 '''Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT Medullina was betrothed to [https://www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-Emperor/6000000002119140027 '''Claudius'''] some time after his first engagement, to his relative [https://www.geni.com/people/Aemilia-Lepida-Minor/600000000
2 CONC 2106239618 '''Aemilia Lepida'''], '''was broken''' by Augustus in AD 8, due to the disgrace of [https://www.geni.com/people/Aemilia-Lepida-Minor/6000000002106239618 '''Aemilia''']'s parents. Tiberius probably pushed for the new be
2 CONC trothal, in order to reward his friend with a connection to the imperial family. The betrothal of Medullina and Claudius is attested by an inscription erected by Camilla's pedagogue, dedicated to "Medullina Camilli f. Ti. Claudi N
2 CONC eronis Germanici sponsa" (Medullina, daughter of Camillus, betrothed of Tiberius Claudius Nero Germanicus).
2 CONT
2 CONT In The Twelve Caesars, Suetonius states that Medullina unexpectedly fell ill, and died on the day of her wedding to [https://www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-Emperor/6000000002119140027 '''Claudius'''], possibly in AD 9 or 10.
2 CONT
2 CONT Medullina's brother [https://www.geni.com/people/Lucius-Arruntius-Camillus-Scribonianus/6000000194120493829 '''Scribonianus'''] was the instigator of the first major rebellion against [https://www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-
2 CONC Emperor/6000000002119140027 '''Claudius'''], while he was governor of [https://www.geni.com/projects/Dalmacija-Dalmatia/40204 Dalmatia] in AD 42. 
2 CONT
