2 CONT Caesar's mother, known for her beauty, common sense, and hard work.
2 CONT
2 CONT Senator and debtor, general and seducer, orator and would-be worldconqueror, Julius Caesar, is both a force of nature and something of amomma's boy. He worships his sophisticated mother Aurelia, "a fount ofexperience and a mine of
2 CONC  common sense," while dismissing as "notimportant" his "expensive, idle, and monumentally silly" second wife,Pompeia. Aurelia is "an excellent and learned lady." According to Durant,"she was a matron of dignity and wisdom, frugall
2 CONC y managing her small homein the unfashionable Subura, a district of shops, taverns, and brothels."Caesar consults his shrewd mother about strategy and depends on her tomanage his household.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caesar, though tenderly loving and protective toward his daughter, Julia,pledges her as a child to the adolescent Brutus, with whose mother-thecruel, scheming Servilia--the future dictator of Rome has a purely sexualaffair. Years 
2 CONC later, Caesar cancels the betrothal in order to use hisblossoming daughter as bait to forge a political alliance with thecommander of the Roman legions. Meanwhile, Cicero, Caesar's main rival,is an incurable vacillator and social 
2 CONC climber who displays scantgratitude toward his "sour" and "ugly" wife, Terentia, despite herfoiling a conspiracy against his life.
