1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Marcus Livius Drusus (consul)==
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Livius_Drusus_(consul)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Livius Drusus (155–108 BC) was a Roman politician who served as consul in 112 BC.[2] He was also governor of Macedonia and campaigned successfully in Thrace against the Scordisci.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Drusus was a son of Gaius Livius Drusus.[3] He had a brother named Gaius Livius Drusus[3] and a sister named Livia.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Drusus was set up as tribune of the plebs by the Senate in 122 BC to undermine Gaius Gracchus' land reform bills. To do this (according to the record of Appian), he proposed creating twelve colonies with 3,000 settlers each from t
2 CONC he poorer classes, and relieving rent on property distributed since 133 BC. He also said the Latin allies should not be mistreated by Roman generals, which was the counteroffer to Gaius' offer of full citizenship. These were known
2 CONC  as the Leges Liviae, but they were never enacted, because the Senate simply wanted to draw support away from Gracchus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their plan was successful, and Drusus had just enough support to veto Gaius' bill. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Drusus was later consul in 112 BC and fought in Macedonia defeating the Scordisci,[2] even pushing them out of Thrace across the Danube. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 109 BC he was elected censor along with the elder Marcus Aemilius Scaurus.[5] He died in office.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Drusus was married to a Cornelia, they had three known children: 
2 CONT * Marcus Livius Drusus, the famous tribune of 91 BC, whose murder incited the Italian Social War.[6]
2 CONT * Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus, the consul of 77 BC (adopted into the Aemilii Lepidi), who married the daughter of Sulla the dictator.[6]
2 CONT * Livia, the mother of Servilia and Cato the Younger.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==''The Magistrates of the Roman Republic'' vol.1==
2 CONT https://www.academia.edu/42882560/the_magistrates_of_the_roman_republic_vol_1
