2 CONT =Quintus Mucius Scaevola Augur=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Mucius Scaevola Augur (c. 159-88 BC) was a politician of the Roman Republic and an early authority on Roman law. He was first educated in law by his father (whose name he shared) and in philosophy by the stoic Panaetius of
2 CONC  Rhodes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scaevola was made tribune in 128 BC, aedile in 125 BC, and praetor in 121 BC, in which capacity he acted as governor of Asia. Upon his return to Rome the following year he faced a charge of extortion brought by Titus Albucius (pro
2 CONC bably on personal grounds) which he successfully defended. In 117 BC, he was elected consul. In his old age, Scaevola vigorously maintained his interest in the law and in the affairs of Rome. He also passed on his knowledge of law
2 CONC  to some of Rome's most celebrated orators, as the teacher of Cicero and Atticus. In 88 BC, he defended Gaius Marius against Sulla's motion to have him named an enemy of the people, saying that he would never agree to have this do
2 CONC ne to a man who had saved Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cicero used the persona of his old master as an interlocutor in three works, his De Oratore, De amicitia, and De republica. This usage places Scaevola as a member of the Scipionic Circle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scaevola married Laelia, a daughter of Gaius Laelius, a close friend of Scipio Aemilianus Africanus, and had a son and two daughters. One of his daughters, Mucia, married the orator Lucius Licinius Crassus. His wife, daughter, and
2 CONC  granddaughters were all famed for the purity of their Latin.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first cousins included the consuls and Pontifices maximi Publius Licinius Crassus Dives Mucianus and Publius Mucius Scaevola. The former was father of Licinia, wife of the ill-fated tribune Gaius Gracchus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
