2 TITL Ancient Lordship of Gower
1 NOTE Rodri, a prince of South Wales while his father Merfyn, and his mother, 
2 CONT Essyllht, both descendants of Llud, ruled over all Wales.  At their death, 
2 CONT he was ruler of three divisions of the Welsh; North Wales, South Wales, 
2 CONT and Powys
2 CONT Land.  He was king from about 850 to 877.  He was killed after 33 years of 
2 CONT constant warfare, while leading the Welsh forces in a great battle against 
2 CONT the English.
1 NOTE OR "RHODRI MAWR""RHODRI THE GREAT""RODERICK"; KING OF WALES 844-878; FIRST TO COMBINE ALMOST ALL OF WALES; HAD 3 OTHER SONS BESIDES THOSE SHOWN; KILLED IN BATTLE.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=:1538846&id=I20112
1 NOTE His name also means Rodrick "the Great" son fo Merfyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri Mawr united the three kingdoms fo Wales.  He became King of all three (having inherited North Wales from his father, Powys from his mother, and South Wales from his wife).  He was slain in battle, after marrying Ankaret (an
2 CONC other spelling for Angharad), Queen of Sourth Wales, 13th in descent from Cunnedda (#1 of his line).  The kingdoms he united were athis death divided amoung three of his sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ap is the same as ap.  Rhondri is another spelling of Rhodri.
1 NOTE 33rd great grandfather
1 NOTE Rhodri Ap Merfyn
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=d90f0e78-0690-4933-b598-4a2a51e19e0c&tid=10145763&pid=-690277935
1 NOTE Rhodri Ap Merfyn
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=d90f0e78-0690-4933-b598-4a2a51e19e0c&tid=10145763&pid=-690277935
1 NOTE Rhodri Mawr, king of Gwynedd, died in 878. Although he was animportant Welsh king, it is not really appropriate to call him the"first King of Wales", for there were large parts of Wales over whichhe did not rule. Most sources give
2 CONC  the name of Rhodri's wife asAngharad, heiress of Ceredigion, and give his mother as Nest, heiressof Powys, but Patrick Sims-Williams [in the article "Historical Needand Literary Narrative: a Caveat from Ninth-Century Wales", Wels
2 CONC hHistory Review, vol. 17 (1994), pp. 1-40] has recently argued(conclusively, in my opinion) that neither Nest nor Angharad everexisted, and that they were inventions of later genealogists whowanted to give Rhodri's family a line o
2 CONC f descent from the earlierkings of Powys and Ceredigion.
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri Mawr, king of Gwynedd, died in 878. Although he was an importantWelsh king, it is not really appropriate to call him the "first King ofWales", for there were large parts of Wales over which he did not rule.Most sources give
2 CONC  the name of Rhodri's wife as Angharad, heiress ofCeredigion, and give his mother as Nest, heiress of Powys, but PatrickSims-Williams [in the article "Historical Need and Literary Narrative: aCaveat from Ninth-Century Wales", Wels
2 CONC h History Review, vol. 17 (1994),pp. 1-40] has recently argued (conclusively, in my opinion) that neitherNest nor Angharad ever existed, and that they were inventions of latergenealogists who wanted to give Rhodri's family a line 
2 CONC of descent fromthe earlier kings of Powys and Ceredigion.
1 NOTE OR "RHODRI MAWR""RHODRI THE GREAT""RODERICK"; KING OF WALES 844-878; FIRST TO
2 CONT COMBINE ALMOST ALL OF WALES; HAD 3 OTHER SONS BESIDES THOSE SHOWN; KILLED IN
2 CONT BATTLE
1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT REFN: 6357
2 CONT Rhodri Mawr, (Rhodri the Great, 820-78) is remembered as the first to
2 CONT claim the title of king of the Welsh. He was the first to unite most of
2 CONT Wales under his rule, 844-78. Professor Davies points out that the title
2 CONT "gre at" was bestowed upon only two other rulers in the same century:
2 CONT Charlemagne (Charles the Great) and Alfred the Great. All three
2 CONT contributed greatly to th e growth of statehood among their respective
2 CONT nations. Rhodri, son of Merfyn F rych, became King of Gwynedd in 844,
2 CONT following the death of his father; of Po wys following the death of his
2 CONT uncle in 855; and of Seisyllwg (including Cere digion and Ystrad Tywi)
2 CONT following the death of his brother-in-law in 872. He was most successful
2 CONT in keeping out the Danes and the English from settling hi s territories.
2 CONT By uniting the three principal kingdoms of Wales under his rule,Rhodri
2 CONT showed that an independent Wales could exist that need not be subser vient
2 CONT to the rule of English monarchs. Gwynfor Evans cites Nora Chadwick,
2 CONT c alling Rhodri "the greatest of all the kings of Wales." His success was
2 CONT mainl y due to his creation of a consciousness in the Welsh-speaking
2 CONT people of Brit ain that they could act together as one. Gwynfor Evans also
2 CONT laments the fact that this great king had no biographer to properly
2 CONT record his achievements, u nlike Alfred of Wessex, who had a biographer
2 CONT Asser, a Welshman from St. David 's. Rhodri was killed in 878 fighting
2 CONT against the English of Mercia.Rhodri Mawr, king of Gwynedd, died in 878.Although he was an important Welsh king, it is not really appropriate tocall him the "first King of Wales", for there were large parts of Walesover which he d
2 CONC id not rule. Most sources give the name of Rhodri's wifeas Angharad, heiress of Ceredigion, and give his mother as Nest, heiressof Powys, but Patrick Sims-Williams [in the article "Historical Need andLiterary Narrative: a Caveat f
2 CONC rom Ninth-Century Wales", Welsh HistoryReview, vol. 17 (1994), pp. 1-40] has recently argued (conclusively, inmy opinion) that neither Nest nor Angharad ever existed, and that theywere inventions of later genealogists who wanted t
2 CONC o give Rhodri's familya line of descent from the earlier kings of Powys and Ceredigion.[CusterFebruary 1, 2002 Family Tree.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT [merge G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri Mawr, king of Gwynedd, died in 878. Although he was an importantWelsh king, it is not really appropriate to call him the "first King ofWales", for there were large parts of Wales over which he did not rule.Most sources give
2 CONC  the name of Rhodri's wife as Angharad, heiress ofCeredigion, and give his mother as Nest, heiress of Powys, but PatrickSims-Williams [in the article "Historical Need and Literary Narrative: aCaveat from Ninth-Century Wales", Wels
2 CONC h History Review, vol. 17 (1994),pp. 1-40] has recently argued (conclusively, in my opinion) that neitherNest nor Angharad ever existed, and that they were inventions of latergenealogists who wanted to give Rhodri's family a line 
2 CONC of descent fromthe earlier kings of Powys and Ceredigion.
1 NOTE 1  DEAT 2  PLAC Anglesey 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Young; Kraentzler 1747a, 1747b;  History of Morgan Family.   Young: Rhodri Mawr (the Great), died 878, king of Deheubarth.
2 CONT    History:   King of Gwynedd and Powys 844. "Rhodri-Mawr (Roderick the Great) led the fight against the Viking invaders and killed the Norse leader Horm in battle. He established a line of Welsh Kings that reigned until the year 
2 CONC 1305, when King Edward I executed Dafydd and imprisoned his sons.   "Rhodri-Mawr inherited the Kingdom of Powys from an uncle, the Kingdom of North Wales from his mother and the Kingdom of South Wales from his wife. He was killed 
2 CONC in battle with the Saxons. Married Angaharad, heiress of South Wales. His six sons split the Kingdom, with Mervyn-Mawr becoming King of Powys and his brother Cadell taking the title of King of South Wales (Seisyllwg)."
2 CONT    K: Rhodi Mawr, King of Wales and Prince of Wales.   Duran calls him Rhodri/Roderic Mawr, The Great, King of Wales 843, d. 876.                           ***** Rhodri Mawr ("the Great") was the first to combine almost all Wales 
2 CONC into a single principality.  He had four sons in addition to those named in the Family Group Record.
2 CONT Rhodri Mawr (the Great), King of Gwynedd, 844, of Powys, 853, of Deheubarth, 872, m. Angharad, dau of Meurig ap Dyfynwallon, who on death of her brother, Gwgan ap Meurig, king of Seisyllwg, inherited Ceredigion and Ystrad Tywi (Ca
2 CONC rdigan and Carmarthen area) which formed his kingdom, where Gwgon, too, was last of Cunedda's line.  Rhodri Mawr, named several times in the "Annals of Ulster" whose court was still in touch with the continent, built Dynevor (the 
2 CONC Great Palace), traditionally for his heir who inherited Deheubarth (the South Land), fought the Danes, and d. in battle, probably against the Saxons, 878, having had, with other issue, a son, Cadell ap Rhodri. [Source 1]
2 CONT King of Gwynedd and Powys, 844.  "Rhodri-Mawr (Roderick the Great) led the fight against the Viking invaders and killed the Norse leader Horm in battle. He established a line of Welsh Kings that reigned until the year 1305, when K
2 CONC ing Edward I executed Dafydd and imprisoned his sons.  Rhodri-Mawr inherited the Kingdom of Powys from an uncle, the Kingdom of North Wales from his mother, and the Kingdom of South Wales from his wife.  He was killed in battle wi
2 CONC th the Saxons.  Married Angharad, heiress of South Wales.  His six sons split the Kingdom, with Mervyn-Mawr becoming King of Powys and his brother Cadell taking the title of King of South Wales (Seisyllwg)". [Source 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:  1.  Burke, Sir Bernard, C.B., LL.D., Ulster King of Arms.  _Landed Gentry
2 CONT       (entry for Powell), page 577.
2 CONT   2.  Morgan, Dennis.  _A History of the Morgan Family_.
1 NOTE 1  DEAT 2  PLAC Anglesey 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT
