2 CONT =Quintus Aelius Tubero=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Aelius Tubero (b. 74 BC - fl. 11 BC) was a Roman jurist, statesman and writer. He may have been a Roman consul with Paullus Fabius Maximus in 11 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Lucius Aelius Tubero, a friend of Cicero. In 48, he fought with the Pomperians at Pharsalus.[2] In 44 BC, he made a speech against Cicero in Pro Ligario, who was defending Ligarius. Tubero failed in his convictio
2 CONC n of Ligarius and thus abandoned oratory. Thereafter, he began studying civil law under Aulus Ofilius.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Servius Sulpicius Rufus. With her, he had a daughter of his own who would become the mother of the jurist C. Cassius Longinus.[4] He was also possibly the father of Sextus Aelius Catus, the consul in 4 AD.
2 CONC  If so, his granddaughter was Aelia Paetina, future wife of Emperor Claudius in 28 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT He may have penned a series of histories encompassing the entire Roman narrative in at least 14 books. As the author of these works has been given the name of 'Aelius Tubero', it potentially could have been the work of his father,
2 CONC  Lucius Aelius Tubero.[5] This writer modeled their style after Valerius Antias and Licinius Macer. The work was later cited by Livy and Dionysius of Halicarnassus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quintus_Aelius_Tubero
