2 CONT Hywel took over the southern kingdom of Dyfed upon the death of his father-in-law (which he may have arranged) in 904. He shared lands in Ceredigon and Ystrad Tywi with his brothers after the death of their father. He united their
2 CONC  inheritance in 920, creating the kingdom of Deheubarth, and accuired Gwynedd after the death of Idwal Foel in 942.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel's greatest achievement was to create the first uniform legal system in Wales. They were called cyfraith Hywel (Hywel's laws) and it was later claimed that he took these laws to Rome on his pilgrimage in 928 and had them bles
2 CONC sed by the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Hywel's death some of his kingdom was broken away. Gwynedd and Powys returned to the line of Idwal ap Anarawd. Glamorgan continued to be subjects of its own kings. Deheubarth passed to Hywel's son, Owain.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hywel took over the southern kingdom of Dyfed upon the death of his father-in-law (which he may have arranged) in 904. He shared lands in Ceredigon and Ystrad Tywi with his brothers after the death of their father. He united their
2 CONC  inheritance in 920, creating the kingdom of Deheubarth, and accuired Gwynedd after the death of Idwal Foel in 942.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel's greatest achievement was to create the first uniform legal system in Wales. They were called cyfraith Hywel (Hywel's laws) and it was later claimed that he took these laws to Rome on his pilgrimage in 928 and had them bles
2 CONC sed by the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Hywel's death some of his kingdom was broken away. Gwynedd and Powys returned to the line of Idwal ap Anarawd. Glamorgan continued to be subjects of its own kings. Deheubarth passed to Hywel's son, Owain.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of all Wales [except Gwent & Glamorgan]. Reign: 916-950
2 CONT
2 CONT  Codified the laws of Wales 	
2 CONT  916-950 	 Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY:  (Welsh), English Hywel The Good (d. AD 950), chieftain called in the prologues to the Welsh lawbooks "king of all Wales." This epithet was indeed appropriate for Howel, particularly during the last years of his reign. 
2 CONC He became ruler of Seisyllwg (roughly the area of Dyfed and the Towy Valley) jointly with his brother Clydog after the death of their father, Cadell (c. 910), but after Clydog's death in 920 he ruled alone. Sovereignty over Dyfed 
2 CONC in southwest Wales came to him through his wife, Elen, daughter of Llywarch ap Hyfaidd (d. 904), the last king of its dynasty; he acquired Gwynedd, in northwest Wales, and probably Powis, in northeast Wales, on the death of his co
2 CONC usin Idwal Foel ap Anarawd, in 942. Howel's reign was remarkable for its peacefulness, the result of his consistent policy of subservience to England. Howel's first recorded act is his homage to Edward the Elder in 918. Thereafter
2 CONC , he often attended the English court, and his name is found as a witness to 12 charters of Athelstan and Edred between 928 and 949. Howel was the only Welsh ruler to issue his own coins. He is remembered chiefly for the codificat
2 CONC ion of Welsh law attributed to him. Although there is no contemporary record of this work, Howel was certainly responsible for a coordination of preexisting law. There are biographies by J.E. Lloyd (1928) and J.G. Edwards (1929). 
2 CONC  Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~summer/Owen.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hywel Dda, (English: Hywel the Good;, sometimes anglicized to Howell the Good) was a well-thought-of king of Deheubarth in south-west Wales, who, using his cunning, eventually came to rule Wales from Prestatyn to Pembroke. As a de
2 CONC scendant of Rhodri Mawr through his father Cadell, Hywel was a member of the Dinefwr branch of the dynasty and is also named Hywel ap Cadell. He was recorded as King of the Britons in the Annales Cambriae and the Annals of Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is remembered as one of the most responsible native Welsh rulers of all time. His name is particularly linked with the development of the Welsh laws, generally known as the Laws of Hywel Dda. The latter part of his name ('Dda' 
2 CONC or 'Good') refers to the fact that his laws were just and good. The historian Dafydd Jenkins sees in them compassion rather than punishment, plenty of common sense and a sense of respect towards women.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel Dda was certainly a well-educated man, even by modern standards, having a good knowledge of Welsh, Latin, and English.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 2008 a merger of Pembrokeshire & Derwen, Ceredigion and Mid Wales, and Carmarthenshire NHS Trusts was named the Hywel Dda NHS Trust in his honour.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Went on a Pilgrimage to Rome in 928
2 CONT
2 CONT See also Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hywel_Dda
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hywel Dda, also known as Hywel the Good (sometimes anglicized to Howell the Good), was a well-thought-of king of Deheubarth in southwest Wales, who, using his cunning, eventually came to rule Wales from Prestatyn to Pembroke. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also called Hywel ap Cadell. It is now thought that his name was actually Hywel Ddu (Howel the Black).
2 CONT
2 CONT He is remembered as one of the most responsible native Welsh rulers of all time and was known as King of the Britons. His name is particularly linked with the development of the Welsh laws, generally known as the Laws of Hywel Dda
2 CONC . The latter part of his name ('Dda' or 'Good') refers to the fact that his laws were just and good. The historian Dafydd Jenkins sees in them compassion rather than punishment, plenty of common sense and a sense of respect toward
2 CONC  women (something unheard of in other laws throughout Europe until recently)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel Dda was certainly a well-educated man, even by modern standards, having a good knowledge of Latin, English, and Welsh. Of the 42 copies of the laws written during his lifetime, 36 were written in Welsh and six in English. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hywel_Dda for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hywel Dda
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel Dda (c. 880?–950), (English Hywel the Good, sometimes anglicized to Howell the Good) was a well-thought-of king [1]of Deheubarth in south-west Wales, who, using his cunning, eventually came to rule Wales from Prestatyn to Pe
2 CONC mbroke[2]. As a descendant of Rhodri Mawr through his father Cadell, Hywel was a member of the Dinefwr branch of the dynasty and is also named Hywel ap Cadell.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is remembered as one of the most responsible native Welsh rulers of all time. His name is particularly linked with the development of the Welsh laws, generally known as the Laws of Hywel Dda. The latter part of his name ('Dda' 
2 CONC or 'Good') refers to the fact that his laws were just and good. The historian Dafydd Jenkins sees in them compassion rather than punishment, plenty of common sense and a sense of respect towards women (something unheard of in othe
2 CONC r laws throughout Europe until recently)[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel Dda was certainly a well-educated man, even by modern standards, having a good knowledge of Welsh, Latin, and English.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 2008 a merger of Pembrokeshire & Derwen, Ceredigion and Mid Wales, and Carmarthenshire NHS Trusts was named the Hywel Dda NHS Trust in his honour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel was born at around 880, the younger son of Cadell ap Rhodri, himself the son of Rhodri the Great. In 905, Cadell, having conquered Dyfed, gave it to his son to rule on his behalf. Hywel was able to consolidate his position b
2 CONC y marrying Elen, whose father Llywarch ap Hyfaidd had ruled Dyfed until his death. Following his father's death in 909, he acquired a share of Seisyllwg, and on his brother's death in 920, he merged Dyfed and Seisyllwg, creating f
2 CONC or himself a new kingdom, which became known as Deheubarth. Following the death of his cousin Idwal Foel in 942, he also seized the Kingdom of Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Accomplishments
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Peace with Wessex
2 CONT
2 CONT Hywel's reign was a violent one, and he achieved an understanding with Athelstan of England. Athelstan and Hywel ruled part of Wales jointly. Such was the relationship between the neighbouring countries that Hywel was able to mint
2 CONC  his own coinage in the English city of Chester. He was the first Welsh ruler to produce coinage for at least a thousand years, since the coinage of his Celtic predecessors. His study of legal systems and his pilgrimage to Rome in
2 CONC  928 combined to enable him to formulate advanced ideas about law. A comparative study of law and lawmaking at the time reveals a deep concern for law and its documentation throughout Europe and also the Islamic world, the Cordoba
2 CONC  Islamic Law translation schools being a fine example, from Greek to Arabic to Latin. The Hywel 'Law' book was written partly in Latin, about laws of court, law of country and the law of justices.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opinions vary as to the motives for Hywel's close association with the court of Athelstan. J.E. Lloyd claimed Hywel was an admirer of Wessex[5], while D.P. Kirby suggests that it may have been the action of a pragmatist who recogn
2 CONC ized the realities of power in mid-10th century Britain.[6] It is notable that he gave one of his sons an Anglo-Saxon name, Edwin. His policies with regard to England were evidently not to the taste of all his subjects. Athelstan 
2 CONC and Hywel had similar interests. They both developed a coinage; they both had a kingdom; both were attributed a Law book. Hywel was aware of the greater power and acceded to it.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Welsh language poem entitled Armes Prydein, considered by Sir Ifor Williams to have been written in Deheubarth during Hywel's reign, called for the Welsh to join a confederation of all the non-English peoples of Britain and Irel
2 CONC and to fight the Saxons. The poem may be linked to the alliance of Norse and Celtic kingdoms which challenged Athelstan at the Battle of Brunanburh in 937.[citation needed] No Welsh forces joined this alliance, and this may well h
2 CONC ave been because of the influence of Hywel.[citation needed] On the other hand neither did he send troops to support Athelstan.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Welsh Law
2 CONT
2 CONT The conference held at Whitland circa 945, was an assembly in which Welsh law was codified and set down in writing for posterity. According to tradition, much of the work was done by the celebrated clerk, Blegywryd. Following Hywe
2 CONC l's death, his kingdom was soon split into three. Gwynedd was reclaimed by the sons of Idwal Foel, while Deheubarth was divided between Hywel's sons. However, his legacy endured in the form of his laws, which remained in active us
2 CONC e throughout Wales until the conquest and were not abolished by the English Parliament until the 16th century. A surviving copy of a Latin text of the Law (ms Peniarth 28) is held at The National Library of Wales in Aberystwyth an
2 CONC d can be seen online.[1] More than 30 manuscripts were recently selected for a discussion of the "Law" of Hywel, by a Welsh professor of Medieval studies, Hywel Emanuel. Only five of them were considered to be of sufficient antiqu
2 CONC ity, dating back to the 13thC or earlier, to merit serious attention. Three of them were in Latin and two in Welsh.
2 CONT
