2 CONC wel Dda. [Source 1]
2 CONT "Hywel (or Howell) the Good was celebrated as the 'Justinian of Cambria' because of his codification of the laws and his institution of trial by jury with a judge who should act as 'Christ withe the twelve Apostles'.  His laws bec
2 CONC ame known as the Law of Hywel Dda (Dda meaning 'The Good').  King of Dyfed in 904, King of Seisyllwg in 909, King of Gwynedd in 942, thus uniting the Kingdom of Wales.  Made a pilgrimage to Rome in 928 and was the only Welsh ruler
2 CONC  to mint his own coinage.  He entered into an alliance with the King of Essex.  Married Elen on 904 and had sons Rhodri, Edwin, and Owen, who split the kingdom upon his death, with Owen taking the Kingdom of South Wales." [Sources
2 CONC  2 and 3]
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:  1.  Burke, Sir Bernard, C.B., LL.D, Ulster King of Arms.  _Landed Gentry_
2 CONT       (entry under Powell), page 577.
2 CONT   2.  Morgan, Dennis.  _A History of the Morgan Family_.
2 CONT   3.  John Duran.
1 NOTE 1  NAME Hywel Dda "the Good" Ap /Cadell/ 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001 1  BIRT 2  DATE ABT. 882 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Young; Llantarnam Abbey, page 191; History of Morgan Family.   King of Wales, 942. Duran says "Prince of South Wales and Powis."
2 CONT    Also known as Hywell Dha. Dha mean good?
2 CONT    Abbey: Owen ap Hywell Dda, ob. 987, Prince of Gwent.   Young: Hywel Dda (the Good), died 949, prionce of Dyfed, Seisyllwg andd Gwynedd, legendary lawgiver of Wales.                         ******* Hywel Dda had other children i
2 CONC n addition to Owain ap Hywel Dda.
2 CONT Hywel Dda, (the Good), 'King of all Wales", king of Gwynedd, 915, did homage to Edwin the Elder, son of Alfred the Great, 918, journied to Hereford, circa 926, to acknowledge overlordship of Aethelstan, in whose reign he attested 
2 CONC many charters, 931-937, made pilgrimage to Rome, 928, minted his own silver pennies, codified the laws of Wales, circa 945, m. Elen, dau of Llywarch ap Hyfaidd, King of Dyfed (Pembs.), and d. ca. 950, having had a son, Owain ap Hy
2 CONC wel Dda. [Source 1]
2 CONT "Hywel (or Howell) the Good was celebrated as the 'Justinian of Cambria' because of his codification of the laws and his institution of trial by jury with a judge who should act as 'Christ withe the twelve Apostles'.  His laws bec
2 CONC ame known as the Law of Hywel Dda (Dda meaning 'The Good').  King of Dyfed in 904, King of Seisyllwg in 909, King of Gwynedd in 942, thus uniting the Kingdom of Wales.  Made a pilgrimage to Rome in 928 and was the only Welsh ruler
2 CONC  to mint his own coinage.  He entered into an alliance with the King of Essex.  Married Elen on 904 and had sons Rhodri, Edwin, and Owen, who split the kingdom upon his death, with Owen taking the Kingdom of South Wales." [Sources
2 CONC  2 and 3]
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:  1.  Burke, Sir Bernard, C.B., LL.D, Ulster King of Arms.  _Landed Gentry_
2 CONT       (entry under Powell), page 577.
2 CONT   2.  Morgan, Dennis.  _A History of the Morgan Family_.
2 CONT   3.  John Duran.
1 NOTE Howell the Good was at first the only prince of South Wales, sharing the 
2 CONT position with his brother, Clydog, but he gradually, and forcibly became 
2 CONT the famous one.  He was finally declared by nearly all the Welsh Kings.  
2 CONT Family record says:  Howell DDA succeeded to his father's government.  
2 CONT Upon the death of his cousin (Son of Anarwd) he acquired that of North 
2 CONT Wales also.  Soon he was called King of all the Welsh.
1 NOTE REF: MFHOWELLJR@aol.com (HOWELL-L@rootsweb.com, Howell readers group):Present Welsh, English and American Howells derive from the old Welshfamily of Abernent in Carmarthenshire, the linage and name deriving fromHywel Dda the King 
2 CONC of Wales from 943 to 950. He was grandson of RhodriMawr, the Great, who united almost all of ancient Wales under hissupremacy. He (Hywel Dda I presume) was married to Ellen, daughter of thelast of the Kings of Dyfed. The reign of 
2 CONC Howel Dda was remarkable for itspeacefulness, and is the only ruler of ancient Wales designated "TheGood". He was also the only King ever to issue his own coinage. On avisit to Rome in 928, became a student of jurisprudence, and t
2 CONC he code oflaws he composed is his lasting claim to fame. Three parts of his codeare still found in English Common Law. He was such a wise and just manthat he was called "the head and glory of all the Britons." There is aplace toda
2 CONC y in Wales called Crickhowell, which is considered to be eitherhis home and/or pob.[Custer February 1, 2002 Family Tree.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT [merge G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: MFHOWELLJR@aol.com (HOWELL-L@rootsweb.com, Howell readers group):Present Welsh, English and American Howells derive from the old Welshfamily of Abernent in Carmarthenshire, the linage and name deriving fromHywel Dda the King 
2 CONC of Wales from 943 to 950. He was grandson of RhodriMawr, the Great, who united almost all of ancient Wales under hissupremacy. He (Hywel Dda I presume) was married to Ellen, daughter of thelast of the Kings of Dyfed. The reign of 
2 CONC Howel Dda was remarkable for itspeacefulness, and is the only ruler of ancient Wales designated "TheGood". He was also the only King ever to issue his own coinage. On avisit to Rome in 928, became a student of jurisprudence, and t
2 CONC he code oflaws he composed is his lasting claim to fame. Three parts of his codeare still found in English Common Law. He was such a wise and just manthat he was called "the head and glory of all the Britons." There is aplace toda
2 CONC y in Wales called Crickhowell, which is considered to be eitherhis home and/or pob.
