1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Saint Paula (347–404) was an ancient Roman saint and early Desert Mother. A member of one of the richest "senatorial" families which frivolously claimed descent from Agamemnon,[2] Paula was the daughter of Blesilla
2 CONC , from the great clan of the Furii Camilli.[3] At the age of 15, Paula was married to the nobleman Toxotius, with whom she had four daughters, Blaesilla, Paulina, Eustochium, and Rufina. She also had a boy, also named Toxotius. We
2 CONC  learn of Paula's early life through the writings of Saint Jerome. In his Letter 108, he states that she had led a luxurious life and held a great status. She dressed in silks, and had been carried about her city by her eunuch sla
2 CONC ves. At the age of 32, Paula was widowed. She continued to dedicate herself to her family, but became more interested in religion as time went on.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through the influence of St. Marcella and her group, Paula became an enthusiastic member of this semi-monastic group of women. In 382, she met Saint Jerome, who had come to Rome with St. Epiphanius and Bishop Paulinus of Antioch. 
2 CONC Born in Dalmatia, Jerome had studied in Rome as a youth and had traveled to Germany and Aquileia, and for some years had lived in the East as an ascetic and scholar. [edit] Paula's family
2 CONT
2 CONT Paula married her daughter, Paulina (d. 395), to the senator Saint Pammachius; Blesilla soon became a widow and died in 384. Of her two other daughters, Rufina died in 386, and Eustochium accompanied her mother to the Orient where
2 CONC  she died in 419. Her son, Toxotius, at first not a Christian, but baptized in 385, married in 389 Laeta, daughter of the pagan priest Albinus. Of this marriage was born Paula the Younger, who in 404 rejoined Eustochium in the Hol
2 CONC y Land and in 420 closed the eyes of St. Jerome. These are the names which recur frequently in the letters of St. Jerome, where they are inseparable from that of Paula. It has been argued that Saint Eustochius of Tours was the bro
2 CONC ther of Paula the Younger and the son of Toxotius.[3] [edit] Relationship with Jerome
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerome's enemies found that his denunciations of clerical indulgence and advocacy of self-denial were odd when they considered his close relationship with Paula.[4] An amorous relationship between Jerome and Paula was suggested as
2 CONC  having occurred.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT An anecdote told of Jerome, of twelfth century origin, tells that Roman clergy hostile to Jerome planned to have him expelled from the city by planting a woman's robe next to his bed. When Jerome awoke in the middle of the night t
2 CONC o attend the service of matins, he absentmindedly put on the female robes. He was thus accused of having had a woman in his bed. This story acknowledges, while at the same time discrediting as a malicious slander, Jerome's relatio
2 CONC nship with women, such the kind he is presumed to have had with Paula.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Chaucer played upon the relationship between Jerome and Paula when he writes the Wife of Bath's Prologue. Chaucer has the Wife visit the same pilgrimage sites as did Paula, and has her constantly cite, not classical authors, but J
2 CONC erome.[2] Many of her comments are counter-arguments to those put forth by St. Jerome, mainly in his work Against Jovinianus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Palladius, a contemporary of Jerome, believed that Paula was hindered by Jerome: "For though she was able to surpass all, having great abilities, he hindered her by his jealousy, having induced her to serve his own plan http://en.
2 CONC wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paula
