1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Arruns Tarquinius=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Arruns Tarquinius was the brother of Lucius Tarquinius Superbus, the seventh and last King of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to most ancient authors, Arruns and his brother were the sons of Lucius Tarquinius Priscus, the fifth Roman king, and Tanaquil. However, in some sources they described as grandsons; their father may have been a certain G
2 CONC naeus Tarquinius, who according to an Etruscan tradition was defeated and killed by the heroes Aulus and Caelius Vibenna, together with a certain Macstarna. Apparently the Etruscan equivalent of the Latin word magister, Macstarna 
2 CONC has been identified with Servius Tullius, the sixth King of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, Servius had come to the palace as a child, following the capture of Corniculum by Tarquinius Priscus. Tanaquil, who was skilled in prophecy, discovered his potential for greatness by various omens. When the el
2 CONC der Tarquin was assassinated, Tanaquil gave out that he was merely wounded, and installed Servius as regent, preferring him to her own sons. The Etruscan tradition may preserve an account of a revolt by Tarquin's sons against Serv
2 CONC ius' magistracy. Servius married a daughter of the elder Tarquin, and in turn he gave his own daughters to Arruns and Lucius Tarquinius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arruns Tarquinius was mild and unassuming, while his brother was ambitious. Ironically, Arruns' wife, known to history as Tullia Minor, as she was the younger daughter, was similarly ambitious, while her elder sister was not. Tull
2 CONC ia sought to place her husband on the throne, which would require her father's death. But as Arruns lacked the ambition to overthrow his father-in-law, Tullia contrived his murder, and that of her own sister, that she might marry 
2 CONC Lucius. Once these deeds were accomplished, she and Lucius plotted her father's destruction.
2 CONT
2 CONT With a sudden and bloody palace coup, the pair deposed and murdered the king, and Lucius seized the throne. Ironically, Lucius' second son was named after his murdered brother; but it was the overweening pride and arrogance of Luc
2 CONC ius' sons that brought about the downfall of the Roman monarchy. The younger Arruns fell in battle against the consul Lucius Junius Brutus in 509 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arruns_Tarquinius_(brother_of_Tarquin_the_Proud)
