2 CONT Llywelyn ap Seisyll (died 1023) was a King of Gwynedd and of Deheubarth in north-west and south-west Wales, also called King of the Britons by the Annals of Ulster. Also called Llywelyn ap Seisyllt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about Llywelyn's father Seisyll, who may not have been of Royal blood. According to some genealogies Siesyll and his son Llywelyn were associated with Rhuddlan, perhaps originally as lords of the Rhuddlan commote i
2 CONC n Rhos.[1] Llywelyn's wife Angharad was the daughter of Maredydd ab Owain, who ruled much of both northern and southern Wales for a period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military successes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn won control of Gwynedd in 1018 when he defeated Aeddan ap Blegywryd in battle, killing him and his four sons. He later gained control of Deheubarth, defeating Rhain, an Irish pretender who claimed to be the son of Maredud
2 CONC d ab Owain, at Abergwili in 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the annals in Brut y Tywysogion, Llywelyn's reign was a period of prosperity, "there was no one needy in his realm, and there was no town empty or deserted".
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession:
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, though still a youth when his father died, was later able to gain control of almost the whole of Wales.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llywelyn_ap_Seisyll
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Seisyll, King of Gwynedd and Deheubarth, married Angharad verch Maredudd o Deheubarth, daughter of Brenin Deheubarth Maredudd ab Owain o Deheubarth in 994, his third cousin once removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn was King of Gwynedd in Wales between 1005 and 1023. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur (1018): "In this year Llywelyn ap Seisyll slew Aeddan ap Blegywryd and his four sons."
2 CONT
