1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The name of the wife of the c. 1030 Sir William of Malpas is not known.  They had three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT * a.  William II, born c. 1060, married Tanglust born c. 1075, a base daughter of Hugh the Fat, Earl of Chester.  His children included David c 1090, who married his 1st cousin, Margaret, daughter of Ralph.
2 CONT * b.  Ralph of Malpas, father of Margaret.  She married her 1st cousin, David b 1090, son of William ll.
2 CONT * c.  David, father of Einion
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://cybergata.com/roots/7454.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of William le Belward's wife is given as both Letitia and Mabilla in difference sources. This source refers to her as Mabilla. Collins's Peerage of England as well as The Baronetage of England, refers to her as Letititia,
2 CONC  both using Sir William Dugdale and Mr. Erdeswicke as sources. She is called Mabel in George Ormerod's The History of County Palatine and City of Chester, Vol II, pg. 628
2 CONT
2 CONT >Robert Fitz-Hugh, whose name appears as a witness to the foundation charter of St. Werburgh's Abbey at Chester in 1093, had two daughters, Letitia and Mabilla*, who in course of time, became his heirs, and the latter of whom afte
2 CONC rwards married William le Belward, of Malpas, son of John le Belward, who was living in the time of William Rufus, and is believed to have been one of the five knights mentioned in the Domesday as holding their lands of the Norman
2 CONC  baron. To this, the Lady Mabilla* conveyed her moiety of the Malpas barony, and from this marriage sprang the house of Egerton.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~County Families of Lancashire & Cheshire, pg. 119
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===comments===
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolcott suggests that Mabella / Letitia belongs in a later generation:
2 CONT
2 CONT From  [http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id152.html THE "MALPAS" FAMILY IN CHESHIRE] By Darrell Wolcott
2 CONT
2 CONT [8] Refer to the following Appendix for the citations from which we extracted the marriage of William II to a base daughter of Earl Hugh the Fat; the marriage of '''William  III to Mabel, daughter of Robert fitz Hugh''' is cited i
2 CONC n Ormerod's History of Cheshire, vol 2, p. 628
