1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see Darrell Wolcott: The "Malpas" Family in Cheshire; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id152.html. (Steven Ferry, April 19, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT there were 3 sons of the c. 1030 Sir William of Malpas: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * b.  Ralph of Malpas, father of Margaret.  She married her 1st cousin, David b 1090, son of William ll.
2 CONT
2 CONT With this marriage, the line of Ralph became extinct and his one-third of Malpas passed to his son-in-law and nephew, David, and thence to his daughter's son, the earliest of the "William ap David ap William" men in the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id152.html THE "MALPAS" FAMILY IN CHESHIRE] By Darrell Wolcott
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/sitebuildercontent/sitebuilderpictures/.pond/malpaspedigreex.jpg.w560h549.jpg 1.  Visitation of Cheshire, 1580]
2 CONT
2 CONT  c.  The 1580 Visitation of Cheshire claims that Ralph, Baron of Malpas, married Beatrix sister of Hugh Cyfeiliog.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ralph of Malpas (2) is a composite man.  When combined with David (4) and Margaret (5) and William (6), he was born c. 1065, '''a son of Sir William'''.  But when combined with Beatrix (3), David (8) and Constance (9), he was 
2 CONC born c. 1125, the son of Einion ap David ap Sir William.
2 CONT   Hugh Cyfeiliog, 5th Earl of Chester, was born about 1147.  His sister, Beatrix, could have been born c. 1135/40 and married '''Ralph ap Einion''' of c. 1125. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=Nzw6AQAAMAAJ&dq=arms+argent+a+cross+patonce+azure&source=gbs_navlinks_s Some Feudal Coats of Arms and Others:] Illustrated with 2,000 Zinco Etchings from the Bayear Tapestry, Greek Vases, Seals
2 CONC , Tiles, Effigies, Brasses and Heralcic Rolls. Some Chart Pedigrees Joseph Foster J. Parker & Company, 1902 - Heraldry - 240 pages
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=38psAAAAMAAJ&dq=arms%20argent%20a%20cross%20patonce%20azure%20malpas&pg=PA157#v=onepage&q=arms%20argent%20a%20cross%20patonce%20azure%20malpas&f=true page 157] of A Treatise on Heraldry, Britis
2 CONC h and Foreign: With English and French Glossaries, Volume 1 by John Woodward, George Burnett W. & A. K. Johnston, 1892 - Devices (Heraldry). - 858 pages.  Argent, a cross patonce gules, is the coat of Colville, and Carlyle. With t
2 CONC he charge sable it is borne by Baxeshire, or Banester; and azure, by the Barons of Malpas.  
2 CONT * [http://cheshire-heraldry.org.uk/visitations/malpass.jpg Radulphus Boro' de Malpass] The Armorial Bearings  Exemplified in  The Cheshire Visitations. illustrated in colour by Martin S. J. Goldstraw
