2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FIRST FIFTEEN GENERATIONS! Cox’s pedigree commences with two men of noble birth, both named Cynwric, whose great-grandchildren were to marry and consolidate the line. One Cynwric was a Briton (with a lion on an ermine field as
2 CONC  his arms) and one English (a sword on a vert or azure field). The lion arms make it clear that the first Cynwric is meant to be identified with Cynwrig ap Rhiwallon ap Dingad ap Tudor Trevor, who was killed during a Danish invasi
2 CONC on in 1073 or 1074 and was buried at Wrexham: he is remembered in the township of Christionydd Cynwrig, which is named after him. Cynwric ap Rhiwallon was succeeded by a son and the English Cynwric by a daughter: neither was named
2 CONC  by Cox. In the third generation ap Rhiwallon was succeeded by his grandson, another Cynwric; and the English Cynwric by his daughter’s heir Kenric. Their children Edward Kenwric (born in Kent) and Frideswid married and the heir i
2 CONC s given as Redulph Kenwric, who married Agnes, daughter of Richard Fitsroberts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some Notes on the Kenrick Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The following genealogical notes were compiled by John Kenrick Davies in a letter to his cousin, Noel Georgina Knight-Adkin, in 1939. In Saxon and Norman times the Kenricks were Welsh princes ruling over Denbighshire, Flintshire a
2 CONC nd parts of Cheshire. They did not acknowledge the sovereignty of the Normans and Plantagenets until Edward I conquered North Wales and compelled Llewellyn to acknowledge him as his suzerain. Amongst the Harleian MSS.¹ in the Brit
2 CONC ish Museum is a genealogical tree tracing the Kenricks back to 200 years before the Norman conquest.
