2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3P-S.htm#_Toc21501818
2 CONT
2 CONT HUGH de Say [I], son of HUGH FitzOsbern & his wife Eustachia de Say (-1190).  “Osbertus filius Hugonis” donated property to Godstow nunnery in Oxfordshire, at the request of “domini Walteri de Clifford” for the souls of "uxoris su
2 CONC æ Margaretæ et…Rosamundæ filiæ suæ", with the consent of "Hugonis fratris mei", by undated charter[502].  "Osbertus filius Hugonis" confirmed the settlement of his dispute with the monks of Worcester Cathedral priory concerning th
2 CONC e boundaries of their woods at Cornwood by charter dated to [1170], witnessed by "Hugone de Sai fratre meo…"[503].  "Osbern fitz Hugh" donated a virgate at Richard’s Castle to Haughmond Abbey by charter dated to [1172/85], witness
2 CONC ed by "Walter de Clifford, Walter his son, Hugh de Say and Lucie his wife"[504].  “Walterus de Clifford” donated property to Godstow nunnery in Oxfordshire, for the souls of "uxoris meæ Margaretæ de Clifford et filiæ nostræ Rosamu
2 CONC ndæ", by undated charter witnessed by "Osbertus filius Hugonis, Hugo de Sey…"[505].  "Osbern de Say and…his brother Hugh" confirmed a charter of Baldwin Bishop of Worcester, dated to after Aug 1180[506].  m as her first husband, L
2 CONC UCY, daughter of WALTER Clifford & his wife Margaret ---.  "Hugh de Say and Lucia his wife, daughter of Walter de Clifford, son of Richard fitz Poncius"
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT *1.         HUGH de Say [II] ([1165/70]-killed in battle ([1196]).  "Lucia de Say daughter of Walter de Clifford" donated land in Rocheforde to Haughmond Abbey, with the consent of "her son Hugh de Say", by undated charter, witnes
2 CONC sed by "Hugh de Say, Walter de Clifford"[511].  The Red Book of the Exchequer, listing scutage payments in [1194/95], records "Hugo de Say" paying "xxiii l, xxiii milites" in Herefordshire, and granted delay to pay in Berkshire[51
2 CONC 2].  m [as her first husband,] MABEL Marmion, daughter of --- (-before 3 Dec 1215).  The primary sources which confirm her parentage and [first] marriage have not been identified.  The chronology of her [first] husband’s family su
2 CONC ggests that Mabel could have belonged to the same generation as Robert Marmion [V].  [She married secondly Roger de Amundeville. Hugh & his wife had two children (the possibility of a third child is discussed above under Hugh’s wi
2 CONC fe): 
2 CONT **a)         LUCY de Say (-[20 Oct/8 Nov] 1204?).  Her existence is confirmed by the following document.  King John instructed the sheriff of Somerset to grant “saisina manerii de Gifle...que fuit Luce de Say aine fil Hug de Say q
2 CONC  est in custodia sua” to “Willo de Cantilupo”, dayed 20 Oct 1204[515].  As her younger sister Margery was already married at that date, maybe Lucy was handicapped in some way.  The order dated 8 Nov 1204 cited below under her pate
2 CONC rnal uncle Gilbert granted “custodiam manerii de Gifle” to “Gilb de Say”.  One possibility is that Lucy had died before that date. 
2 CONT ** b)         MARGERY de Say ([1190/96]-1230).  Her family origin and first marriage are confirmed by the 5 Jul 1219 document cited below.  … m firstly (1197 or after) HUGH de Ferrers of Lechlade, son of WALKELIN de Ferrers & his 
2 CONC wife --- (-before [15/21] Aug 1204).  m secondly ([6 May 1209/Jun 1211]) ROBERT de Mortimer, son of ROGER [III] de Mortimer & his [second] wife Isabel de Ferrers (-before 5 Jul 1219).  m thirdly (Royal licence 23 Nov 1219) WILLIAM
2 CONC  de Stuteville, son of WILLIAM de Stuteville & his wife --- (-before 20 May 1259). 
2 CONT * 2.         RICHARD de Say of Rochford (-after 1200).  "Richard de Say" donated property at Givele to Montacute priory, to keep the anniversary of "the lady Lucy the grantor’s mother", by undated charter which names "his brother 
2 CONC Sir Gilbert"[527]. 
2 CONT *3.         GILBERT de Say (-after 1210).  "Richard de Say" donated property at Givele to Montacute priory, to keep the anniversary of "the lady Lucy the grantor’s mother", by undated charter which names "his brother Sir Gilbert"[
2 CONC 528].  King John notified the sheriff of Somerset that he had granted “custodiam manerii de Gifle” to “Gilb de Say”, dated 8 Nov 1204[529].  The Red Book of the Exchequer records "Gilbertus de Say" holding one knight’s fee "in Giv
2 CONC ele" in Dorset, Somerset in [1210/12][530].  m MATILDA de Clivedon, daughter of MATTHEW de Clivedon & his wife --- de Montagu (-after 1223).  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by a claim dated 1223, noted by Bracton, by "Wi
2 CONC llelmus filius Drogonis de Monte Acuto…infra etatem" against "Matheum de Cliuedona" for "ecclesiam de Suttona…advocacionem", the defendant claiming that "Willelmus de Monte Acuto avus predicti Willelmi" gave the advocacy to "Gilbe
2 CONC rto de Say in…maritagium cum Matillide nepte sua filia eiusdem Mathei"[531]. 
2 CONT
