2 CONT Maurice FitzGerald, Lord of Lanstephan
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice FitzGerald, Lord of Lanstephan[1] (c1100 - 1 September 1176) was a major figure in the Norman conquest of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Gerald de Windsor, Constable of Pembroke Castle. His mother was Nest, a Welsh princess and former mistress of Henry I. He fought under Robert FitzMartin at the Battle of Crug Mawr in 1136. The exiled High King of
2 CONC  Ireland, Dermot MacMurrough (Irish Diarmait Mac Murchada) sought his assistance to regain his position.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The original Earldom of Desmond was based on land holdings in Munster belonging to his descendents. His son Sir Gerald FitzMaurice (c1152 -c1203) married the daughter of Robert de Birmingham. Their son was Sir Maurice FitzGerald, 
2 CONC Justiciar of Ireland, Lord of Offaly, (1190 - 1257, died at Youghal. His son was Sir Maurice FitzMaurice Fitz Gerald, Justiciar of Ireland, Lord of Offaly, (c1239, Wexford - 1286, Ross,Ireland)
2 CONT
