2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonedd_Gw%C5%B7r_y_Gogledd
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elffin_ap_Gwyddno
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT This family traces its descent to the most remote period of British history, and through a line of ancestors whose names are conspicuous in the records of ancient Britain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Descended from a line of ancient British princes was Teon, who at the whose of his life, in the Fifth Century, became a member of the college of St. Illtyd, and was first Bishop of Caer Loew (now Gloucester) and afterwards Bishop
2 CONC  of London, from whence he was driven by the Pagan Saxons. The patrimony of Teon is presumed to have been in that part of Britain now called Shropshire, as a range of hills in that county are still called, after him, by the Welch,
2 CONC  Carneddau Teon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twelfth in descent from Teon, was Gwaethfoed, Lord of Cardigan and Guynang. Gwaethfoed was contemporary of Edgar, King of the Saxons, (d. 1057) He married Morfydd, one of the daughters and co-heirs of Ivor, King or Lord of Gwent. 
2 CONC His descendants were feudal barons of Llandaff, county Glamorgan, Wales."
2 CONT
2 CONT The English surname variously spelled, Mathes, Mathews and Matthews, is derived from "Mathew." "The baptismal name 'Mathew' introduced into Great Britain at the Conquest has not only become a surname, but the parent of many others
2 CONC , among which is Mathews. The Welsh family Mathew claim from the Prince of Cardigan in the XI Century. The name is of course identical with 'Matthew." (''Patronymica, Brittanica, Lower''.)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mathew, Hebrew; A reward, a recompense." (''What's in a Name, Nichols, London, 1857'')
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mathews was first used in London in 1618. Surnames began to be used about A.D. 1000. The practice commenced in Normandy and gradually hereditary among Anglo-Saxons before the Conquest." (''British Family Names, Barber'')
