2 CONT
2 CONT Miles de Gloucester, 1st Earl of Hereford
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Miles de Gloucester, 1st Earl of Hereford, Lord of Brecknock (1100–24 December 1143), was the son of Walter de Gloucester, who appears as sheriff of that county between 1104 and 1121.
2 CONT
2 CONT Milo or Miles succeeded his father about the latter year.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was high in the service of Henry I between 1130 and 1135, he was Constable of England and combined the hereditary office of Sheriff of Gloucester with that of local justiciar for Gloucestershire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil war allegiances
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of King Henry he declared for Stephen, at whose court he appears as constable in 1136. King Stephen granted him the honour of Gloucester and Brecknock. However, in 1139, when the empress Matilda appeared in England
2 CONC , he declared for her, and placed the city of Gloucester at her disposal; he was further distinguished by sacking the nearby royalist city of Worcester, attacking Stephen's siege works at Wallingford Castle and reducing the county
2 CONC  of Hereford. He was retained as her Constable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earldom
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1141, he was rewarded with the earldom of Hereford when Matilda ruled the country. He remained loyal to the Empress after her defeat at Winchester the same year. John of Salisbury classes him with Geoffrey de Mandeville and oth
2 CONC ers who were non tam comites regni quam hostes publici. The charge is justified by his public policy; but the materials for appraising his personal character do not exist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sybil de Neufmarche, daughter of Bernard de Neufmarche, Lord of Brecon and Nest, granddaughter of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, in 1121. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Bertha of Hereford, married William de Braose before 1150, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Roger Fitzmiles, 2nd Earl of Hereford.
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Walter de Hereford died after 1159 in the Holy Land. He was Sheriff of Gloucester in 1155-1157 and Sheriff of Hereford in 1155-1159.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Henry Fitzmiles Henry of Hereford, died 12 April 1165. He succeeded to the title of Baron Abergavenny in 1141/42.
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. William de Hereford. He died before 1160 without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Mahel de Hereford, died October 1165 at Bronllys Castle, Breconshire, Wales, mortally hurt when a stone dropped from the tower during a fire; died without issue. Buried at Llanthony Priory.
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Margaret de Gloucester, married Humphrey de Bohun, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Lucy of Gloucester, married Herbert FitzHerbert of Winchester, Lord Chamberlain, by whom she had issue. Buried at Llanthony Priory.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Impression of the Seal of Miles Fitzwalter of Gloucester, Earl of Hereford, Lord of Abergavenny & Caldicot
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Continuation of Florence of Worcester (ed. B Thorpe, 1848-1849); the Cartulary of Gloucester Abbey (Rolls series); and John Horace Round's Geoffrey de Mandeville (1892).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Miles of Gloucester, Earl of Hereford, and Constable of England, was also called Milo de Gloecestria.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received from King Henry I Sibyl, daughter of Bernard de Neufmarche, in marriage with all the lands of her father and mother after their death or before is they so willed it, whence came the lordship of Brecknock in 1121.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father as constable and sheriff of Gloucester circa 1126. He switched sides and joined King Stephen's enemies, and joined Robert of Gloucester in inviting the Empress Matilda to England in 1139. He assisted Brien 
2 CONC fitzCount in saving Brien's castle at Wallingford, for which Brien granted Miles the honour of Abergavenny in 1139/40. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was named earl of Hereford in recognition of his services on 25 July 1141 by the Empress Maud. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died of a hunting accident. He was buried in the Chapterhouse of Llanthony Priory, Gloucester, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron of Brecknock and Lord High Constable of England 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  Earl of Hereford
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Miles de Gloucester, 1st Earl of Hereford, Lord of Brecknock (1100–24 December 1143), was the son of Walter de Gloucester, who appears as sheriff of that county between 1104 and 1121.
2 CONT
2 CONT Milo or Miles succeeded his father about the latter year.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was high in the service of Henry I between 1130 and 1135, he was Constable of England and combined the hereditary office of Sheriff of Gloucester with that of local justiciar for Gloucestershire.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sybil de Neufmarche, daughter of Bernard de Neufmarche, Lord of Brecon and Nest, granddaughter of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, in 1121. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha of Hereford, married William de Braose before 1150, by whom she had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Fitzmiles, 2nd Earl of Hereford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter de Hereford died after 1159 in the Holy Land. He was Sheriff of Gloucester in 1155-1157 and Sheriff of Hereford in 1155-1159. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Fitzmiles Henry of Hereford, died 12 April 1165. He succeeded to the title of Baron Abergavenny in 1141/42. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Hereford. He died before 1160 without issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mahel de Hereford, died October 1165 at Bronllys Castle, Breconshire, Wales, mortally hurt when a stone dropped from the tower during a fire; died without issue. Buried at Llanthony Priory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret de Gloucester, married Humphrey de Bohun, by whom she had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy of Gloucester, married Herbert FitzHerbert of Winchester, Lord Chamberlain, by whom she had issue. Buried at Llanthony Priory.
