1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Detached https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-le-Scrope-of-Bridlington/6000000003086996632?through=6000000003086994335 as parent of the children below. Their father has not been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Medlands ''10 Jun 2024'' [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3P-S.htm#_ftnref575 Medlands] English Lords P–S) doesn't show Robert le Scrope as father of:'''
2 CONT >1. PHILIP Scrope....&c.
2 CONT >2. ROBERT Scrope....&c.
2 CONT >3. SIMON....&c.
2 CONT >4. HUGH....&c.
2 CONT >'''Medlands states that these are brothers are of parents unknown.'''
2 CONT >'''''The Complete Peerage'' does not state or infer a name of the father for these b brothers.''' Please review details in the Overview for https://www.geni.com/people/Simon-le-Scrope/6000000003086994910 ..
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Le Scrope was the son Hugh Le Scrope. He was born in 1134 in Bridlington, Yorkshire, England. He like his father, was granted lands to the Priory of Bridlington, in Yorkshire.
2 CONT He was married to his wife (born in 1138 in Bridlington, Yorkshire, England) in 1159 in Bridlington, Yorkshire, England.
2 CONT He had two sons:
2 CONT Philip Le Scrope - employed in 6 Rich. I. 1195 to render and account to the king of the revenues of the Archbishop of York, had two daughters: Alice - died without issue, succeeded in inheritance by uncle Maud - died without issue
2 CONC , succeeded in inheritance by uncle Simon Le Scrope Robert Le Scrope was granted lands to the same priory as his father, listed as holding a knight's fee in Yorkshire in 1198.
2 CONT He died after 1198.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Early Scropes
2 CONT The first well-documented ancestor of the Yorkshire Scropes appears to be Robert le Scrope (1134 – aft.1198), who is described as the son of the aunt[2] of Alice de Gant, Countess of Northampton by her husband Richard le Scrope. T
2 CONC he Scrope family appear to be related and allied to the Gant family in the 12th century, and possibly trace their origins to Lincolnshire or Northamptonshire."
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrope]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Family of Robert * of SCROPE
2 CONT Partner:	Robert * of SCROPE (1134-1198)
2 CONT Partner:	(unknown)
2 CONT Children:	Simon * of SCROPE (1162-1225)
2 CONT Partner: Robert * of SCROPE
2 CONT Name:	Robert * of SCROPE
2 CONT Sex:	Male
2 CONT Father: Hugh * of SCROPE (1103-1149)
2 CONT Mother:	-
2 CONT Birth	1134 Bridlington, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT Death 1198 (age 63-64)	Bridlington, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT Child 1: Simon * of SCROPE
2 CONT Name: Simon * of SCROPE
2 CONT Sex:	Male
2 CONT Spouse:	Imgoline * (1166-1225)
2 CONT Birth	1162 Flotmanby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT Death 1225 (age 62-63)	Flotmanby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT Note on Husband: Robert * of SCROPE
2 CONT The first well-documented ancestor of the Yorkshire Scropes appears to be Robert le Scrope (1134-aft.1198), who is described as the son of the aunt[5] of Alice de Gant, Countess of Northampton by her husband Richard le Scrope.[6] 
2 CONC The Scrope family appear to be related and allied to the Gant family in the 12th century, and possibly trace their origins to Lincolnshire or Northamptonshire."
2 CONT [https://sites.rootsweb.com/~dearbornboutwell/fam7329.html]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Robert le Scrope, granted lands to the Priory of Bridlington in Yorkshire. He left two sons, Philip, who left two daughters, Alice and Maud, both of whom died without issue. Their inheritance went to their uncle, the second son o
2 CONC f Robert le Scrope, Simon le Scrope.
2 CONT ~Burke's Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Landed Gentry, Volume 2, p. 1203
2 CONT • Background Information. 141
2 CONT In 1166 a Robert Scrope is returned as holding a knight's fee of Earl Simon. He can be identified with the Robert, son of Richard Scrope and of his wife Agnes, daughter of Richard FitzGilbert of Clare and sister of Gilbert and Rog
2 CONC er, Earls of Hertford, to whom Alice de Gant, "comitissa," daughter of Gilbert de Gant and wife of Earl Simon, confirmed all the land which his antecessores had held of her antecessores and of herself in Barton on Humber, namely, 
2 CONC a quarter of the vill, to hold by service of half a knight's fee. The other half fee held by Robert has not been conclusively identified, though it may, as Farrer has suggested, have lain in Flotmanby. From Robert, who may be the 
2 CONC Robert Scrope of Barton who died at Acre in 1190, the holding in Barton, or part of it, descended in the male line through his son Walter to his great-greatgrandson Joice who died in 1304. In the 12th and early 13th centuries ther
2 CONC e were a number of Scropes holding in Barton and Flotmanby; but there is no satisfactory evidence to identify their holdings, or to determine their relationships. It can, however, be suggested with confidence that Philip Escrop an
2 CONC d his brother Simon were members of the family whose senior line was represented by Robert Scrope of Barton on Humber in 1166. Philip Scrope was in 1194 assisting William de Stuteville in accounting for the revenues of the Archbis
2 CONC hopric of York, and in 1201, 1202, and 1203 was acting as his deputy sheriff of Westmorland and Cumberland. In 1204 he was dead, being returned as responsible for a sum paid to the sheriff of Cumberland, for which his heirs were t
2 CONC o answer. He married Alice, who survived him. His daughters by Alice were married, Maud to Thomas, son of Adelard de Willardby; and Alice to Ives, son of Walter de Staxton, also described as Ives de Willardby.
2 CONT ~Cokayne's Complete Peerage, 2nd Edition, Vol. XI, pp. 531-533"
2 CONT [https://cybergata.com/roots/21288.htm]
