2 CONT
2 CONT '''RHYS Gryg/the Hoarse''' (-Llandeilo 1234, bur St David´s).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys and Maredudd the sons of the lord Rhys subjected the castle of Dinevwr and the castle of Cantrev Bychan" in 11
2 CONC 95[472].  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys the Hoarse died at Llandeilo the Great and was buried in Menevia near the grave of his father" in 1233[473].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (1219) as her second husband, '''MATILDA [Joan] de Clare''', widow of WILLIAM de Briouse, daughter of RICHARD de Clare Earl of Hertford & his wife Amicie of Gloucester ([1185/90]-).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wale
2 CONC s records that "Rhys the Hoarse married the daughter of the earl of Clare" in 1219[474].  Her birth date range is estimated from her having had at least one child by her first marriage and at least three by her second.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys & his first wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >a)         RHYS Mechyll (-1244).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys the Hoarse was captured at Llanarthneu by his son Rhys the little, and for the castle of Llanymddyvri was liberated" in 1227[475].  If thi
2 CONC s event is correctly dated, Rhys must have been too old to have born from his father´s marriage to Matilda de Clare.  Lord of Dinefwr.  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys Mechyll son of Rhys the Hoarse, son o
2 CONC f the lord Rhys" died in 1244[476].  m MATILDA de Briouse, daughter of ---.  Rhys & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>i)          RHYS Fychan/junior [Ieuanc] (-Dinevwr Feb 1271, bur Tal y Llychau).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "young Rhys son of Rhys Mechyll son of Rhys the Hoarse" died in 1271 "on the octave of the feast
2 CONC  of St Laurence in the castle of Dinevwr and was buried at Tal y Llychau"[477].  m GWLADUS of Gwynedd, daughter of GRUFFYD ap Llywelyn Prince of Gwynedd & his wife --- (-1261).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "
2 CONC Gwladus the daughter of Gruffudd son of Llywelyn, the wife of the lord Rhys, son of Rhys Mechyll" died in 1261[478].  Rhys & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>(a)       RHYS Wyndod (-after 1283).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys son of Maredudd son of Owain son of Gruffudd son of the lord Rhys, and Rhys Wyndod son of young Rhys son of Rhys Mechell son of Rhys
2 CONC  the Hoarse son of the lord Rhys, nephew sister´s son to the prince, became reconciled to Pain son of Patrick" in 1276[479]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>(b)       GRUFFYDD . 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>(c)       LLYWELYN (-after 1276).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "…Rhys Wyndod son of young Rhys son of Rhys Mechell son of Rhys the Hoarse son of the lord Rhys, nephew sister´s son to the prince…Llywelyn h
2 CONC is brother and Howel and Rhys the Hoarse quitted their territory and went to Gwynedd to Llywelyn" in 1276[480]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>ii)         GWENLLIAN .  m GILBERT Talbot, son of [RICHARD Talbot of Linton & his wife Aline Basset] (-before 8 Sep 1274, bur Wormesley Priory, Herefordshire). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys & his [first/second] wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >b)         MAREDUDD (-Dyryslwyn 6 Aug 1271, bur Whitland).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Maredudd son of Rhys the Hoarse" died 6 Aug 1271 "in the castle of Dyryslwyn and was buried at Whitland"[481].  m ---
2 CONC .  The name of Maredudd´s wife is not known.  Maredudd & his wife had five childen:
2 CONT
2 CONT >>i)          RHYS (-1292).  m (1285) AUDA Hastings, daughter of HENRY Hastings & his wife ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >c)         HYWEL . 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Son of Rhys ap Gruffydd and Gwenllian ferch Madog of Powys
2 CONT Husband of Ellyw verch Thomas; Gwerfyl verch Maelgwn and Mathilde de Clare Father of Llywelyn Dhiriaid ap Rhys Gryg; Madog ap Rhys; Rhys Mechyll ap Rhys Gryg; Maredudd "Goch" ap Rhys Prince of Deheubarth and 2 others Brother of Gr
2 CONC yffydd Ap Rhys Madog; Maelgwn "Hen" ap Rhys; Annest Agnes verch Rhys; Gwenllian Verch Rhys Cynwrig and 2 others Half brother of Elinor verch Rhys; Gruffydd ap Rhys; Cadwgan ap Rhys; Margred verch Rhys; Angharad verch Rhys; Maredud
2 CONC d Gethin ap Gruffydd; Gwenllian #1 verch Rhys; Nest verch Rhys; Gwenllian verch Rhys; Hywel Sais ap Rhys, arglwydd of Sanclêr; Morgan ap Rhys; Cynwrig ap Rhys; Maredudd Ddall ap Rhys; Maredudd ap Rhys and Maelgwyn "Fawr" ap Rhys  
2 CONC
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rhys Gryg (English "Rhys the Hoarse") (died 1234), real name Rhys ap Rhys, also known as Rhys Fychan(i.e. "The Younger") was a Welsh Prince who ruled part of the Kingdom of Deheubarth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys was the fourth son of Rhys ap Gruffydd (The Lord Rhys) and his wife Gwenllian, daughter of Madog ap Maredudd of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mathilde, the daughter of Richard de Clare, 3rd Earl of Hertford. In Rhys ap Gruffydd's old age he had a great deal of trouble keeping control of his sons, and a bitter feud broke out between Gruffydd ap Rhys II and Mae
2 CONC lgwn ap Rhys. Rhys Gryg formed an alliance with Gruffydd against Maelgwn, then in 1195 joined with another brother, Maredudd, in a conspiracy against their father and captured Dinefwr Castle. Their father however retaliated by cap
2 CONC turing both of them and imprisoning them in Ystrad Meurig Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys Gryg reappears in 1204. Rhys ap Gruffydd had died in 1197 and Rhys Gryg's ally Gruffydd in 1201 and Maelgwn had taken possession of most of Deheubarth. In 1204 Rhys made an alliance with Gruffydd's sons, Rhys and Owain, and d
2 CONC rove Maelgwn out of Ystrad Tywi which was then shared between them, with Rhys Gryg getting the Cantref Mawr. By 1211 Rhys had fallen out with his nephews, who were supporters of Llywelyn the Great and intervened on behalf of King 
2 CONC John of England, attacking and capturing Llandovery from them with the help of English Royal troops. After John has forced Llywelyn to give up all his conquests outside the core area of Gwynedd, Rhys joined with Maelgwn to eject h
2 CONC is nephews from the remainder of their lands. However when King John built a castle at Aberystwyth, Rhys and Maelgwn changed sides, attacked the castle and burnt it. In 1212 Rhys attacked and burnt Swansea.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Gruffydd ap Rhys, Rhys and Owain, had now made their peace with King John and gone over to the English Royal side. In 1213 and English army led by Falkes of Breauté was sent to strip Rhys Gryg of his lands and give the
2 CONC m to his nephews. Rhys was defeated in a battle at Llandeilo and was forced to flee to Ceredigion to seek the protection of his brother Maelgwn. Later in the year he was captured by the English and imprisoned at Carmarthen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1215 however the sons of Gruffydd ap Rhys turned against the king and made an alliance with their uncle Maelgwn. The English released Rhys Gryg in the hope that he would start a civil war but instead Rhys joined forces with Lly
2 CONC welyn the Great, and he, along with Maelgwn and the sons of Gruffydd ap Rhys were with Llywelyn in the attack which captured many castles in South Wales in December that year. At the parliament held by Llywelyn at Aberdovey in 121
2 CONC 6, Rhys Gryg was allocated Cantref Mawr and Cantref Bychan and other lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys supported Llywelyn during the remainder of his career. In the war of 1231 he joined with his brother Maelgwn's son, Maelgwn the Younger, to burn Cardigan and then capture the castle for Llywelyn. In 1234 he joined with Maelgw
2 CONC n Fychan again to attack Carmarthen, but received wounds of which he died at Llandeilo Fawr shortly afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in St. David's Cathedral and was succeeded by his son Maredudd. He left another son known as Rhys Mechyll(d.1244), who had a son named Rhys Fychan ap Rhys Mechyll and a daughter named Gwenllian Mechyll, who eventuall
2 CONC y became his heiress and married Gilbert Talbot(d.1274), grandfather of Gilbert Talbot, 1st Baron Talbot(d.1345/6).[2][3] The ancient arms of the House of Dinefwr Gules, a lion rampant or within a bordure or were inherited and ass
2 CONC umed in lieu of the Talbot paternal arms as "arms of alliance" on marriage to a great princess.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5266/bleddyn%20ap%20maenyrch%201.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (August 13, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator)
