2 CONT         "Princess Joan died in 1237 at Garth Celyn and Llywelyn suffered a paralytic stroke later in the same year. He died at the Cistercian Abbey of Aberconwy, his own foundation, on 11th April, 1240 and was buried there. His st
2 CONC one coffin was later removed to the parish church of Llanwrst, where it can still be seen."[24] 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Llywelyn spent most of his life restoring and enhancing the hegemony of his grandfather Owain Gwynedd. A striking youth and a successful warrior at an early age, he acquired (from 1194) lands at the expense of his kinsmen, enabli
2 CONC ng him to master Gwynedd by 1203. Good relations with King John brought recognition and his marriage to John's natural daughter Joan."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Residence
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dolwyddelan castle: built by Llywelyn; old castle nearby may have been his birthplace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llywelyn's main home and court throughout his reign was at Garth Celyn on the north coast of Gwynedd. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT 1197:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "After the death of the lord Rhys, his son Gruffudd succeeded him in the government of the dominion, which was held by Maelgwn his brother, when the said Maelgwn, after being banished before from his territory, came, accompani
2 CONC ed by his men, and also by the family of Gwenwynwyn, to Aberystwyth, and subjugated the town and castle, killing many of the people, and carrying others into bondage, and taking possession of the whole of Ceredigion with its castl
2 CONC es. And after seizing his brother Gruffudd, he sent him to the prison of Gwenwynwyn, who agreeably to his desire sent him to an English prison. And then Gwenwynwyn subjugated Arwystli, and captured Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth and Da
2 CONC vid son of Owain Gwynedd. That year, Owain Cyveiliog died at Ystrad Harebell, the monastery which he himself had founded after putting on the habit of religion."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1199: captured castle of Mold ... using title "prince of the whole of North Wales"; (Latin: tocius norwallie princeps). Llywelyn was probably not in fact master of all Gwynedd at this time since it was his cousin Gruffudd ap Cynan
2 CONC  who promised homage to King John for Gwynedd in 1199.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1201
2 CONT
2 CONT     "The ensuing year, Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, subdued the cantrev of Lleyn, having expelled Maredudd, son of Cynan, on account of his treachery."[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1202
2 CONT
2 CONT     "about the first feast of St. Mary in the autumn, Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, raised an army from Powys, to control Gwenwynwyn, and possess the country. Although Gwenwynwyn was close to him, ... he called ... the other princes 
2 CONC ... related to him, to ... war ... against Gwenwynwyn. ... Elise, son of Madog, son of Maredudd ... refused ... and ... endeavoured to bring ... peace with Gwenwynwyn. And therefore, after the clergy and the religious had conclude
2 CONC d a peace between Gwenwynwyn and Llywelyn], the territory of Elise, son of Madog, his uncle was taken from him. And ultimately there was given him for maintenance, in charity, the castle of Crogen with seven small townships. And t
2 CONC hus, after conquering the castle of Bala, Llywelyn returned back happily. That year, about the feast of St. Michael, the family of young Rhys, son of Gruffudd, son of the lord Rhys, obtained possession of the castle of Llanymddyvr
2 CONC i.[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1203
2 CONT
2 CONT     "The ensuing year, young Rhys, son of Gruffudd subdued the castle of Llanegwad. And then died David, son of Owain, in England, after having been banished out of Wales by Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth. That year Gwenwynwyn, and Mae
2 CONC lgwn, son of Rhys, by devices got possession of the castle of Llanymddyvri, and the castle of Llangadog; and the castle of Dineirth was completed."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1208
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Rhys and Owain, sons of Gruffudd, attacked the castle of Llangadog, which they burned, killing some of the garrison, and imprisoning others."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwenwynwyn of Powys fell out with King John (John summoned him to Shrewsbury in October ... then arrested ... and stripped him of his lands. Llywelyn annexed southern Powys and northern Ceredigion and rebuild Aberystwyth castl
2 CONC e.)[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Summer 1209: accompanied John on a campaign against William I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1209.
2 CONT
2 CONT     "King John went with an immense army into Ireland; and he took from the sons of Hugh de Lacy their land and their a castles. After receiving homage of all in Ireland, and capturing the wife of William Bruse, and young William,
2 CONC  his son, with his wife and his son and daughter, he returned with honour to England. He then put young William and his mother unmercifully to death in the castle of Windsor. That year, the earl of Caerleon built the castle of Dyg
2 CONC anwy, which Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, had previously demolished, for fear of the king. And then also, that earl built the castle of Holywell; and Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, ravaged the territory of that earl." 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1210
2 CONT
2 CONT     "One thousand two hundred and ten was the year of Christ, when Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, made cruel attacks upon the English; and on that account king John became enraged, and formed a design of entirely divesting Llywelyn of
2 CONC  his dominion. And he collected a vast army towards Gwynedd, with the view of utterly destroying it."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1211
2 CONT
2 CONT     "The ensuing year, as Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, prince of Gwynedd, could not brook the many insults done to him by the men of the king, who had been left in the new castle at Aberconway he confederated with the Welsh princes,
2 CONC  namely, Gwenwynwyn, and Maelgwn, son of Rhys, and Madog, son of Gruffudd Maelor, and Maredudd, son of Robert; and rose against the king, subduing all the castles which he had made in Gwynedd, except Dyganwy and Rhuddlan; Mathrava
2 CONC l, in Powys, made by Robert Vepont, they subdued, and whilst they were reducing that, the king, with a vast army, came to oppose them, and he himself burned it with fire. That year, Robert Vepont hanged, at Shrewsbury, Rhys, son o
2 CONC f Maelgwn, who was a hostage to the king, not being yet seven years old. And in the same year, Robert, bishop of Bangor, died."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1215
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Then Giles, bishop of Hereford, made peace with the king, from fear of the pope; ... and he died at Gloucester, about the feast of St. Martin; and his patrimony came to his brother Rheinallt de Bruse, who took for his wife th
2 CONC e (Gladwys), daughter of Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, prince of Gwynedd. ...That year, Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, and the Welsh princes in general, collected a vast army to Caermarthen; and before the end of five days, he obtained t
2 CONC he castle, and razed it to the ground. And then they demolished the castles of Llanstephan and Talacharn and St. Clare. And from thence, on the eve of the feast of St. Thomas the Apostle, they proceeded to Ceredigion, and fought a
2 CONC gainst the castle of Emlyn. Then the men of Cemaes did homage to Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, and the castle of Trevdraeth was delivered to him; which, by general consent, was shattered. And when the garrison of Aberystwyth saw that
2 CONC  they could not maintain the castle, they delivered it up to Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, on the feast of St. Stephen; and the following day, the feast of St. John the Apostle, the castle of Cilgerran was delivered to bim. And then 
2 CONC Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, and all the Welsh princew that were with him, returned to their countries, happy and joyful with victory. And here are the names of the princes who were on that expedition from Gwynedd:— Llywelyn, son of
2 CONC  Iorwerth, prince of Gwynedd, and Howel, son of Gruffudd, son of Cynan, his uncle, and Llywelyn, son of Maredudd, son of Cynan; out of Powys, Gwenwynwyn, son of Owain Cyveiliog, and Maredudd, son of Robert of Cydewain, and the fam
2 CONC ily of Madog, son of Gruffudd Madog, and the two sons of Maelgwn, son of Cadwallon; and out of South Wales, Maelgwn, son of Rhys, and Rhys the Hoarse, his brother, and young Rhys, and Owain, the sons of Gruffudd, son of Rhys. And 
2 CONC these are the names of the castles which were subjugated in that expedition; that is to say, the castle of Senghenydd, the castle of Cydweli, Caermarthen, Llanstephan, St. Clare, Talacharn, Trevdraeth, Aberteivi, and Cilgerran."[2
2 CONC 6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn allied with Philip II Augustus of France, then with the barons rebelling against John, marching on Shrewsbury and capturing it without resistance in 1215.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John was forced to sign Magna Carta, Llywelyn was rewarded with several favorable provisions relating to Wales, including the release of his son Gruffydd, a hostage since 1211. The same year Ednyfed Fychan was appointed sense
2 CONC chal of Gwynedd and was to work closely with Llywelyn for the remainder of his reign. Llywelyn the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT 1216
2 CONT
2 CONT     A year after that and then there was a partition of land between Maelgwn, son of Rhys, and his brother, Rhys the Hoarse, and Rhys and Owain, the sons of Gruffudd, son of Rhys, at Aberdovey, in the presence of Llywelyn, son of 
2 CONC Iorwerth, when all the Welsh princes, for the most part and all the wise men of Gwynedd were summoned thither together. And to Maelgwn, son of Rhys, were allotted three cantrevs of Dyved, that is to say, the cantrev of Gwarthav, t
2 CONC he cantrev of Cemaes, and the cantrev of Emlyn, with Penllwynog and the castle of Cilgerran; and of the Vale of Tywi, the castle of Llanymddyvri, with two comots, namely, Hirvryn and Mallaen, and the manor of Myddvai; and of Cered
2 CONC igion, the two comots of Gwynionydd and Mabwynion. And to young Rhys, and his brother Owain, the sons of Gruffudd, son of Rhys, were allotted the castle of Aberteivi, and the castle of Nant yr Ariant, with throe cantrevs of Ceredi
2 CONC gion. And to Rhys the Hoarse were allotted, as his share the whole of Cantrev Mawr, except Mallaen, and the Cantrev Bychan, except Hirvryn and Myddvai; and to him likewise came Cydweli and Carnwyllon. In that year, Gwenwynwyn, lor
2 CONC d of Powys, made peace with John, king of England, treating with contempt the oath and the engagement which he had plighted to the chieftains of England and Wales, and violating the homage which he had done to Llywelyn, son of Ior
2 CONC werth, and surrendering the hostages that he had given thereon."[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1217:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Reginald de Braose of Brecon and Abergavenny, who had been allied to Llywelyn and married his daughter Gwladus Ddu changed sides. Llywelyn responded by invading his lands, first threatening Brecon, where the burgesses offered 
2 CONC hostages for the payment of 100 marks, then heading for Swansea where Reginald de Braose met him to offer submission and to surrender the town. He then continued westwards to threaten Haverfordwest where the burgesses offered host
2 CONC ages for their submission to his rule or the payment of a fine of 1,000 marks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Treaty of Worcester and border campaigns 1218–1229 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following King John's death Llywelyn concluded the Treaty of Worcester with his successor Henry III in 1218. This treaty confirmed him in possession of all his recent conquests. From then until his death Llywelyn was the dominant 
2 CONC force in Wales, though there were further outbreaks of hostilities with marcher lords, particularly the Marshall family and Hubert de Burgh, and sometimes with the king. Llywelyn built up marriage alliances with several of the Mar
2 CONC cher families.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1220
2 CONT
2 CONT     "That year, on the feast of S. Jean de Collaces next after that Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, cited to him most of the princes of all Wales, and collected a vast army to go against the Flemings of Rhos and Pembroke (William Marsh
2 CONC al, Earl of Pembroke), because of their breaking the peace and the treaty, which the men of England had made between the English and the Welsh, by their committing frequent depredations upon the Welsh, and harrassing them. On the 
2 CONC first day he attacked the castle of Arberth, which the Flemings had built, after having been formerly destroyed by the Welsh; and he obtained the castle by force, and threw it to the ground, after killing some of the garrison, bur
2 CONC ning others, and capturing others. And the following day he destroyed the castle of Gwys, and burned the town. The third day he came to Haverford, and burned the whole of the town to the castle gate. And thus he went round Rhos an
2 CONC d Deugleddyv in five days, making vast slaughter of the people of the country. And after making a truce with the Flemings until the calends of May, he returned back joyful and happy."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early 1223: Llywelyn captured Kinnerley and Whittington castles in Shropshire. In April, Marshalls recaptured Cardigan and Carmarthen. Their campaign was supported by a royal army that took Montgomery. Llywelyn came to an agreemen
2 CONC t with the king at Montgomery in October. His allies in south Wales were given back lands taken by the Marshalls, and Llywelyn gave up Shropshire conquests.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1228: Llywelyn campaigned against Hubert de Burgh, Justiciar of England and Ireland, and one of the most powerful men in the kingdom. Hubert was given the lordship and castle of Montgomery by the king and encroached on Llywelyn's 
2 CONC nearby lands. The king raised an army to help Hubert, who began to build another castle in the commote of Ceri. But in October, the royal army retreated and Henry agreed to destroy the half-built castle for £2,000 from Llywelyn. L
2 CONC lywelyn raised the money by demanding the same sum as the ransom of William de Braose, Lord of Abergavenny, who he captured in the fighting.[27] 1205 Marriage to Joan and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT 1205
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn m. 18 Jun 1205 England; (Aft 16 Apr 1205) Joan (c.1188 - 1236/37), dau of King John I of England. Her mother is disputed; see her profile for further discussion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married Llywelyn the Great[28][29]at Ascensiontide 1206,[30] Joan and Llywelyn were married , probably around mid-May.[31][32]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1211-1232
2 CONT
2 CONT Her role as ambassadress and intermediary between her husband and the Crown in the period 1211-32 was an important one. [33]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1228:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1228 Llywelyn's daughter Susanna was made a ward of Nicholas of Verdun: "Henry III King of England granted the upbringing of "L. princeps Norwallie et Johanna uxor sua et…soror nostra Susannam filiam suam" to "Nicholao de Verdu
2 CONC n et Clementie uxori sue" by order dated 24 Nov 1228[292]. [34]
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Helen ferch Llywellyn of Caernarvon (c. 1214)
2 CONT     Elen (Helen) verch Llywellyn of Caernarvon, (c.1206 -before 24 Oct. 1253) of Chester. m.1 abt 1222 John de Scotia, Earl of Chester, m.2 Robert de Quincy
2 CONT     ANGHARAD Verch Llywellyn (c.1212)
2 CONT     DAFYDD ap Llywellyn (c.1209 - Mar 1246)
2 CONT     Margaret ferch Llywelyn (c.1230 - aft 1263 Clifford Castle, Herefordshire). m.1 John de Braose; m.2 Walter de Clifford c.1232
2 CONT     Gwenllian "LAS" Verch Llywellyn Of Caernarvon, b. circa 1207.
2 CONT     Gwladus dhu (d.1251) m. Reginald de Braose of Brecon and Abergavenny[35], m. 2nd (1230) Ralph de Mortimer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistresses and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT While some sources suggest that the mother of most of Llywelyn's illegitimate children was his mistress, Tangwystl Goch (c.1168-1198), Cawley [14] reports only Gruffudd as a child of Tangwystl, with the other illegitimate children
2 CONC  the issue of Llywelyn's other mistreses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (c.1196-1244) eldest son. Mother: Tangwystl. m. Senena, Dau. Caradoc ap Thomas of Anglesey. Issue: 4 sons:
2 CONT         Llywelyn ap Gruffydd. 
2 CONT     Tegwared ap Llywelyn[36]
2 CONT     Marared ferch Llywelyn (c.1198-after 1263) m.1John de Braose of Gower, nephew of Reginald de Braose; m.2 Walter Clifford of Bronllys and Clifford.
2 CONT     Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn m. William de Lacey
2 CONT     Angharad ferch Llywelyn, Angharad may have been Joan's dau. Her illegitimacy is uncertain. See Wikipedia: Angharad ferch Llywelyn m. Maelgwn Fychan 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marital problems
2 CONT
2 CONT 1230 William de Braose, 10th Baron Abergavenny, was executed for sleeping with Llywelyn's wife. They were caught in Llwelyn's bedroom.[26][37]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Previously ... Braose allied with Llywelyn during his capture, and arranged a marriage between his daughter Isabella and Llywelyn's heir, Dafydd ap Llywelyn. This was upheld after his execution. Shortly after Braose' death, a 
2 CONC letter from Llywelyn to William's wife, Eva asks if she still wants their kid's wedding to take place ... which did happen. Meanwhile, Joan was put on house arrest, but restored to her the next year.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Spouse
2 CONT
2 CONT 1237: Joan died, "in February, at the court of Aber, and was buried in a new cemetery on the side of the strand ... Howel, bishop of Llanelwy, had consecrated. And in honour of her, Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, built there a monaste
2 CONC ry for barefooted monks, which is called Llanvaes in Mona. And then Ieuan, earl of Caerleon, and Cynvrig, son of the lord Rhys, died. That year, there came again a cardinal from Rome to England, sent, as his legate, by pope Gregor
2 CONC y the ninth."[26] Strata Florida Abbey — council of 1238 & son Dafydd
2 CONT
2 CONT 1238
2 CONT
2 CONT     "The ensuing year, on the morrow after the feast of St. Luke the Evangelist [Oct. 19], all the princes of Wales sware fidelity to David, son of Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, at Strata Florida. And then he took, from his brother G
2 CONC ruffudd, Arwystli and Ceri and Cyveiliog and Mawddwy and Mochnant and Caereinion; leaving to him nothing but the cantrev of Lleyn itself. And then Maredudd, son of Madog, son of Gruffudd Maelor, slew his brother Gruffudd; and imme
2 CONC diately Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, divested him of his territory on that account."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT 1240:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, prince of Wales, died ... and was buried at Aberconway, after taking the habit of religion. And after him David, his son, by Joan, the daughter of king John ... reigned. The month of May following, 
2 CONC David, son of Llywelyn, having with him the barons of Wales, went to Gloucester, to do homage to the king his uncle, and to receive from him his territory lawfully. And then the English sent Walter Marshall, and an army with him, 
2 CONC to fortify Aberteivi."[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial of Llywelyn the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Lee (1893), Llywelyn died 11 April 1240 at the Cisterician Abbey of Aberconwy, Arllechwedd Isaf, Caernarvonshire, Wales, where he had "taken on the habit of religion." He is buried at the abbey.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of Aberffraw, Lord of Snowdon, died at Aberconway Abbey 11 April 1240, where he was buried.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was later reburied at Llanrwst Parish Church. Gwydir Chapel next to the Church of St. Grwst in Llanrwst, Conway, Wales, contains the empty coffin of Llywelyn the Great. He was originally buried at Aberconwy Abbey, but his coffi
2 CONC n was moved to the new abbey at Maenan when the Cistercian monks were forced to move there by Edward I.
2 CONT
2 CONT The whereabouts of Llywelyn's corpse remains a mystery. (http;//www.walesdirectory.co.uk/Towns_in_Wales/Llanrwst_Town_2.htm)
2 CONT
2 CONT         Aberconway Abbey was also known as Aberconway Abbey Parish Church and is now known as St. Mary and All Saints Church, in Conwy, Conwy County, North Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes on a Name
2 CONT
2 CONT Why "ap Iorwerth-26" as the WikiTree ID? Richardson[7] and FMG/MedLands[14] both show him as Llywelyn ap Iorwerth; the Cymru project's naming guide calls for including ab, ap, or ferch in the Last Name at Birth (LNAB) field; and "
2 CONC ap" because of the way Iorwerth is pronounced:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llywelyn ap Iorwerth
2 CONT     [ɬəˈwɛlɨ̞n][4] ap [ˈjɔrwɛrθ][38] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	Himself 	Parents ap Llywelyn & ferch Llywelyn (see list above) 	Llywelyn ap Iorwerth 	Iorwerth ab Owain ap Gruffudd Margred ferch Madog ap Maredudd
2 CONT
2 CONT ab, ap or ferch[39]
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Ferch" actually means "daughter of", so if a woman was named "Elen ferch Llywelyn ap Gwilym", this would mean that her name was Elen, her father's name was Llywelyn, and her father's father's name was Gwilym. Properly, none o
2 CONC f these words, "ap", "ab", or "ferch", are capitalized. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The terms come from the fact that those are the words in the Welsh anguage which mean "son of" and "daughter of". Kind of like "filius" in Latin or "...sson" in Scandinavian languages. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The difference between "ap" and "ab" is the sound which follows it. We do the same in the English language with "a bicycle" and "an orange". The spelling and pronunciation of the indefinite article changes depending on whether
2 CONC  it preceeds a vowel sound or a consonant sound. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     In Welsh, "ap" comes before a consonant sound, thus "Einion ap Llywelyn", "Jenkin ap Gruffudd", "Llywarch ap Bran". "Ab", on the other hand, comes before a vowel sound, thus "Maredudd ab Einion", "Huw ab Owain". The reason I s
2 CONC aid "vowel sound" and not "vowel" is that in certain circumstances the initial letter "I" in Welsh can sound like a consonant. Thus "Rhys ap Iorwerth" and "Dafydd ap Ieuan". Kind of like in English where an initial "U" can sound l
2 CONC ike a consonant. We say "a university", not "an university". 
2 CONT
2 CONT     As you would expect, not everybody knows enough Welsh to handle patronyms properly, so you'll find lots of variations in the forms, even among people who write on Welsh genealogies. But the above is the correct Welsh way of do
2 CONC ing things. 
2 CONT
2 CONT What's in a name? The following "Meaning & History" information is from Behind the Name:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Iorwerth: Means "handsome lord" from Welsh ior "lord" and berth "handsome". This name is used in the Mabinogion, a collection of tales from Welsh myth, where it belongs to a son of Maredudd. This name is sometimes used as a We
2 CONC lsh form of Edward.
2 CONT     Llywelyn: Possibly a Welsh form of the old Celtic name Lugubelenus, a combination of the names of the gods LUGUS and BELENUS. Alternatively it may be derived from Welsh llyw "leader". This was the name of several Welsh rulers,
2 CONC  notably the 13th-century Llywelyn the Great who fought against England. 
2 CONT
