2 CONT -------------------- Henri II "Courtmanteau", roi d' Angleterre had a personality which commanded attention and drew men to his service; a good companion, with ready repartee in a jostling crowd, but displaying at times the ungove
2 CONC rnable temper of a furious animal; and he could be heartless and ruthless when necessary.4 He lacked visible majesty, was of stocky build, with a freckled face, close-cut tawny hair, and gray eyes, dressing carelessly and he grew 
2 CONC to be bulky.4 Also called Henricus Rex Et Dux.5 Henri II "Courtmanteau", roi d' Angleterre also went by the name of Henry Fitz Empress. Henri II "Courtmanteau", roi d' Angleterre also went by the name of Henry II "Curtmantle." Ann
2 CONC als of Monte Fernando 1133: "Henricus filius imperatricis nascitur."6 He was born on 5 March 1133 in Le Mans, Sarthe, Anjou, France.2,7 He was the son of Geoffroy V "Plantagenêt", comte d'Anjou and Matilda "le Impératrice", reine 
2 CONC d' Angleterre.8,5 He was was chartered in 1150 in Rouen, Normandy, France. 11th Count of Anjou in France, between 7 September 1151 and 1189.9 Count of Maine in France, between 7 September 1151 and 1189. Duke of Normandy in France,
2 CONC  between 7 September 1151 and 1189.9 Arms: Gules two lions passant guardant or.10 He becomes Duke of Aquitaine, by right of his wife, and this position is more valuable than England itself, in 1152. Duke of Aquitaine in France, be
2 CONC tween 11 May 1152 and 1168.9 He married Aliénor d' Aquitaine, reine d' Angleterre, daughter of Guillaume X "le Toulousain", duc de Guyenne, comte de Poitiers and Ænor de Châtellerault, on 18 May 1152 in Bordeaux Cathedral, Gironde
2 CONC , France; Her 2nd. 3rd cousins, 1x removed.11,12 Styled Dei Gratia Rex Anglorum, dux Normannorum, dux Aquitanorum et comes Andegavorum in 1154.13 Annals of Monte Fernando 1154: "Ob. Rex Stephanus, subiit Henricus dux Normannie."14
2 CONC  Arms: Gules, three lions passant guardant or. King of England between 25 October 1154 and 6 July 1189.9 He was crowned King of England on 19 December 1154.5 Annals of Monte Fernando 1155: "Coronacio regis Henrici London.15'" Anna
2 CONC ls of Monte Fernando 1157: "Henricus duxit exercitum in Walliam."16 He was defeated by Owain Gwynedd in 1157 in Coleshill on the Dee Estuary. Annals of Monte Fernando 1158: "Coronatur rex Henricus apud Lincolniem."17 He associated
2 CONC  with Countess Ida de Tosny, daughter of seigneur de Conches Roger III de Tosny and Gertrud de Hainaut; A mistress. Annals of Monte Fernando 1171: "Anglici ceperunt Hiberniam."18 Ward of Ireland. He sailed to Ireland with two hund
2 CONC red and forty ships, putting in at Port-Lairge, in 1171.19 He associated with "Fair" Rosamund de Clifford, daughter of Walter, 1st Lord Clifford and Margaret de Tosny, before 1173; A concubine of Henry's.20,21 He associated with A
2 CONC nnabel Balliol, daughter of Barnard Balliol and Agnes Piquigny, circa 1173. Annals of Monte Fernando 1173: "Dissencio inter regem Francie et Anglie, et inter regem Curt Mauntel et Henricum filium ejus."22 He and William I "the Lio
2 CONC n", King of Scotland concluded a peace treaty between England and Scotland in 1174.23 Lord of Ireland between June 1176 and 1177.9 Annals of Monte Fernando 1182: "Ob. Henricus primus rex Anglie."24 Annals of Monte Fernando 1188: "
2 CONC Ob. secundus Henricus rex, subiit Ricardus filius ejus."25 He died on 6 July 1189 in Chinon Castle, Indre-et-Loire, France, at age 56 years, 4 months and 1 day. "Died an embittered old man."5 Henri II "Courtmanteau", roi d' Anglet
2 CONC erre was buried in the Royal Abbey, Fontevraud-l'Abbaye, Maine-et-Loire, Pays de la Loire, France. The 12th-century abbey church contains the painted effigies of the English Plantagenet kings Henry II and Richard I (“the Lion Hear
2 CONC t”), Henry's wife, Eleanor of Aquitaine, and Isabella d'Angoulême, wife of King John.26
