0 @N6000000001355646583@ NOTE Ruled BET. 582 - 602 Byzantine Emperor 2
1 CONT Event: Crowned 13 AUG 582 Byzantine Emperor 2
1 CONT Event: Acceded 5 AUG 582 Byzantine Emperor 2
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Maurice, Latin in full MAURICIUS FLAVIUS TIBERIUS (b. c. 539, Cappadocia--d. 602, Constantinople), outstanding general and emperor (582-602) who helped transform the shattered late Roman Empire into a new andwell-organized medieva
1 CONC l Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT Maurice first entered the government as a notary but in 578 was made commander of the imperial forces in the East. Distinguished by his successes against the Persians, he was selected by the emperor Tiberius II as his successor. O
1 CONC n Aug. 5, 582, he was made emperor and betrothedto Tiberius' daughter Constantina. He was crowned on August 13, the day preceding Tiberius' death.
1 CONT In the East, Maurice led his armies against Persia, reaching a satisfactory peace settlement after helping Khosrow II gain the Persian throne. With peace restored, Maurice could turn to the North, where nomadic Slavs and Avars wer
1 CONC e establishing permanent settlements in the empire. His campaign had some success, for in 602 the Avars went over to the imperial side. In the West, Maurice is credited with establishing anew kind of civil administration in war-to
1 CONC rn Italy. He appointed military governors for Rome and Ravenna--the exarchate of Ravenna--when herealized that the civil authorities were unable to protect remaining Byzantine territory from the advancing Lombards. He later create
1 CONC d an exarchate at Carthage, in North Africa, designed to withstand the attacks of Berber tribesmen. The two exarchates were provinces whose civil administration was placed in the hands of military officials. They arebelieved to ha
1 CONC ve been the basis for the system of provincial rule (themes) used in the later Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT Maurice's campaigns against Persians, Slavs, Avars, and Lombards drained the imperial treasury and necessitated the collection of high taxes. Dissatisfaction grew within the army, and, when he ordered some troops to set up winter 
1 CONC quarters on the far side of the Danube River, a revolt broke out. The mutinous soldiers rallied behind Phocas, one of their junior officers, and marched on Constantinople. The citizens revolted, Maurice was overthrown, and Phocas 
1 CONC was crowned emperor.
0 @N6000000002187918520@ NOTE Maurice (emperor)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Mauricius Tiberius Augustus, Maurice or Maurikios (*539 in Arabissus/Cappadocia, †602 in Constantinople), known in English as Maurice, was a Byzantine Emperor who ruled from 582-602. He was one of the most important rulers
1 CONC  of the early 'Byzantine' era, whose reign was troubled by almost unending wars on all frontiers.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Persian War and accession to the throne
1 CONT     * 2 Balkan warfare
1 CONT     * 3 Measures of domestic policy
1 CONT     * 4 Death
1 CONT     * 5 Assessment
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT     * 7 Notes
1 CONT     * 8 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Persian War and accession to the throne
1 CONT
1 CONT Maurice originated from Arabissus in Cappadocia and was a successful commander-in-chief. He was adopted by his predecessor Tiberius II, and succeeded him after the latter’s death. His reign is an accurately documented era of the l
1 CONC ate classical antiquity (most important source is the historian Theophylact Simocatta).
1 CONT
1 CONT During a war with the Sassanid Empire, already under way in 572 under Justin II, Maurice was in service as commander-in-chief from 579 on. He scored a crushing victory against the Persians in 581. A year later, he married Constant
1 CONC ina, the Emperor’s daughter. On August 13th, he succeeded his father-in-law. At that time, he ruled a bankrupt Empire, paying extremely high tribute to the Avars, its Balkan provinces thoroughly devastated by the Slavs and at war 
1 CONC with Persia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maurice had to continue the war against Persia. In 586, his troops defeated the Persians at Daraa. Despite serious mutiny in 588, they managed to stand up to the Persians for two more years, until Chosroes II and Persian commander
1 CONC -in-chief Bahram Chobin 590 overthrew King Hormizd IV. Chosroes II subsequently fled to the Byzantine court. Although the Senate advised against it one voice, Maurice helped Chosroes II. to regain his throne. Maurice could finally
1 CONC  bring the war to a successful conclusion by means of a new accession of Chosroes II and defeat of Bahram Chobin. As agreed upon, Chosroes II, probably adopted by Maurice, rewarded the latter by ceding north eastern Mesopotamia an
1 CONC d Armenia up to the capital Dvin and the Lake Van and Iberia (eastern Georgia) up to the capital Tbilisi. Afterwards, Maurice imposed a Union between the Armenian Church and the Patriarchate of Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Balkan warfare
1 CONT
1 CONT After his victory on the eastern frontier, Maurice focused on the Balkans and transferred parts of Armenian nobility to south eastern Europe. The Slavs, having pillaged the Roman Balkan provinces for decades, began settling the la
1 CONC nd from the 580’s on. The Avars took the strategically important fort of Sirmium in 582, using it as a base of operations against several poorly defended forts alongside the Danube from 583 on. In 584 the Slavs threatened the capi
1 CONC tal and in 586 Avars besieged Thessaloniki, while Slavs went as far as the Peloponnese.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maurice launched several campaigns against Slavs and Avars from 591 on, with good prospect of turning the tide. In 592 his troops retook Singidunum from the Avars. His commander-in-chief Priscus defeated Slavs, Avars and Gepids in
1 CONC  593 on Roman territory south of the Danube, before he crossed the Danube into modern-day Wallachia to continue his series of victories. Maurice replaced Priskos (594) with his rather inexperienced brother Peter, who nonetheless s
1 CONC cored another victory in Wallachia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Priskos, again in command of another army further upstream, defeated the Avars (595). The latter only dared to attack again in 597 to score a success. In 598 a treaty was signed with the Avar leader Bayan, only to be broken for re
1 CONC taliation campaigns inside Avar homeland. In 599 and 601, the Byzantine forces wreaked havoc amongst the Avars and Gepids. In 602 the Slavs suffered a crucial defeat in Wallachia. The Byzantine troops were now able to hold the Dan
1 CONC ube line again. Meanwhile, Maurice was making plans for resettling devastated areas in the Balkans by using Armenian settlers.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Measures of domestic policy
1 CONT
1 CONT In the west, he organized the threatened Byzantine dominions in Italy and Africa into exarchates, ruled by military governors or exarchs, being mentioned in 584 and 591 respectively. The exarchs had more or less complete military 
1 CONC and civilian competences. This was remarkable due to the usual separation of civilian and military competences in that era. By founding the exarchate of Ravenna, Maurice managed to slow down the Lombard advance in Italy, if not to
1 CONC  halt it. In 597, an ailing Maurice wrote his last will, in which he described his ideas of governing the Empire. His eldest son, Theodosius, would be a ruler of the East from Constantinople, the second one, Tiberius, of the West 
1 CONC with the capital in Rome. Some historians believe that two youngest sons were supposed to gain Illiricum and North Africa. But as he intended to maintain unity of the Empire, this idea bears a strong similarity with the Tetrarchy 
1 CONC of Diocletian, given the fact that Maurice also maintained claims on the former western provinces now ruled by Germanic tribes. Maurice's violent death thwarted these plans however.
1 CONT
1 CONT In religious matters, he was very tolerant towards Monophysitism, although he was a supporter of the Council of Chalcedon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Summed up, his attempts to consolidate the Empire slowly but steadily met with success, last but not least thanks to the peace with Persia. His initial popularity apparently decreased during his reign, mostly because of his fiscal
1 CONC  politics. In 588, his announcement to cut military wages by 25% led to serious mutiny of troops on the Persian front. He is said to have refused to pay a very little ransom in 599 or 600 to deliver thousands of Byzantine soldiers
1 CONC  taken prisoners by the Avars. It is said that the prisoners were killed and a military delegation, headed by an officer named Phocas was humiliated and rejected in Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT In 602 Maurice, always dealing with the lack of money, decreed that army should stay for winter beyond the Danube, which would prove to be a serious mistake. The exhausted troops mutinied against the Emperor. Probably misjudging t
1 CONC he situation, Maurice repeatedly ordered his troops to start a new offensive rather than returning to winter quarters. After a while, his troops gained the impression that Maurice no longer mastered the situation, they proclaimed 
1 CONC Phocas their leader and demanded Maurice to abdicate and proclaim the successor either his son Theodosius or General Germanus. Both men were accused of treason, but the riots broke out in Constantinople and the emperor with his fa
1 CONC mily left the city for Nicomedia. Theodosius headed east to Persia, but historians are not sure whether he had been sent there by his father or if he had fled there. Phocas entered Constantinople in November, supported by the Gree
1 CONC n Circus party, where he was crowned emperor, while his troops captured Maurice and his family. Maurice was brutally murdered on November 27th 602. It is said that the deposed emperor was forced to watch his three sons executed be
1 CONC fore his eyes, before he was beheaded himself (He is worshipped as a Saint in the Orthodox Church). Empress Constantina and her three daughters were spared and sent to a monastery. The Persian King Chosroes II used this coup and t
1 CONC he murder of his Patron as an excuse for a renewed war against the Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Assessment
1 CONT
1 CONT Maurice, whose court still used Latin in the same way as the army and administration did, was in total an able Emperor and commander-in-chief, even though Theophylact’s description may be a bit too glorifying. He possessed insight
1 CONC , public spirit and courage. He proved his expertise on military and foreign affairs during his campaigns against Persians and Avars/Slavs in the same way as during peace negotiations with Chosroes II. His administrative reforms p
1 CONC ortray him as a statesman with farsightness, the more so since they outlasted his death by far and were the basis for the introduction of the themes as military districts.
1 CONT
1 CONT He also promoted Science and Arts; Maurice is also the traditional author of the military treatise Strategikon which is praised in military circles as the first and only sophisticated combined arms theory until World War II. Howev
1 CONC er, some historians now believe the Strategikon is the work of his brother or another general in his court.
1 CONT
1 CONT His greatest weakness was his inability to judge how unpopular his decisions were. Or to cite the historian Previte-Orton, listing a number of character flaws in the Emperor's personality:
1 CONT “ His fault was too much faith in his own excellent judgement without regard to the disagreement and unpopularity which he provoked by decisions in themselves right and wise. He was a better judge of policy than of men.[1] ”
1 CONT
1 CONT It was this flaw that cost him throne and life and thwarted most of his efforts to prevent the disintegration of the great empire of Justinian I.
1 CONT
1 CONT It seems, as if Maurice attempted to have his way on behalf of Imperial pretension with respect to the old Imperium Romanum, but as his end shows, he met strong resistance.
1 CONT
1 CONT His demise is a turning point in history, given the fact that the new war against Persia weakened both empires in a way enabling the Slavs to permanently settle the Balkans and paving the way for Arab/Muslim expansion. The English
1 CONC  Historian A.H.M. Jones conludes the final era of classical antiquity with Maurice’s death, as the turmoil which shattered the Byzantine Empire in the next four decades permanently and thoroughly changed Society and Politics.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bagnell Bury, John (1899). Later Roman Empire. 
1 CONT     * Charles, R. H. The Chronicle of John, Bishop of Nikiu: Translated from Zotenberg's Ethiopic Text, 1916. Reprinted 2007. Evolution Publishing, ISBN 978-1-889758-87-9. [1]
1 CONT     * Gibbon, Edward (1912). J. B. Bury ed.: Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire. 
1 CONT     * Martindale, John (1992). Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire IIIb, 855–860. 
1 CONT     * Schreiner, Peter (1985). Theophylaktes Simokates: Geschicht
1 CONT     * Shlosser, Franziska E. (1994). The Reign of the Emperor Maurikios (582–602). A reassessment (Historical Monographs 14). 
1 CONT     * Whitby, Michael (1988). The Emperor Maurice and His Historian – Theophylact Simocatta on Persian and Balkan Warfare. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Previte-Orton, Charles William, The shorter Cambridge medieval history (Cambridge: University Press, 1952), p. 203.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Media on Maurice in the Wikicommons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Tiberius II Constantine Byzantine Emperor
1 CONT 582 - 602
1 CONT with Tiberius II Constantine (582) Succeeded by
1 CONT Phocas
