0 @N6000000002187576207@ NOTE Pulcheria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Pulcheria (daughter of Theodosius I))
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Pulcheria (January 19, 399 – 453) was the daughter of the Eastern Roman Emperor Arcadius and Aelia Eudoxia.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the elder sister of Theodosius II, she held much of the power when he came to the throne as a child in 408. She took a vow of virginity to avoid being forced into a marriage, and tended to rely on the support of the various Ger
1 CONC manic military officers she appointed, such as the Alan Aspar. By 416 Theodosius was capable of ruling by himself, but she remained a very strong influence, having had herself proclaimed Empress in 414. That year she had Theodosiu
1 CONC s remove all pagans from the civil service; she was a devout Christian and under her influence both Theodosius and his wife Aelia Eudocia (a former pagan) became devout Christians as well. In 421 Theodosius declared war on Persia,
1 CONC  due to Pulcheria's influence, apparently because the Persians were persecuting Christians. She did not oppose Arianism, the form of Christianity practised by the Germanic tribes. Theodosius originally supported Patriarch Nestoriu
1 CONC s, but Pulcheria, with the help of Archbishop Cyril of Alexandria, convinced him to return to Orthodoxy. Nestorius was exiled in 435. Pulcheria also convinced her brother to exile the Jews and destroy their synagogues in Constanti
1 CONC nople.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 441 Chrysaphius, a eunuch, convinced Theodosius to dismiss Pulcheria, although Chrysaphius simply took her place leaving Theodosius with little power. Pulcheria became a nun, but by 450, when Theodosius died, Pulcheria had been
1 CONC  allowed to return to court. Chrysaphius and Pulcheria struggled for control after Theodosius' death; Pulcheria allied with the Germanic military officers, and married one of Aspar's generals, Marcian, declaring that Theodosius ha
1 CONC d declared Marcian his successor. The marriage was arranged with the understanding that he respect Pulcheria's vow of chastity. Pulcheria then had Marcian execute Chrysaphius.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 451 she helped organize the Council of Chalcedon to condemn Nestorianism. The Council also condemned the Robber Synod of 449, which had supported the Monophysite heresy and the Monophysite abbot Eutyches, who was deposed and ex
1 CONC iled at Chalcedon. At Chalcedon Pulcheria declared Flavian of Constantinople a martyr, after his deposition at the Robber Synod and his death at the hands of the supporters of Eutyches, whom he had opposed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pulcheria also commissioned many new churches in Constantinople, especially to the Virgin Mary. She died in July, 453, leaving Aspar as the dominant influence on Marcian, who himself died in 457. Pulcheria is recognized as a saint
1 CONC  by the Roman Catholic Church and the Eastern Orthodox Church.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Aunt and namesake
1 CONT Pulcheria was also the name of a daughter of Theodosius I and Aelia Flaccilla. She was born in 378 and died in 385.
0 @N6000000002187665709@ NOTE Justa Grata Honoria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Honoria crowned Augusta by the hand of God.Justa Grata Honoria was the sister of the western Roman emperor Valentinian III. Coins of her attest that she was granted the title of Augusta.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of several striking examples, in which Late Antique history seems to abound, of strong women whose scope of action was restricted by her gender, she seems to have despised her brother for his weakness and lack of energy, and a
1 CONC ttempted to make away with him using as an instrument her lover, the palace comptroller Eugenius. When found out, she was hurriedly betrothed to Flavius Bassus Herculanus, a senatorial nonentity: at which point she achieved her gr
1 CONC eatest notoriety, writing a letter to Attila the Hun in spring 450 asking him to save her from the unwanted marriage. Taking the letter — which came with her ring as proof it was sent by her — as a betrothal and thus an invitation
1 CONC  to invade the Western Roman Empire, using the letter as his excuse and "asking for half of the western Empire as dowry", Attila ravaged Gaul and Italy from 451 to 453.
1 CONT
1 CONT Only the influence of their mother Galla Placidia convinced Valentinian to exile, rather than kill, Honoria, after the emperor discovered his sister's plan.
1 CONT
1 CONT The sources for Honoria's life are Merobaudes, Carmina, I; Priscus, fragments 2, 7, 8, De legibus gentium; John of Antioch, frag. 84 De insidiis; and Jordanes, Get. 223-224, Rom. 328.
0 @N6000000000437226343@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Vandals 1  NAME /Hunneric/ 2SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001 1  DEAT 2  DATE480 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import:Jan 17, 2001
