0 @N6000000002188327631@ NOTE Flavius Dalmatius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Dalmatius (died 337), also known as Dalmatius the Censor, was a censor (333), and a member of the Constantinian dynasty, which ruled over the Roman Empire at the beginning of the 4th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dalmatius was the son of Constantius Chlorus and Flavia Maximiana Theodora, and thus half-brother of the Emperor Constantine I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dalmatius spent his youth in the Gallic Tolosa. It is probable that his two sons, Dalmatius and Hannibalianus, were born here. During the mid-320s, Flavius Dalmatius returned to Constantinople, to the court of his half-brother, an
1 CONC d was appointed consul and censor in 333.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Antioch, Flavius was responsible for the security of the eastern borders of the realm. During this period, he examined the case of bishop Athanasius of Alexandria, the important opponent of the Arianism, who was accused of murd
1 CONC er. In 334, Flavius suppressed the revolt of Calocaerus, who had proclaimed himself emperor in Cyprus. In the following year he sent some soldiers to the council of Tyros to save the life of Athanasius.
1 CONT
1 CONT His two sons were appointed of important offices under Constantine's administration, but Flavius Dalmatius and his sons were killed in the purges that followed the Emperor's death in May 337.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Roman Emperors DIR
0 @N6000000002188327749@ NOTE Flavia Julia Constantia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavia Julia Constantia, (after 293 – c. 330), was the daughter of the Roman Emperor Constantius Chlorus and his second wife, Flavia Maximiana Theodora.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 313, Emperor Constantine I, who was half-brother of Constantia, gave her in marriage to his co-emperor Licinius, on occasion of their meeting in Mediolanum. She bore a son, Valerius Licinianus Licinius, in 315, and when the str
1 CONC uggle between Constantine and Licinius began in 316, she stayed on her husband's side. A second war started between the two emperors in 324; after Licinius' defeat, Constantia interceded with Constantine for her husband's life. Co
1 CONC nstantine spared Licinius life, and obliged him to live in Thessalonica as a private citizen, but the following year (325), he ordered that Licinius be killed. A second blow for Constantia was the death, also by order of Constanti
1 CONC ne, of her son Licinianus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following years, Constantia lived at her brother's court, receiving honors (her title was nobilissima femina). She converted to Christianity,[1] supporting the Arian party at the First Council of Nicaea (325).[2]
1 CONT
