1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Constantina (daughter of Constantine I)", De Imperatoribus Romanis
0 @N6000000002188327571@ NOTE Helena of Constantinople
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Helena
1 CONT
1 CONT Eastern Orthodox icon of Saint Constantine the Great and Saint Helena, his mother
1 CONT Mother of Constantine the Great
1 CONT Born ca. 250
1 CONT Died ca. 330
1 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholic, Orthodox and Lutheran authorities.
1 CONT Canonized Her canonization precedes the practice of formal Canonization by the Pope[1] or the relevant Orthodox and Lutheran churches.
1 CONT Feast August 18 (RC), May 21 (L & O), May 19 (L), 9 Pashons (Coptic Orthodox)
1 CONT Attributes Cross
1 CONT Patronage archeologists, converts, difficult marriages, divorced people, empresses, Helena, the capital of Montana
1 CONT Saints Portal
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavia Iulia Helena Augusta, also known as Saint Helena, Saint Helen, Helena Augusta or Helena of Constantinople (ca. 250 – ca. 330) was consort of Constantius Chlorus, and the mother of Emperor Constantine I. She is traditionally
1 CONC  credited with finding the relics of the True Cross and eventually gave name to the island Saint Helena in the South Atlantic Ocean.
1 CONT Contents
