1 CONC 982, 27), Lenski (2006, 61), and Potter (2004, 344–5) reject the narrative as propaganda or legend. Odahl (2004, 77, 308–9) credits it as true, calling those who deny it "a few revisionists" (309). Later narratives of the event, s
1 CONC uch as Lactantius, Victor, the Epitome, and Zosimus, have Constantine arriving in Britain at the deathbed of his father. Such narratives are generally assumed to be contracted versions of earlier sources, such as the Origo and Pan
1 CONC egyrici Latini 6(7).
