1 CONT Rome
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Virius Nepotianus
1 CONT Mother Eutropia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Iulius Popilius Nepotianus Constantinus (died 30 June 350) was a member of the Constantinian dynasty, and a short-lived usurper of the Roman Empire. He ruled the city of Rome for twenty-eight days, before being killed by h
1 CONC is rival usurper Magnentius' general Marcellinus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Nepotianus was the son of Eutropia, half-sister of Emperor Constantine I, and of Virius Nepotianus. On his mother's side, he was the grandson of Emperor Constantius Chlorus and Flavia Maximiana Theodora.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Events
1 CONT
1 CONT After the revolt of Magnentius, Nepotianus proclaimed himself "emperor" and entered Rome with a band of gladiators on 3 June 350. After attempting to resist Nepotianus with an undisciplined force of Roman citizens, the defeated Pr
1 CONC aefectus urbi Titianus (or Anicius, or Anicetus), a supporter of Magnentius, fled the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnentius quickly dealt with this revolt by sending his trusted magister officiorum Marcellinus to Rome. According to Eutropius, Nepotianus was killed in the resulting struggle (on 30 June), his head put on a lance and brought ar
1 CONC ound the city. In the following days, his mother Eutropia was also killed, during the persecution of the supporters of Nepotianus, most of whom were senators.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Nepotian (350 A.D.)", DIR
1 CONT     * Aurelius Victor De Caesaribus 42.6, Epitome 42.3
1 CONT     * Eutropius x.11
1 CONT     * Zosimus, ii.59
0 @N6000000002188327546@ NOTE Maximian
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximian
1 CONT Caesar then Augustus of the West
1 CONT Coin featuring Maximian
1 CONT Reign July 21[1] or July 25[2] 285 – 286 (as Caesar under Diocletian)
1 CONT March 1, 286[3] – May 1, 305 (as Augustus of the West, with Diocletian as Augustus of the East)[4]
1 CONT Late 306 – November 11, 308 (declared himself Augustus)[5]
1 CONT 310 (declared himself Augustus)[6]
1 CONT Full name Marcus Aurelius Valerius Maximianus Herculius[7]
1 CONT Born ca. 250[8]
1 CONT Sirmium (Sremska Mitrovica, Serbia)
1 CONT Died ca. July 310[8]
1 CONT Massilia (Marseille, France)
1 CONT Predecessor Numerian
1 CONT Successor Constantius Chlorus and Galerius
1 CONT Consort to Eutropia
1 CONT Children Flavia Maximiana Theodora
1 CONT Maxentius
1 CONT Fausta
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Aurelius Valerius Maximianus Herculius (ca. 250 – ca. July 310[8]), commonly referred to as Maximian, was Caesar (junior Roman Emperor) from July 285[1][2] and Augustus (senior Roman Emperor) from April 1, 286[3] to May 1, 
1 CONC 305.[4] He shared the latter title with his co-emperor and superior, Diocletian, whose political brain complemented Maximian's military brawn. Maximian established his residence at Trier but spent most of his time on campaign. In 
1 CONC the late summer of 285, he suppressed rebels in Gaul known as the Bagaudae. From 285 to 288, he fought against Germanic tribes along the Rhine frontier. Together with Diocletian, he ran a scorched earth campaign deep into the terr
1 CONC itory of the Alamanni tribes in 288, temporarily relieving the Rhenish provinces from the threat of Germanic invasion.
1 CONT
1 CONT The man he appointed to police the Channel shores, Carausius, rebelled in 286, causing the secession of Britain and northwestern Gaul. Maximian failed to oust Carausius, and his invasion fleet was destroyed by storms in 289 or 290
1 CONC . Maximan's subordinate, Constantius, campaigned against Carausius' successor, Allectus, while Maximian held the Rhenish frontier. The rebel leader was ousted in 296, and Maximian moved south to combat Moorish pirates in Iberia an
1 CONC d Berber incursions in Mauretania. When these campaigns concluded in 298, he departed for Italy, where he lived in comfort until 305. At Diocletian's behest, Maximian abdicated on May 1, 305, gave the Augustan office to Constantiu
1 CONC s, and retired to southern Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In late 306, Maximian took the title of Augustus again and aided his son Maxentius' rebellion in Italy. In April 307, he attempted to depose his son, but failed and fled to the court of Constantius' successor, Constantine, in Trie
1 CONC r. At the Council of Carnuntum in November 308, Diocletian and his successor, Galerius, forced Maximian to renounce his imperial claim again. In early 310, Maximian attempted to seize Constantine's title while the emperor was on c
1 CONC ampaign on the Rhine. Few supported him, and he was captured by Constantine in Marseille. Maximian committed suicide in the summer of 310 on Constantine's orders. During Constantine's war with Maxentius, Maximian's image was purge
1 CONC d from all public places. However, after Constantine ousted and killed Maxentius, Maximian's image was rehabilitated, and he was deified.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
