0 @N6000000002188289013@ NOTE Faustus Cornelius Sulla (senator)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla (78 BC-47 BC) was a Roman senator. Faustus was eldest surviving son of the Dictator of Rome Lucius Cornelius Sulla, born in Arrentium (another source states he was born in Greece where his mother had fled b
1 CONC etween 86 and 81 BC). He was known simply as Faustus, the only Roman to be called that since Faustus Valerius in the time of Numa Pompilius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla was the head of the branch of the Patrician family Cornelius cognimated Sulla, a family consisting only of himself, and his cousin Publius Cornelius Sulla, adherent to Faustus' nephew by marriage Julius Cae
1 CONC sar. As a member of one of the most ancient patrician families, the Cornelii, and the son of the great dictator, promising things were prophesied for the young Sulla, doubly so as, after his father's death, he was brought up by hi
1 CONC s guardian, his father's friend Lucullus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus married Pompeia Magna, daughter of the notable Pompey. They had three children. His career as an advocate was cut short, however, by the civil war between Pompey and Caesar. He, as Lucullus' ward and Pompey's son-in-law, s
1 CONC ided with the former and was killed in a minor skirmish with Caesarian troops in 47 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT by Pompeia Magna 
1 CONT Faustus (II) Cornelius Sulla 
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla Felix Ahenobarbus 
1 CONT Cornelia Sulla married Lucius Scribonius Libo 
1 CONT By a Peasant woman 
1 CONT Gnaeus Ulpius 
0 @N6000000002188289111@ NOTE Mucia Tertia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mucia Tertia was a Roman matrona that lived in the 1st century BC. She was the daughter of Quintus Mucius Scaevola, the pontifex maximus, consul in 95 BC. Her mother was a Licinia that divorced her father to marry Quintus Caeciliu
1 CONC s Metellus Nepos, in a scandal mentioned by several sources. Her name, Mucia Tertia, suggests that she was a third daughter, according to the Roman naming convention for women. Mucia had also two younger brothers from her mother's
1 CONC  second marriage (see Caecilius Metellus family tree), and she was a cousin (soror) of Q. Metellus Celer, consul in 60 BC, and of Q. Metellus Nepos, consul in 57 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla, as dictator, needed to secure Pompey's loyalty and to do that, he arranged his marriage to Mucia around 79 BC. This marriage resulted in three children: Gnaeus Pompeius (Pompey the Younger), the girl Pompeia Magna (married 
1 CONC to Faustus Cornelius Sulla) and Sextus Pompeius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Between 76 and 61 BC, Pompey spent most of the time away from Rome, campaigning in Hispania against Sertorius, in the Mediterranean Sea against the pirates and in the East, and fighting king Mithridates VI of Pontus. On his final 
1 CONC return, in 61 BC, Pompey sent Mucia a letter of divorce. According to Cicero's personal correspondence, the motive was adultery (it is said that she was one of Julius Caesar's many affairs). Mucia next married M. Aemilius Scaurus,
1 CONC  a step­son of the dictator Sulla. In 39 BC, Mucia, at the earnest request of the Roman people, went to Sicily to mediate between her son Sextus Pompey and Augustus. She was living at the time of the battle of Actium, 31 BC. Augus
1 CONC tus treated her with great respect.
1 CONT
1 CONT
