0 @N6000000002188289289@ NOTE Sextus Pompeius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextus Pompeius Magnus Pius, in English Sextus Pompey, was a Roman general from the late Republic (1st century BC). He was the last focus of opposition to the Second Triumvirate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextus Pompeius was the youngest son of Pompey the Great (Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus) by his third wife, Mucia Tertia. His older brother was Gnaeus Pompeius, from the same mother. Both boys grew up in the shadow of their father, one o
1 CONC f Rome's best generals and originally non-conservative politician who drifted to the more traditional faction when Julius Caesar became a threat.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BC, thus starting a civil war, Sextus' older brother Gnaeus followed their father in his escape to the East, as did most of the conservative senators. Sextus stayed in Rome in the care of his 
1 CONC stepmother, Cornelia Metella. Pompey's army lost the battle of Pharsalus in 48 BC and Pompey himself had to run for his life. Cornelia and Sextus met him in the island of Mytilene and together they fled to Egypt. On the arrival, S
1 CONC extus watched his father being killed by treachery on September 29 of the same year. After the murder, Cornelia returned to Rome, but in the following years Sextus joined the resistance against Caesar in the African provinces. Tog
1 CONC ether with Metellus Scipio, Cato the younger, his brother Gnaeus and other senators, they prepared to oppose Caesar and his army to the end.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar won the first battle at Thapsus in 46 BC against Metellus Scipio and Cato, who committed suicide. In 45 BC, Caesar managed to defeat the Pompeius brothers in the battle of Munda, in Hispania (the Iberian Peninsula, comprisi
1 CONC ng modern Spain and Portugal). Gnaeus Pompeius was executed, but young Sextus escaped once more, this time to Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT Back in Rome, Julius Caesar was murdered on the Ides of March (March 15) 44 BC by a group of senators led by Cassius and Brutus. This incident did not lead to a return to normality, but provoked yet another civil war between Caesa
1 CONC r's political heirs and his assassins. The second triumvirate was formed by Octavian, Mark Antony and Lepidus, with the intention of avenging Caesar and subduing all opposition. Sextus Pompeius in Sicily was certainly a rebellious
1 CONC  man, but the Cassius and Brutus faction was the second triumvirate's first priority. Thus, with the whole island as his base, Sextus had the time and resources to develop an army and, even more importantly, a strong navy operated
1 CONC  by Sicilian marines.
1 CONT
1 CONT Brutus and Cassius lost the twin battles of Philippi and committed suicide in 42 BC. After this, the triumvirs turned their attentions to Sicily and Sextus.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextus Pompeius denarius, minted for his victory over Augustus' fleet. On the obverse the Pharus of Messina, on the reverse monster Scylla, who defeated Augustus.But by this time, Sextus was prepared for strong resistance. In the 
1 CONC following years, military confrontations failed to return a conclusive victory for either side and in 39 BC, Sextus and the triumvirs signed for peace in the Pact of Misenum. The reason for this peace treaty was the anticipated ca
1 CONC mpaign against the Parthian Empire. Antony, the leader, needed all the legions he could get so it was useful to secure an armistice in the Sicilian front. The peace did not last for long. Octavian and Antony's frequent quarrels we
1 CONC re a strong political motivation for resuming the war against Sextus. Octavian tried again to conquer Sicily, but he was defeated in the naval battle of Messina (37 BC) and again in August 36 BC. But Octavian had Marcus Vipsanius 
1 CONC Agrippa, a very talented general, on his side. Only a month afterwards, Agrippa destroyed Sextus' navy off Naulochus cape. Sextus escaped to the East and, by abandoning Sicily, lost his only base of support.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextus Pompeius was caught in Miletus in 35 BC and executed without trial (an illegal act since Sextus was a Roman citizen) by order of Marcus Titius, Antony's minion. His violent death would be one of the weapons used by Octavian
1 CONC  against Antony several years later, when the situation between the two reached a boiling point.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextus had married his great, great niece Scribonia. Scribonia was the daughter of Lucius Scribonius Libo, consul of 34 BC. Scribonia's father was Sextus' great nephew. He was the son of Cornelia Sulla and Lucius Scribonius Libo. 
1 CONC Cornelia Sulla was a daughter of Pompeia Magna from her first marriage. Pompeia Magna was Sextus' elder sister. Sextus and Scribonia had a daughter and only child called Pompeia Magna.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Chronology
1 CONT 48 BC - in Egypt with his father, who is assassinated 
1 CONT 47/45 BC – resistance in Africa 
1 CONT 45 BC - defeated in Munda 
1 CONT 42 BC - controls Sicily with a powerful navy 
1 CONT 39 BC - pact of Misenum with Octavianus and Antony 
1 CONT 37 BC - defeats Octavian off Messina 
1 CONT 36 BC - 
1 CONT August, defeats Octavian 
1 CONT September, defeated by Agrippa off Naulochus (Sicily) 
1 CONT 35 BC - captured and executed in Asia Minor (Miletus) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT  Media related to Sextus Pompeius from the Wikimedia Commons. 
0 @N6000000002188286992@ NOTE Lucius Scribonius Libo
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT There were three men of plebs status called Lucius Scribonius Libo who lived in the Roman Republic and Roman Empire who were members of the gens Scribonia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo (flourished 1st century BC) was a member of a Roman Senatorial family. Lucius had married Cornelia Sulla, the daughter of Pompeia Magna (daughter of triumvir Pompey from his third marriage to Mucia Tertia) a
1 CONC nd senator Faustus Cornelius Sulla (son of dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla). Cornelia bore Lucius two children: a daughter called Scribonia (the second wife of future Roman Emperor Augustus) and a son of the same name; 
1 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo - (flourished 1st century BC) son to the above. He was the maternal uncle to consul Publius Cornelius Scipio, Cornelia Scipio and Julia the Elder. Lucius served as a consul of 34 BC. Lucius had died after hi
1 CONC s consulship. His wife was a member of the gens Sulpicius, the family that the Roman Emperor Galba, had descended from his paternal side. Lucius and wife had three children, two sons: Lucius Scribonius Libo (below) and Marcus Scri
1 CONC bonius Libo Drusus and a daughter Scribonia (she married her great great uncle the political rebel Sextus Pompeius. He was the maternal uncle of her paternal grandmother). Their sons had died from violent deaths during Roman Emper
1 CONC or Tiberius' first treason trials in 16. 
1 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo - (flourished 1st century BC & 1st century, died 16) son of the above. He was a consul in 16. This nobleman had planned to revolt against the Roman Emperor Tiberius. The Emperor had tried him in a Senatorial
1 CONC  Court. Lucius had pleaded to the Emperor for the support of Tiberius‘ son Julius Caesar Drusus but the emperor rejected this. Lucius and Tiberius took part in a sacrifice among the priests. During the ceremony, the Emperor had as
1 CONC ked Lucius for assistance, when the ceremony was over, Tiberius stabbed him with a knife. This occurred after the trial. Lucius had married Cornelia Pompeia Magna a distant relative, who was the daughter of Pompeia Magna from her 
1 CONC second marriage to consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna. Pompeia bore Lucius, a daughter and only child Scribonia. Scribonia married the consul Marcus Licinius Crassus Frugi and had children. 
0 @N6000000001876715445@ NOTE Flavius Magnus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Flavius Magnus (born ca 390 or 405 ΓÇô 475) was a great personage in Gaul, where he was widely respected for his integrity and practical wisdom. He was a Roman Senator of Narbonne (then Narbo), Consul of Rome with Flavius Apolloni
1 CONC us in 460 and the Prefect of Gaul in 469.
1 CONT His father, born ca 380, might have been the son of Ennodius, Proconsul of Africa. He might have been Flavius Felix (380 ΓÇô 430), Consul of Rome in 428, who married Padusia and was an ancestor of Felix, Consul in 511 (?). His mot
1 CONC her (b. 385) was a daughter of Flavius Julius Agricola, Consul of Rome in 421 and father of Emperor Avitus.
1 CONT He was the father of:
1 CONT * Magnus Felix (430 ΓÇô after 469), a Patron in 469, married to Attica (b. 440), perhaps the parents of:
1 CONT * a son or a daughter (b. 460 or 465), who begot:
1 CONT * Flavius Arcadius Placidus Magnus Felix, Consul of Rome in 511
1 CONT * Araneola (b. 435 or 440), married to Polemius, perhaps them the parents of:
1 CONT * a son or a daughter (b. 460 or 465), who begot:
1 CONT * Flavius Arcadius Placidus Magnus Felix, Consul of Rome in 511
1 CONT * Flavius Probus, a Roman Senator
1 CONT * Ennodius (perhaps) (CRP 458), the father of Cynegia?
1 CONT Sources and references
1 CONT * Sidonius Apollinaris, The Letters of Sidonius (Oxford: Clarendon, 1915), pp. clx-clxxxiii
1 CONT * Christian Settipani, Les Anc├¬tres de Charlemagne (France: ├ëditions Christian, 1989).
1 CONT * Christian Settipani , Continuite Gentilice et Continuite Familiale Dans Les Familles Senatoriales Romaines A L'epoque Imperiale, Mythe et Realite, Addenda I - III (juillet 2000- octobre 2002) (n.p.: Prosopographica et Genealogic
1 CONC a, 2002).
