0 @N6000000002187718186@ NOTE Lepidus the Younger
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Aemilius Lepidus the Younger was the only child of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus the triumvir. He was executed by Caesar Augustus as a leader in a conspiracy against him. His wife was a descendant of Pompey the Great and Roman Di
1 CONC ctator Lucius Cornelius Sulla. His children were Manius Aemilius Lepidus and Aemilia Lepida II.
0 @N6000000002187718167@ NOTE Manius Aemilius Lepidus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Manius Aemilius Lepidus was the son of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus the Younger, and consul in AD 11.
1 CONT
1 CONT He defended his sister at her trial in 20. In 21 Tiberius appointed him to the governorship of Asia. Tiberius disliked him, describing him as "a lazy degenerate pauper" in the Roman senate. However, the senate thought of him as "m
1 CONC ild, rather than lazy".
1 CONT
1 CONT Aemilia Lepida IV was his only child.
0 @N6000000002187718211@ NOTE Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (triumvir)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT This page is about the contemporary of Caesar Augustus. For other generations of his family see Marcus Aemilius Lepidus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (Latin: M·AEMILIVS·M·F·Q·N·LEPIDVS¹), died 13 BC, was a patrician Roman politician of the 1st century BC. Known simply as Lepidus he was the son of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus and Appuleia, the daughter of the 
1 CONC rebellious Lucius Appuleius Saturninus. His father was also involved in a rebellion against the Republic and died because of it. He was a member of the second triumvirate and pontifex maximus. Lepidus was among Julius Caesar's gre
1 CONC atest supporters. He started his cursus honorum as a praetor in 49 BC, and was rewarded with the consulship in 47 BC, after the defeat of the Pompeians in the East. Following Caesar's assassination in March 15, 44 BC (the Ides of 
1 CONC March), Lepidus allied himself with Mark Antony in a joint bid for power. But Caesar had left an heir: Octavian, his great-nephew and adopted son, had a matching ambition and the popularity with which to climb the hierarchy. Toget
1 CONC her they formed the second triumvirate, legalized with the name of Triumvirs for the Organization of the People by the Lex Titia of 43 BC. The triumvirate had a legal life span of five years and was renewed in 38 BC with the Treat
1 CONC y of Tarentum for an equal period of time.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the pacification of the East and the defeat of the assassins faction, during which Lepidus remained in Rome, he went to rule the western provinces of Hispania and Africa. Somehow, Lepidus managed to remain aside the frequent
1 CONC  quarrels between his colleagues Antony and Octavian. However, in 36 BC, an ill-judged political move was the excuse that Octavian needed. Lepidus was accused of usurping power in Sicily and attempt of rebellion and was forced to 
1 CONC exile. All of his offices were taken away, except the one of pontifex maximus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lepidus died peacefully in 13 BC. Lepidus was the brother of Lucius Paullus and father to Lepidus the Younger.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1- English: "Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, son of Marcus, nephew of Quintus".
0 @N6000000002187718049@ NOTE Claudia Marcella
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Claudia Marcella was the name of both daughters of Octavia the sister of Caesar Augustus, and her first husband, the consul Gaius Claudius Marcellus.
1 CONT
1 CONT The elder, Claudia Marcella Major (b. 44 BC), was married to Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa in 28 BC to cement a political alliance between Agrippa and her uncle. Their daughter Vipsania Marcella was born in Rome in 27 BC. In 21 BC Agri
1 CONC ppa divorced Claudia to marry Augustus' daughter Julia Caesaris. She then married Iullus Antonius, the son of Mark Antony, who was later exiled for adultery with Julia. Their son Lucius Antonius was born in 20 or 19 BC. After Iull
1 CONC us' exile, Claudia married Sextus Appuleius, the consul of AD 14. In AD 17 their daughter Appuleia Varilla was charged with adultery and speaking insultingly about the imperial family. Although she was acquitted, her loved Manlius
1 CONC  was banished from Italy and Africa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her younger sister, Claudia Marcella Minor (b. 43 BC), was married first to the consul Aemilius Lepidus Paullus. Their daughter Claudia Pulchra was born in 14 BC. After Paullus' death in 13 BC, Claudia married the consul Marcus Va
1 CONC lerius Messalla Messallinus. Through their son, Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus, she was the grandmother of the emperor Claudius' wife Messalina.
