0 @N6000000002187712755@ NOTE Sister of Julius Caesar
1 CONT Julia Caesaris was the beloved elder sister of Julius Caesar. She was born in Rome in 101BC and had an elder sister of the same name who died young.
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia married Marcus Atius Balbus, a man from a senatorial family. They had 3 daughters Atia Balba Prima, Atia Balba Caesonia and Atia Balba Tertia. Through her second daughter she became the grandmother of Caesar Augustus. Her hu
1 CONC sband died in 52BC and she died in 51BC. Augustus at age 12 delivered her funeral eulogy.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Wikipedia.com
0 @N6000000002188108805@ NOTE Julia Caesaris (wife of Marius)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Caesaris (c.130 BC - 69 BC) was a daughter of Gaius Julius Caesar II and Marcia (daughter of consul Quintus Marcius Rex). She was a sister of Gaius Julius Caesar III (the father of Julius Caesar) and Sextus Julius Caesar III
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT At about 110 BC she married Gaius Marius; as a result, she is sometimes referred to as Julia Maria. They had a son, Gaius Marius the Younger.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Plutarch, it was by marrying her, a patrician woman, that the up-start Marius got the snobbish attention of the senate and launched his political career[1]. Julia is remembered as a virtuous woman devoted to her husba
1 CONC nd and their only child. Her reputation alone permitted her to keep her status, even after Sulla's persecutions against Marius himself and his allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Caesaris died in 69 BC and received a devoted funeral eulogy from her nephew Julius Caesar[2].
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Julii Caesares 
1 CONT Julio-Claudian family tree 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Plutarch, Parallel Lives, "(The Life of) (Caius) Marius", VI 
1 CONT ^ Plutarch, Parallel Lives, "(The Life of) (Julius) Caesar", V; Also: Suetonius, The Twelve Caesars, "I. Julius Caesar", VI: 
1 CONT When quaestor, he pronounced the customary orations from the rostra in praise of his aunt Julia and his wife Cornelia, who had both died. And in the eulogy of his aunt he spoke in the following terms of her paternal and maternal a
1 CONC ncestry and that of his own father: "The family of my aunt Julia is descended by her mother from the kings, and on her father's side is akin to the immortal Gods; for the Marcii Reges (her mother's family name) go back to Ancus Ma
1 CONC rcius, and the Julii, the family of which ours is a branch, to Venus. Our stock therefore has at once the sanctity of kings, whose power is supreme among mortal men, and the claim to reverence which attaches to the Gods, who hold 
1 CONC sway over kings themselves."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Plutarch, Parallel Lives, written approx. 75 CE: 
1 CONT "(The Life of) (Caius) Marius" 
1 CONT John Dryden translation at MIT classics 
1 CONT Stewart/Long translation at Project Gutenberg 
1 CONT Loeb translation at LacusCurtius 
