0 @N6000000002187718060@ NOTE Gaius Claudius Marcellus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Gaius Claudius Marcellus was the name of several men in ancient Rome. Two of the most prominent bearers of this name were first cousins and held the consulship in successive years (50-49 BC).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT C. Claudius Marcellus Major was son of another Gaius and first cousin of the C. Claudius Marcellus Min. (below). He was consul in 49 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT C. Claudius Marcellus Minor (d. 40 BC) was a member of the Claudian Family and a direct descendant of the consul Marcus Claudius Marcellus. His father was also named Marcus, and his mother was named Junia.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was consul in 50 BC. He attempted to recall Julius Caesar from his ten-year governorship in Gaul two years early. Failing this, he called unsuccessfully upon Caesar to resign. He also obstructed Caesar from standing for a secon
1 CONC d consulship in absentia, insisting that he should return to Rome to stand, thereby forgoing the protection of his armies in Gaul. When Caesar finally invaded Italy, Marcellus, unlike his brother and nephew, did not take up arms a
1 CONC gainst him. Caesar subsequently pardoned him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcellus married in an arranged ceremony Octavia Thurinia Minor, a great-niece of the Julius Caesar and sister of Octavian. They had three children: two daughters, both named Claudia Marcella and born in Rome, and a son, Marcus, 
1 CONC born in Baia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Caesar's assassination, Marcellus became a supporter of Octavian. He died in May 40 BC. Five months later, his widow married Mark Antony.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Roman General Publius Quinctilius Varus and his 2 sisters were grandchildren from his first marriage.
