0 @N6000000002187718085@ NOTE Marcus Valerius Messalla Corvinus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Marcus Valerius Messalla Corvinus (64 BC - AD 8) was a Roman general, author and patron of literature and art. He was the son of Marcus Valerius Messalla.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was educated partly at Athens, together with Horace and the younger Cicero. In early life he became attached to republican principles, which he never abandoned, although he avoided offending Caesar Augustus by not mentioning th
1 CONC em too openly. He moved that the title of pater patriae should be bestowed upon Augustus, and yet resigned the appointment of prefect of the city after six days’ tenure of office, because it was opposed to his ideas of constitutio
1 CONC nalism. In 43 BC he was proscribed, but managed to escape to the camp of Brutus and Cassius. After the battle of Philippi (42) he went over to Antony, but subsequently transferred his support to Octavian. In 31 Messalla was appoin
1 CONC ted consul in place of Antony, and took part in the battle of Actium. He subsequently held commands in the East, and suppressed the revolted Aquitanians; for this latter feat he celebrated a triumph in 27.
1 CONT
1 CONT Messalla restored the road between Tusculum and Alba, and many handsome buildings were due to his initiative. His influence on literature, which he encouraged after the manner of Maecenas, was considerable, and the group of litera
1 CONC ry persons whom he gathered round him--including Tibullus, Lygdamus and the poet Sulpicia--has been called "the Messalla circle." With Horace and Tibullus he was on intimate terms, and Ovid expresses his gratitude to him as the fi
1 CONC rst to notice and encourage his work.’ The two panegyrics by unknown authors (one printed among the poems of Tibullus as iv. 1; the other included in the Catalepton, the collection of small poems attributed to Virgil) indicate the
1 CONC  esteem in which he was held.
1 CONT
1 CONT Messalla was himself the author of various works, all of which are lost. They included Memoirs of the civil wars after the death of Caesar, used by Suetonius and Plutarch; bucolic poems in Greek; translations of Greek speeches; oc
1 CONC casional satirical and erotic verses; essays on the minutiae of grammar. As an orator, he followed Cicero instead of the Atticizing school, but his style was affected and artificial. Later critics considered him superior to Cicero
1 CONC , and Tiberius adopted him as a model. Late in life he wrote a work on the great Roman families, wrongly identified with an extant poem De pro genie Augusti Caesaris bearing the name of Messalla, but really a 12th-century producti
1 CONC on.
1 CONT
1 CONT Monographs by L. Wiese (Berlin, 1829), J. M. Valeton (Groningen, 5874), L. Fontaine (Versailles, 1878); H. Schulz, De MV aetate (1886); "Messalla in Aquitania" by J. P. Postgate in Classical Review, March 1903; WY Sellar, Roman Po
1 CONC ets of the Augusian Age. Horace and the Elegiac Poets (Oxford, 1892), pp. 213 and 221 to 258; the spurious poem ed. by R. Mecenatë (1820).
1 CONT
1 CONT Messala's great grandson, also named Marcus Valerius Messala Corvinus, was consul in AD 58 under Nero. He was the elder brother to Roman Empress Valeria Messalina.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000002187712920@ NOTE Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (consul AD 32)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (1st century AD))
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (11 December 17 BC - January 40 AD) was a close relative to the Roman Emperors of the Julio-Claudian dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domitius was the only son to Antonia Major and Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus. Antonia was daughter to Mark Antony and Octavia, sister of Roman Emperor Caesar Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT His only siblings were Domitia Lepida Major and Domitia Lepida Minor, mother of the Empress Valeria Messalina.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notable relatives
1 CONT Domitius' maternal aunts included Antonia Minor, Cleopatra Selene, Claudia Marcella Major, Claudia Marcella Minor. His maternal uncles included Iullus Antonius, Alexander Helios and Ptolemy Philadelphus and Marcellus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domitius was thus first cousin to among others Germanicus, Livilla, Claudius, Ptolemy of Mauretania, Drusilla of Mauretania, Claudia Pulchra and Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domitius was also first cousin once removed to their children. A group including among others Julia Livilla, Drusilla, Agrippina the younger, Drusus Caesar, Nero Caesar, Caligula, Claudia Julia, Tiberius Gemellus, Britannicus, a y
1 CONC ounger Octavia, allegedly Julia Venissa and Messalina.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life account
1 CONT Domitius was thus related to several notable figures who would dominate the Roman Empire during the 1st century. Suetonius describes him as ‘despicable and dishonest’. As a young man, Domitius was serving on the staff of his secon
1 CONC d cousin Gaius Caesar in the East. Gaius was son of Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa and Julia Caesaris, daughter to the previously mentioned Caesar Augustus. Domitius fortified their friendship by killing his freedman. The reported reaso
1 CONC n was that the freedman did not get as drunk as Domitius did. On the Appian Way, Domitius was reported of having deliberately run over a child who was playing with his doll. At the Roman Forum Domitius reportedly pulled out an eye
1 CONC  of a knight because the knight openly criticized him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domitius cheated on bankers, for purchases he made. When Praetor, Domitius would swindle the prize money of victorious charioteers. Managers would complain, but Domitius decreed that future prizes would be paid on the spot. Tiberi
1 CONC us charged him with treason, adultery and incest with his sister but the ascension of Caligula saved him. Domitius was also considered a serious womanizer.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domitius married his cousin Julia Agrippina after her thirteenth birthday in 28 AD. Tiberius arranged and ordered the marriage which was celebrated at the Capital, Rome. Domitius was wealthy but apparently chose to live between An
1 CONC tium and Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domitius was Consul in 32 AD and appointed by Tiberius as a commissioner in early 37 AD.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus (Nero) was born on 15 December 37 AD in Antium. He died of dropsy at Pyrgi (an ancient Etruscan city) in January, 40 AD. In Domitius' will Lucius inherited 1/3 of his estate. But Caligula, who w
1 CONC as also mentioned in the will, took Lucius' inheritance for himself. When Claudius became Emperor, Lucius' inheritance was restored.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT Domitius' widow Agrippina later married her widowed uncle Claudius. Lucius was adopted by the elderly Emperor as "Nero Claudius Caesar Drusus". When Claudius died on October 13, 54, Lucius succeeded him as "Nero Claudius Caesar Au
1 CONC gustus Germanicus". Nero exalted Domitius’ memory and the Roman Senate arranged for the construction of his statue in 55 AD.
0 @N6000000002188286918@ NOTE Nero Caesar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Nero Julius Caesar Germanicus (6–30) was a close relative of the Roman Emperors of the Julio-Claudian dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nero was born in 6 to Germanicus and Agrippina the Elder. His paternal grandparents were Nero Claudius Drusus and Antonia Minor, daughter of Mark Antony and Octavia Minor. His maternal grandparents were Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa an
1 CONC d Julia the Elder, daughter of Caesar Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nero's siblings included four brothers (Tiberius and Gaius Julius, who died young; Drusus; and the future Emperor Caligula) and three sisters (Julia Livilla, Drusilla and Agrippina the younger). In 20, he married Julia, daughter o
1 CONC f Livilla and Drusus "Castor" (Tiberius' only son by Vipsania).
1 CONT
1 CONT Nero was uncle to Julia Drusilla and Emperor Nero Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus (born Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus).
1 CONT
1 CONT His father Germanicus was heir apparent to his own adoptive father Emperor Tiberius, but Germanicus predeceased the Emperor in 19. He was replaced as heir by Julius Caesar Drusus, son of Tiberius and his first wife Vipsania Agripp
1 CONC ina. But he too predeceased the Emperor on September 14, 23.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nero was the oldest adoptive grandson of Tiberius, and was seen as the emperor's most obvious successor. However, he was accused of treason along with his mother in 29. Nero was exiled to the island of Ponza where in 30 he was eit
1 CONC her induced to commit suicide or else starved to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Portraiture
1 CONT Rose, Charles Brian, Dynastic Commemoration and Imperial Portraiture in the Julio-Claudian Period. Cambridge, 1997, nr. 17, pp.66-67. 
0 @N6000000003635723510@ NOTE [4628] "Bloodline ...", p 409
