0 @N6000000002187712442@ NOTE Britannicus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Tiberius Claudius Caesar Britannicus (41 - 55) was the son of the Roman emperor Claudius and his third wife Messalina. Britannicus was probably born on 12 February 41, although this has a margin of error of one year. As the oldest
1 CONC  surviving son of his father, he took Claudius name prior to his accession - Tiberius Claudius Germanicus. To mark the birth, the emperor issued sestertii with the obverse Spes Augusta - the hope of the imperial family. In 43 AD, 
1 CONC Claudius was granted the honorific "Britannicus" by the senate as a reward for his conquest of Britain. The emperor refused it for himself, but accepted it on behalf of his young son. This is the name by which the boy became known
1 CONC  to posterity. According to Suetonius, Claudius doted extensively on his young son. He carried him around at public events, and shouted "Good luck to you, my boy!" to elicit a similar response from the crowds. He was supposedly a 
1 CONC precocious child.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 48, Britannicus became a pawn in the acts that lead to the demise of Messalina. One of the conditions for her bigamous marriage to Gaius Silius was that he adopt her son as his own. They apparently then planned to rule as regen
1 CONC ts for the boy after the planned overthrow of Claudius. Messalina may have believed this was the only way to prevent her son from being killed with his father. Messalina and Silius were discovered shortly after their wedding and p
1 CONC ut to death, having never launched their coup.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the downfall of his mother, Britannicus' youth became a liability for Claudius. The lack of an adult heir made the emperor vulnerable to conspiracies aimed at overthrowing the dynasty, especially those by other Julio-Claudia
1 CONC ns. It was suggested that he should find an older heir and try to bring an end to strife within the family by marrying Agrippina the Younger, the last adult Julian. Shortly after Claudius married Agrippina, he adopted her son Nero
1 CONC , who was older than Britannicus and a direct descendent of Augustus. Nero was married to Britannicus' sister Octavia and named joint-heir with Britannicus until such time as the latter came of age. Nero was a popular young man, a
1 CONC nd his adoption did indeed stave off coup attempts in the second half of Claudius' reign. Britannicus did not get along with his step-family. According to Tacitus, Britannicus continued to refer to Nero by his birth-name, Domitius
1 CONC , long after the adoption. This included public events where the boys were honored as a pair, and jealousy was heightened by Nero's rise to manhood. Nero reacted to these slights by insisting that Britannicus was illegitimate, but
1 CONC  Claudius gave no indication of believing this. Tacitus reports that those who had reason to oppose Agrippina and Nero formed a faction around Britannicus, taking advantage of this discord. Agrippina retaliated against these suppo
1 CONC rters with force, changing Britannicus' circle. His tutor, Sosibius, had once been a tool of Messalina's, and Agrippina quickly disposed of him. Claudius may have agreed to this because of their links to his old officially damned 
1 CONC wife. Such warring factions would have undermined his very reason for adopting Nero and marrying Agrippina.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT A sestertius issued to commemorate Britannicus' birthUnfortunately, the actions Claudius took to preserve his rule in the short-term were not easily undone as Britannicus approached manhood. In late 54, Britannicus was within 6 mo
1 CONC nths of reaching manhood according to Roman tradition, and had matured early. According to Suetonius, Claudius began to mention divorcing Agrippina and dismissing Nero now that he was no longer needed. In preparation, Claudius com
1 CONC mended both his son and adopted son to the Senate as equals in his last Senate address. Suetonius reports that Claudius now admonished his son to grow up quickly, and that everything would be righted when he assumed the toga viril
1 CONC is.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unfortunately for Britannicus, Nero's supporters acted to prevent this. On October 13, 54, Claudius died, probably murdered by Agrippina. Britannicus and his sisters were hidden away in the palace in the aftermath of the murder to
1 CONC  prevent problems with Nero's succession. Nero spoke the elegy at the emperor's funeral and took sole power. Claudius' new will, which either granted joint-rule to Britannicus and Nero or just Britannicus, was suppressed by the ne
1 CONC w emperor's men in the senate. The freedman Narcissus, Britannicus' champion according to Tacitus, was quickly put to death. Britannicus was pushed to the background.
1 CONT
1 CONT Britannicus survived for a few months under the rule of his adopted brother, mostly ignored, but the doings of his step-mother Agrippina lead to his death. In early 55, one of Agrippina's favorites, the freedman Pallas, was sacked
1 CONC  from his job as secretary of the treasury - a post he had held since the reign of Claudius. According to Tacitus, Agrippina reacted violently to this slight by Nero. She declared that she repented of her actions to bring Nero to 
1 CONC the throne, and would throw in her lot with Britannicus, the true heir who would soon come of age. She threatened to take the boy to the Praetorian camp, where she would admit to murdering Claudius and Britannicus would be declare
1 CONC d emperor. Nero did not take this threat lightly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after, Nero moved against Britannicus, employing the same poisoner who had been hired to murder his father. The first dose failed, and Nero decided to throw caution in the wind. Britannicus was poisoned at a dinner party atte
1 CONC nded by his sister, Claudia Octavia, Agrippina, and several other notables. The substance was instantly fatal, and Britannicus fell to the floor foaming at the mouth. Nero dismissed this obvious murder by claiming that the boy suf
1 CONC fered from epilepsy. Some modern historians, particularly Anthony Barrett, suggest that he may have indeed suffered from the disease, and that a particularly bad seizure killed him. This may also be the reason for his eclipse by N
1 CONC ero - although Claudius never spurned his son on purpose. Britannicus was quickly buried in the Mausoleum of Augustus, the last of the Julio-Claudians to be interred there.
1 CONT
1 CONT According the Suetonius, Britannicus was good friends with the future Emperor Titus, whose father Vespasian had commanded legions in Britain. As part of the Flavian's attempts to link themselves with the Julio-Claudians, Titus cla
1 CONC imed that he had been seated with Britannicus on the night he was killed. He even claimed to have tasted the poison, which resulted in a serious and long illness. Titus would go on to erect a gold statue of his friend, and issue c
1 CONC oins in his memory.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Britannicus is also the title of a tragedy by the French dramatist Jean Racine (1669).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Barrett, Anthony. Agrippina: Sex, Power, and Politics in the Early Empire. Yale University Press. New Haven, 1999. 
1 CONT Dio Cassius. Historia Romanum. Books LX-LXII. 
1 CONT Levick, Barbara. Claudius. Yale University Press. New Haven, 1990. 
1 CONT Momigliano, Arnaldo. Claudius: the Emperor and His Achievement. Trans. W.D. Hogarth. W. Heffer and Sons. Cambridge, 1934. 
1 CONT Oost, S.V., "The Career of M. Antonius Pallas", American Journal of Philology, 79 (1958). 113-139. 
1 CONT Scramuzza, Vincent. The Emperor Claudius. Harvard University Press. Cambridge, 1940. 
1 CONT Suetonius. Twelve Caesars. Life of Claudius. 
1 CONT Suetonius. Twelve Caesars. Life of Titus. 
1 CONT Tacitus. Annals. Books XI-XIII. 
0 @N6000000002187712911@ NOTE Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus was the only child of Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (consul 32 BC) and Aemilia Lepida. His mother was a paternal cousin to triumvir Marcus Aemilius Lepidus. His paternal grandmother was Porcia Catones. He
1 CONC  won an honorary triumph, by penetrating deeper into Germany, than anyone else before him. As a young man he was a famous charioteer. Suetonius describes as "arrogant, cruel, notorious and extravagant." Lucius held the office of a
1 CONC edile. As praetor and consul made married knights and married women star in pantomimes. He enjoyed presenting gladiatorial contests and wild animal hunts. In Augustus' will he was nominated to purchase his household possessions. L
1 CONC ucius married Antonia Major, Augustus' niece. They had Domitia Lepida Major, Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and Domitia Lepida. Lucius died in 25 AD.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Gaius Furnius and Gaius Iunius Silanus Consul of the Roman Empire
1 CONT 16 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Marcus Livius Drusus Libo and Lucius Calpurnius Piso 
0 @N6000000002187712961@ NOTE Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, son of Gn. Domitius Ahenobarbus cos 96 BC, husband of Porcia Catones the sister of Cato the younger, friend of Cicero and enemy of Julius Caesar, and a strong supporter of the aristocratical party. At 
1 CONC first strongly opposed to Pompey, he afterwards sided with him against Caesar. He was consul in 54 BC, and in 49 he was appointed by the senate to succeed Caesar as governor of Gaul. After the outbreak of the civil war he commande
1 CONC d the Pompeian troops at Corfinium, but was obliged to surrender. Although treated with great generosity by Caesar, he stirred up Massilia (today's Marseille) to an unsuccessful resistance against him. After its surrender, he join
1 CONC ed Pompey in Greece and was slain in the flight after the battle of Pharsalus, in which he commanded the right wing against Antony (Caesar, Bellum Civile, i., ii., iii.; Dio Cassius xxxix., xli.; Appian, B.C. ii. 82). 
1 CONT
1 CONT From wikipedia.com
