0 @N6000000002187712621@ NOTE Marcus Livius Drusus Claudianus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Livius Drusus Claudianus was the father of the Roman Empress Livia Drusilla. Marcus was born as Appius Claudius Pulcher, who descended from the first Appius Claudius Pulcher, son of the Roman dictator Appius Claudius Caecus
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT As an infant, he lost his parents. He was adopted and raised by his father’s friend Marcus Livius Drusus. Drusus' father and himself were both tribunes.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Alfidia, daughter of a Roman Magistrate. Their daughter Livia Drusilla was born in 58 BC. It’s not known whether they had any other children. Marcus was praetor in 50 BC. He committed suicide along with Cassius and Brut
1 CONC us (the assassins of Julius Caesar) in Philippi Greece. Before his death, he married his daughter to Tiberius Nero, a relative of his.
0 @N6000000002188289183@ NOTE Quintus Mucius Scaevola (consul 174 BC)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Quintus Mucius Scaevola (flourished early 2nd century BC) was a politician of the Roman Republic, believed to be the son of his namesake who was praetor in 215 BC. He was made praetor in 179 BC, and became governor of Sicily. In 1
1 CONC 74 BC he was made consul, and in 171 BC, tribune. In this latter position he accompanied his brother-in-law Publius Licinius Crassus on campaign against Perseus of Macedonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son was Quintus Mucius Scaevola Augur (ca. 159 BC – 88 BC), much younger than his cousins.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
