0 @N6000000002187599912@ NOTE Poppilia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT The Popilii Laenates was the name of an ancient Roman Plebeian family, who lived during the Roman Republic Era. The Popilii were known for their cruel and arrogant nature. The gens name Popilii is of Etruscan origin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Poppilia or Poppillia was the name given to the women of the Popilius Laenas family. During the Roman Republic, there were two women with the name Poppilia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Poppilia, wife of Lucius Julius Caesar II. Their children were Lucius Julius Caesar III (consul of 90 BC and later senator) and a Gaius Julius Caesar Strabo Vopiscus (a humble orator). Strabo Vopiscus might have had a twin brother
1 CONC . She was the paternal grandmother to the consul Lucius Julius Caesar IV and Julia Antonia (mother to Roman Triumvir Mark Antony). Poppilia died in December 110 BC. 
1 CONT Poppilia, wife of consul and Roman statesman Quintus Lutatius Catulus. They had a younger Quintus Lutatius Catulus (120 BC - 61 BC) who would become the consul in 78 BC, become an ally to Roman General Pompey the Great and would d
1 CONC islike Julius Caesar. This Poppilia died, after her husband in 87 BC. According to Cicero (de. Orat. ii. 11.) she was the first Roman woman to received a funeral eulogy over her grave. The eulogy was given by her son and this was 
1 CONC at the time, the highest honor given to a woman in Rome. 
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopaedia 2002 - Quintus Lutatius Catulus 
0 @N6000000000437011543@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Praetor Of Rome
0 @N6000000002187599858@ NOTE Marcus Antonius Creticus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Antonius Creticus (lived 1st century BC) was a Roman politician, member of the Antonius family. Creticus was son of Marcus Antonius Orator and by his marriage to Julia Antonia (cousin of Julius Caesar) he had three sons, Ga
1 CONC ius, Lucius and his namesake Mark Antony. He was elected praetor in 74 BC and in the next year he received an extraordinary commission, similar to that bestowed upon Pompey by the Gabinian law years later and on his father three d
1 CONC ecades before, to clear the Mediterranean Sea of the threat of piracy, and thereby assist the operations against Mithradates VI. Creticus not only failed in the task, but plundered the provinces he was supposed to protect from rob
1 CONC bery (Sallust, Hist. iii., fragments ed. B. Maurenbrecher, p. 108; Velleius Paterculus ii. 31; Cicero, In Verrem, iii. 91). He attacked the Cretans, who had made an alliance with the pirates, but was totally defeated, most of his 
1 CONC ships being sunk. Diodorus Siculus (xl. 1) states that he only saved himself by a disgraceful treaty. He died soon afterwards (72-71) in Crete. All authorities are agreed as to his avarice and incompetence, which earned him the ni
1 CONC ckname Creticus, meaning man of chalk.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
