0 @N6000000002187905376@ NOTE Merfyn Frych ap Gwriad
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Merfyn Frych ap Gwriad (died 844) was a King of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merfyn Frych (=Merfyn the Freckled) seized control of Gwynedd in 825 on the death of Hywel ap Rhodri Molwynog, though he may have held power in Anglesey since 818. According to bardic tradition, Merfyn came "from the land of Manaw
1 CONC ", but it is uncertain whether this refers to the Isle of Man ("Ynys Manaw" in Welsh) or to Manaw Gododdin, the area around the Firth of Forth. It would seem likely that it were the latter on account of the probability he would be
1 CONC  a blood relative of Cunedda, the founder of the Gwynedd dynasty, who was a prince of Manaw Gododdin. He may have had a claim to the throne of Gwynedd through his mother Esyllt, daughter of Cynan ap Rhodri Molwynog. On the other h
1 CONC and there is an inscription "Crux Guriat" on a cross in the Isle of Man. This cross has been dated to the eighth or ninth century and might possibly refer to Merfyn's father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merfyn allied himself to the royal house of Powys by marrying Nest, daughter of Cadell ap Brochwel and sister of Cyngen king of Powys. He had a reputation as a patron of scholars, for example the Historia Britonum attributed to Ne
1 CONC nnius is thought to have been written in Gwynedd during his reign, possibly instigated by Merfyn himself. Despite Danish raids, Merfyn was able to maintain his position and on his death in 844 to hand the kingdom over intact to hi
1 CONC s son Rhodri the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hywel ap Rhodri Molwynog King of Gwynedd
1 CONT 825–844 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Rhodri the Great
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * John Edward Lloyd (1911). A history of Wales: from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest, Longmans, Green & Co..
0 @N6000000001744871429@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Merfyn of the line of Kings of Gwynedd married Nest of the Kings of Powys.  See http://www.castlewales.com/gwynedd.html.MERFYN FRYCH (son of No. 14). Some authorities make Merfyn Frych the husband of No. 14, Ethelytt, instead of h
1 CONC er son, but he appears as the son of Gwriad and No. 14, Ethelyth, in the
1 CONT           earliest records. He married Nest, sister and heiress of Congen ap Cadell, King of Powys. Through
1 CONT           his mother he became King of Gwynedd on the death of her uncle, Hywel in 825. Merfyn Frych was
1 CONT           a descendant of "Llywarch Hen (Llywarch the Aged), poet and warrior, who is said to have lived in
1 CONT           the sixth century and to have held his court on the mound, near Llanfor church, which bears his name."
1 CONT           (Lloyd). He is said to have come from the land of Manaw. The evidence indicates that he came from
1 CONT           the Isle of Man and not from Manaw in Scotland. He was a man of unusual force and energy. "For
1 CONT           nineteen years he maintained his power against all rivals and against the Danish irruptions, and on his
1 CONT           death in 844 he was able to hand it on to his son Rhodri." (Lloyd).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was at war with the Saxons in 823 and in 830 and probably at other times. Burchard, King of
1 CONT           Mercia, made war against Gwynedd and its king, Merfyn Frych, was slain in battle in 844.
1 CONT
1 CONT           In Jesus College MS. 20 (Y Cymmrodor VIII. 87) his pedigree on his father's side is traced back to
1 CONT           Coel Hen, the father-in-law of No. 1 Cunedda, and through his father's grandmother, Celenion, it is
1 CONT           traced to Maxen Wledic (Prince Maximus), who was a Roman official of Spanish birth in Britain, and
1 CONT           who took advantage of the popular discontent with the reign of the Roman Emperor Gratian, caused
1 CONT           himself to be proclaimed Emperor, raised an army, crossed over to the continent and, in A.D. 383,
1 CONT           fought with the army of Gratian who was killed in the battle. Maximus then became Emperor of
1 CONT           Western Europe, including Britain, France, Spain, Holland, Switzerland and a part of Germany, "and
1 CONT           for several years
1 CONT
1 CONT           he ruled them not unjustly." (Lloyd). He was defeated and overthrown in 388 in an ambitious effort to
1 CONT           conquer Italy. He was a Christian and "took pains to put himself forward as a special champion of
1 CONT           orthodoxy." (Lloyd). Thus we have one more ancestor of distinction.Merfyn of the line of Kings of Gwynedd married Nest of the Kings of Powys.  See http://www.castlewales.com/gwynedd.html.MERFYN FRYCH (son of No. 14). Som
1 CONC e authorities make Merfyn Frych the husband of No. 14, Ethelytt, instead of her son, but he appears as the son of Gwriad and No. 14, Ethelyth, in the
1 CONT           earliest records. He married Nest, sister and heiress of Congen ap Cadell, King of Powys. Through
1 CONT           his mother he became King of Gwynedd on the death of her uncle, Hywel in 825. Merfyn Frych was
1 CONT           a descendant of "Llywarch Hen (Llywarch the Aged), poet and warrior, who is said to have lived in
1 CONT           the sixth century and to have held his court on the mound, near Llanfor church, which bears his name."
1 CONT           (Lloyd). He is said to have come from the land of Manaw. The evidence indicates that he came from
1 CONT           the Isle of Man and not from Manaw in Scotland. He was a man of unusual force and energy. "For
1 CONT           nineteen years he maintained his power against all rivals and against the Danish irruptions, and on his
1 CONT           death in 844 he was able to hand it on to his son Rhodri." (Lloyd).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was at war with the Saxons in 823 and in 830 and probably at other times. Burchard, King of
1 CONT           Mercia, made war against Gwynedd and its king, Merfyn Frych, was slain in battle in 844.
1 CONT
1 CONT           In Jesus College MS. 20 (Y Cymmrodor VIII. 87) his pedigree on his father's side is traced back to
1 CONT           Coel Hen, the father-in-law of No. 1 Cunedda, and through his father's grandmother, Celenion, it is
1 CONT           traced to Maxen Wledic (Prince Maximus), who was a Roman official of Spanish birth in Britain, and
1 CONT           who took advantage of the popular discontent with the reign of the Roman Emperor Gratian, caused
1 CONT           himself to be proclaimed Emperor, raised an army, crossed over to the continent and, in A.D. 383,
1 CONT           fought with the army of Gratian who was killed in the battle. Maximus then became Emperor of
1 CONT           Western Europe, including Britain, France, Spain, Holland, Switzerland and a part of Germany, "and
1 CONT           for several years
1 CONT
1 CONT           he ruled them not unjustly." (Lloyd). He was defeated and overthrown in 388 in an ambitious effort to
1 CONT           conquer Italy. He was a Christian and "took pains to put himself forward as a special champion of
1 CONT           orthodoxy." (Lloyd). Thus we have one more ancestor of distinction.
