0 @N6000000003051221255@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Merfyn Frych ap Gwriad", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Merfyn Frych ap Gwriad (or Merfyn the Freckled) (died 844) was a King of Gwynedd and possibly also of Powys, who styled himself King of the Britons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merfyn Frych seized control of Gwynedd in 825 on the death of Hywel ap Rhodri Molwynog, though he may have held power in Anglesey since 818. Merfyn was not a member of the traditional dynasty of Gwynedd, the direct male line of Ma
1 CONC elgwn Gwynedd, and his succession marked the start of a new dynasty. His claim was apparently based on the fact that his mother, Esyllt, was the daughter of Cynan Dindaethwy ap Rhodri and the niece of Hywel ap Rhodri. According to
1 CONC  bardic tradition, Merfyn came "from the land of Manaw", but it is uncertain whether this refers to the Isle of Man ("Ynys Manaw" in Welsh) or to Manaw Gododdin, the area around the Firth of Forth. It would seem likely that it was
1 CONC  the latter on account of the probability he would be a blood relative of Cunedda, the founder of the Gwynedd dynasty, who was a prince of Manaw Gododdin. On the other hand there is an inscription "Crux Guriat" on a cross in the I
1 CONC sle of Man. This cross has been dated to the eighth or ninth century and might possibly refer to Merfyn's father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merfyn allied himself to the royal house of Powys by marrying Nest, daughter of Cadell ap Brochwel and sister of Cyngen king of Powys. He had a reputation as a patron of scholars; for example the Historia Britonum attributed to Ne
1 CONC nnius is thought to have been written in Gwynedd during his reign, possibly by request of Merfyn himself. A manuscript found at Bamberg gives a further insight into Merfyn's scholarly interests. Irish visitors to his court were gi
1 CONC ven a cryptogram which could only be solved by transposing the letters from Latin into Greek.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite Danish raids, Merfyn was able to maintain his position and on his death in 844 to hand the kingdom over intact to his son Rhodri the Great. He is said to have died in battle, but the circumstances are not recorded. His des
1 CONC cendants came to rule not only Gwynedd but also Powys and Deheubarth and played a major role in Welsh politics until the end of Welsh independence in 1283.
0 @N6000000002795189627@ NOTE From NationMaster.com
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Encyclopedia > Rhodri the Great
1 CONT Rhodri the Great a.k.a. Roderick the Great (in Welsh, "Rhodri Mawr") (c. 820–878) was the first ruler of Wales to be called "Great", and the first to rule most of present-day Wales. Welsh redirects here, and this article describes
1 CONC  the Welsh language. ... Events Michael II succeeds Leo V as Byzantine Emperor The Historia Brittonum is written (approximate date) Births Rhodri Mawr (the Great), ruler of Gwynedd (Wales) (approximate date) Photius I, patriarch o
1 CONC f Constantinople (approximate date) Deaths December 24: Leo V, Byzantine Emperor (assassinated) Shankara, Hinduist teacher Tang Xian Zong, emperor of... Events The Danes force king Alfred the Great of Wessex to retreat to a fort i
1 CONC n Athelney, Somerset. ... National motto: Cymru am byth (Welsh: Wales for ever) Waless location within the UK Official languages English(100%), Welsh(20. ... 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Merfyn Frych, he inherited the principality of Gwynedd on his father's death in 844, and Powys from his uncle. He proceeded to marry Angharad, daughter of the ruler of Seisyllwg, which he duly inherited on the accidenta
1 CONC l death of his brother-in-law in 871. Gwynedd was one of the kingdoms or principalities of medieval Wales. ... Events Succession of Pope Sergius II (844 - 847). ... Powys is an administrative county in Wales, over 2000 sq. ... Sei
1 CONC syllwg was a kingdom of medieval Wales. ... Events Nine battles are fought between the Danes and Wessex. ... 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri succeeded in holding off both Viking and Saxon invaders, offering security to his subjects at a time of great danger and unrest throughout the British Isles. It was, however, the Vikings who drove Rhodri away from his home 
1 CONC territory in 877, and he fled to Ireland. Returning the following year in an attempt to regain his kingdom, he was killed in battle against the Mercians of Ceolwulf II. Rhodri slew Gorm, a famous Norse viking leader, during a batt
1 CONC lefield duel, and shortly afterwards wiped out his army; the slaying of Gorm sent shockwaves across the contemporary Norse world. The name Viking is a loan from the native Scandinavian term for the Norse seafaring warriors who rai
1 CONC ded the coasts of Scandinavia, the British Isles, and other parts of Europe from the late 8th century to the 11th century, the period of European history referred to as the Viking Age. ... The Saxons were a large and powerful Germ
1 CONC anic people located in what is now northwestern Germany and a small section of the eastern Netherlands. ... The British Isles consist of Great Britain, Ireland and a number of much smaller surrounding islands. ... The name Viking 
1 CONC is a loan from the native Scandinavian term for the Norse seafaring warriors who raided the coasts of Scandinavia, the British Isles, and other parts of Europe from the late 8th century to the 11th century, the period of European 
1 CONC history referred to as the Viking Age. ... Events The Danes take Exeter Indravarman II succeeds Jayavarman III as ruler of the Khmer Empire. ... Mercia, sometimes spelled Mierce, was one of the kingdoms of the Anglo-Saxon heptarch
1 CONC y, in what is now England, in the region of the Midlands, with its heart in the valley of the River Trent and its tributary streams. ... Ceolwulf II was the twenty-sixth king of Mercia, during 873 to 879. ... 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Merfyn Frych Prince of Gwynedd Succeeded by:
1 CONT Anarawd ap Rhodri 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cyngen Prince of Powys 
1 CONT
1 CONT Categories: 820 births | 878 deaths | History of Wales | Welsh monarchs Before the Norman Conquest of Wales was completed in 1282, Wales consisted of a number of independent principalities, the most important being Gwynedd, Powys,
1 CONC  Deheubarth (originally Seisyllwg and Dyfed), Gwent and Morgannwg. ... Before the Norman Conquest of Wales was completed in 1282, Wales consisted of a number of independent principalities, the most important being Gwynedd, Powys, 
1 CONC Deheubarth (originally Seisyllwg and Dyfed), Gwent and Morgannwg. ... 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT   Results from FactBites: 
1 CONT   GO BRITANNIA! Wales: A Unified People (529 words) 
1 CONT  The first leader of importance to emerge among the Welsh was the warrior king Rhodri Mawr (Rhodri the Great).  
1 CONT  Unfortunately for the future of an independent Wales, Rhodri Mawr's death in 878 was followed by a period of internal strife, and the alliance of his sons with Alfred led to Wales' dependence upon the English king for protection.
1 CONC
1 CONT  Rhodri was killed in battle fighting an English army; it was left to his grandson, Hywel Dda (Howell the Good, right) to re-establish some sort of predominance among the various petty kingdoms of Wales by wisely keeping the peace
1 CONC  with his English neighbors through a policy of conciliation. 
1 CONT Rhodri ap Merfyn (Rhodri Mawr) / 100 Welsh Heroes / 100 Arwyr Cymru (299 words) 
1 CONT  The son of Merfyn Frych, Rhodri inherited the principality of Gwynedd on his father"s death in 844, and Powys from his uncle.  
1 CONT  Anglesey bore the brunt of the attacks, and it was there in 856 that Rhodri won his great victory over Horn, the leader of the Danes.  
1 CONT  Rhodri?s grandson Hywel Dda built on the foundations he had laid, creating the kingdom of Deheubarth. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
