0 @N6000000002187905349@ NOTE Rhodri the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri the Great (in Welsh, Rhodri Mawr; occasionally in English, Roderick the Great) (c. 820–878) was the first ruler of Wales to be called 'Great', and the first to rule most of present-day Wales. He was called King of the Brito
1 CONC ns by the Annals of Ulster.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Lineage & inheritance
1 CONT     * 2 Resistance against Danes
1 CONT     * 3 Defeat and death
1 CONT     * 4 Succession
1 CONT     * 5 External links
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lineage & inheritance
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Merfyn Frych, King of Gwynedd, and Nest ferch Cadell of the Royal line of Powys, he inherited the Kingdom of Gwynedd on his father's death in 844.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his uncle Cyngen ap Cadell ruler of Powys died on a pilgrimage to Rome in 855 Rhodri inherited Powys. In 872 Gwgon, ruler of Seisyllwg in southern Wales, was accidentally drowned, and Rhodri added his Kingdom to his domains b
1 CONC y virtue of his marriage to Angharad, Gwgon's sister. This made him the ruler of the larger part of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Resistance against Danes
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri faced pressure both from the English and increasingly from the Danes, who were recorded as ravaging Anglesey in 854. In 856 Rhodri won a notable victory over the Danes, killing their leader Gorm (sometimes given as Horm). T
1 CONC wo poems by Sedulius Scotus written at the court of Charles the Bald, King of the Western Franks, celebrate the victory of "Roricus" over the Norsemen.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 877 Rhodri fought another battle against the Norse invaders on Anglesey, this time being forced to flee to Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Defeat and death
1 CONT
1 CONT On his return the following year, he and his son Gwriad were said to have been killed by the English under Alfred the Great, though the precise manner of his death is unknown. When his son, Anarawd ap Rhodri won a victory over the
1 CONC  Mercians a few years later, it was hailed in the annals as "God's vengeance for Rhodri".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT His son Cadell ap Rhodri conquered Dyfed, which was later joined with Seisyllwg by Rhodri's grandson Hywel Dda to become Deheubarth. Like his grandfather, Hywel would come to rule most of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Rhodri the Great
1 CONT     * Rhodri Mawr - King of Wales
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Nora K. Chadwick (1963). Celtic Britain. Thames and Hudso
1 CONT     * John Edward Lloyd (1911). A history of Wales: from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest. Longmans, Green & Co.. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Regnal titles
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Merfyn Frych ap Gwriad Prince of Gwynedd
1 CONT 844-878 Succeeded by
1 CONT Anarawd ap Rhodri
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Cyngen ap Cadell King of Powys
1 CONT 855-878
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gwgon Prince of Seisyllwg
1 CONT 872-878 Succeeded by
1 CONT Cadell ap Rhodri
0 @N6000000001744855071@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 844-878. Source: Royal Genealogies <http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/>
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.castlewales.com/rhodri.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri Mawr, King of Wales
1 CONT
1 CONT (Sources: Walker 1990; Davies 1990)
1 CONT
1 CONT According to legend, the first Dinefwr Castle was built by Rhodri Mawr - King of
1 CONT Wales in the 9th century. It is unavoidable that attention should focus on those Welsh rulers who extended their power over much of Wales in the centuries prior to the Norman conquest. They foreshadowed the attempts by the princes
1 CONC  of Gwynedd in the 13th century to create a unified Welsh state, and they matched
1 CONT contemporary developments in England, and similar, but later, developments in Scotland. So, Rhodri Mawr (844-78) is presented as one who set a pattern for the future. He either ruled or, by his personal qualities, dominated much o
1 CONC f Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Chroniclers of his generation hailed Rhodri ap Merfyn as Rhodri Mawr (Rhodri the Great), a distinction bestowed upon two other rulers in the same century - Charles the Great (Charlemagne, died 814) and Alfred the Great (died 899).
1 CONC  The three tributes are of a similar nature - recognition of the achievements of men who contributed significantly to the growth of statehood among the nations of the Welsh, the Franks and the English. Unfortunately, the entire ev
1 CONC idence relating to the life of Rhodri consists of a few sentences; yet he must have made a deep impression upon the Welsh, for in later centuries being of the line of Rhodri was a primary qualification for their rulers. Until his 
1 CONC death, Rhodri was acknowledged as ruler of more than half of Wales, and that as much by diplomacy as by conquest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri's fame sprang from his success as a warrior. That success was noted by The Ulster Chronicle and by Sedulius Scottus, an Irish scholar at the court of the Emperor Charles the Bald at Liege. It was his victory over the Viking
1 CONC s in 856 which brought him international acclaim. Wales was less richly provided with fertile land and with the navigable rivers that attracted the Vikings, and the Welsh kings had considerable success in resisting them. Anglesey 
1 CONC bore the brunt of the attacks, and it was there in 856 that Rhodri won his great victory over Horn, the leader of the Danes, much to the delight of the Irish and the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was not only from the west that the kingdom of Rhodri was threatened. By becoming the ruler of Powys, his mother's land, he inherited the old struggle with the kingdom of Mercia. Although Offa's Dyke had been constructed in ord
1 CONC er to define the territories of the Welsh and the English, this did not prevent the successors of Offa from attacking Wales. The pressure on Powys continued; after 855, Rhodri was its defender, and he and his son, Gwriad, were kil
1 CONC led in battle against the English in 878.
1 CONT
1 CONT _______________
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Weaver, The Weaver Family, 1928:
1 CONT
1 CONT RHODRI MAWR (son of No. 15). He succeeded his father as King of Gwynedd. He was called
1 CONT Rhodri Mawr (Rhodri the Great) on account of his skill in extending the boundaries of his kingdom
1 CONT and in uniting the Welsh people under one government. He became ruler of all Wales except a small
1 CONT part in the extreme south. He married Angharad, daughter of Meurig ap Dyfnwallon. His mother's
1 CONT brother, Cyngen, King of Powys, died in 855. He immediately took possession of Powys and no one
1 CONT seems to have disputed his right to rule. He acquired Seisyllwg on the death of its King Gwgon,
1 CONT brother of Rhodri's wife, who died in 872. He was somewhat successful in resisting the invasions of
1 CONT the Saxons and the Danes, but in 876 he sustained a crushing defeat and fled to Ireland. He returned
1 CONT in 877 and attempted to regain the kingdom, but he was killed by the Saxons in 878. After his death
1 CONT the kingdom does not seem to have been held by the Saxons as it was divided between three of
1 CONT Rhodri's sons, Anawrad, Cadell and Merfyn.Ruled 844-878. Source: Royal Genealogies <http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/>
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.castlewales.com/rhodri.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri Mawr, King of Wales
1 CONT
1 CONT (Sources: Walker 1990; Davies 1990)
1 CONT
1 CONT According to legend, the first Dinefwr Castle was built by Rhodri Mawr - King of
1 CONT Wales in the 9th century. It is unavoidable that attention should focus on those Welsh rulers who extended their power over much of Wales in the centuries prior to the Norman conquest. They foreshadowed the attempts by the princes
1 CONC  of Gwynedd in the 13th century to create a unified Welsh state, and they matched
1 CONT contemporary developments in England, and similar, but later, developments in Scotland. So, Rhodri Mawr (844-78) is presented as one who set a pattern for the future. He either ruled or, by his personal qualities, dominated much o
1 CONC f Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Chroniclers of his generation hailed Rhodri ap Merfyn as Rhodri Mawr (Rhodri the Great), a distinction bestowed upon two other rulers in the same century - Charles the Great (Charlemagne, died 814) and Alfred the Great (died 899).
1 CONC  The three tributes are of a similar nature - recognition of the achievements of men who contributed significantly to the growth of statehood among the nations of the Welsh, the Franks and the English. Unfortunately, the entire ev
1 CONC idence relating to the life of Rhodri consists of a few sentences; yet he must have made a deep impression upon the Welsh, for in later centuries being of the line of Rhodri was a primary qualification for their rulers. Until his 
1 CONC death, Rhodri was acknowledged as ruler of more than half of Wales, and that as much by diplomacy as by conquest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri's fame sprang from his success as a warrior. That success was noted by The Ulster Chronicle and by Sedulius Scottus, an Irish scholar at the court of the Emperor Charles the Bald at Liege. It was his victory over the Viking
1 CONC s in 856 which brought him international acclaim. Wales was less richly provided with fertile land and with the navigable rivers that attracted the Vikings, and the Welsh kings had considerable success in resisting them. Anglesey 
1 CONC bore the brunt of the attacks, and it was there in 856 that Rhodri won his great victory over Horn, the leader of the Danes, much to the delight of the Irish and the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was not only from the west that the kingdom of Rhodri was threatened. By becoming the ruler of Powys, his mother's land, he inherited the old struggle with the kingdom of Mercia. Although Offa's Dyke had been constructed in ord
1 CONC er to define the territories of the Welsh and the English, this did not prevent the successors of Offa from attacking Wales. The pressure on Powys continued; after 855, Rhodri was its defender, and he and his son, Gwriad, were kil
1 CONC led in battle against the English in 878.
1 CONT
1 CONT _______________
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Weaver, The Weaver Family, 1928:
1 CONT
1 CONT RHODRI MAWR (son of No. 15). He succeeded his father as King of Gwynedd. He was called
1 CONT Rhodri Mawr (Rhodri the Great) on account of his skill in extending the boundaries of his kingdom
1 CONT and in uniting the Welsh people under one government. He became ruler of all Wales except a small
1 CONT part in the extreme south. He married Angharad, daughter of Meurig ap Dyfnwallon. His mother's
1 CONT brother, Cyngen, King of Powys, died in 855. He immediately took possession of Powys and no one
1 CONT seems to have disputed his right to rule. He acquired Seisyllwg on the death of its King Gwgon,
1 CONT brother of Rhodri's wife, who died in 872. He was somewhat successful in resisting the invasions of
1 CONT the Saxons and the Danes, but in 876 he sustained a crushing defeat and fled to Ireland. He returned
1 CONT in 877 and attempted to regain the kingdom, but he was killed by the Saxons in 878. After his death
1 CONT the kingdom does not seem to have been held by the Saxons as it was divided between three of
1 CONT Rhodri's sons, Anawrad, Cadell and Merfyn.
0 @N6000000002248215052@ NOTE Rhodri Mawr (the Great) ap Merfyn, Prince of Wales, who became King of Gwynedd in 844 on the death of his father Merfyn Frych, King of Powys in 855 on the death of his uncle Caell ap Brochwell, and King of Seisyllwg in 871 on the 
1 CONC death of his brother-in-law Gwgon. Rhodri Mawr was the first ruler recognised as Prince of Wales. He defeated the Danish leader Horn in 856. 
1 CONT Died in 877 or 878, killed in battle with the English as was his son Gwriad. 
1 CONT Rhodri Mawr ap Merfyn married Angharad, daughter of Meuric ap Dyfnwal ap Asthin ap Sitsllt, Lord Caerdigan, and they had the following sons: 
1 CONT Anarawd ap Rhodri Mawr, King of Gwynedd. Click on Gwynedd <http://www3.sympatico.ca/robert.sewell/wales.html> for his descent. 
1 CONT Cadell ap Rhodri Mawr 
1 CONT Merfyn ap Rhodri Mawr, King of Powys. Click on Powys <http://www3.sympatico.ca/robert.sewell/powys.html> for his descent. 
1 CONT Gwriad ap Rhodri Mawr, killed in battle in 877 or 878, as was his father. 
1 CONT Two sons.
