0 @N6000000000437605788@ NOTE Welsh chieftain called “king of all Wales.” or Howel Dda Hywel the Good He inherited Seisyllwg and secured Dyfed by marriage, thereby creating the kingdom ofDeheubarth. Eventually Gwynedd and Powys also came under his rule. By 942
1 CONC  hisrealm was larger than that of any earlier Welsh ruler. He codified Welsh law during his peaceful reign and accepted the status of subking to the Anglo-Saxon king of Wessex
0 @N6000000001354425324@ NOTE Howel DDA (Welsh), English HYWEL THE GOOD (d. AD 950), chieftain called in the prologues to the Welsh lawbooks "king of all Wales." This epithet was indeed appropriate for Howel, particularly during the last years of his reign.
1 CONT He became ruler of Seisyllwg (roughly the area of Dyfed and the Towy Valley) jointly with his brother Clydog after the death of their father, Cadell (c. 910), but after Clydog's death in 920 he ruled alone. Sovereignty over Dyfed 
1 CONC in southwest Wales came to him through his wife, Elen, daughter of Llywarch ap Hyfaidd (d. 904), the last king of its dynasty; he acquired Gwynedd, in northwest Wales, and probably Powis, in northeast Wales, on the death of his co
1 CONC usin Idwal Foel ap Anarawd, in 942. Howel's reign was remarkable for its peacefulness, the result of his consistent policy of subservience to England. Howel's first recorded act is his homage to Edward the Elder in 918. Thereafter
1 CONC , he often attended the English court, and his name is found as a witness to 12 charters of Athelstan and Edred between 928 and 949.
1 CONT Howel was the only Welsh ruler to issue his own coins. He is remembered chiefly for the codification of Welsh law attributed to him. Although there is no contemporary record of this work, Howel was certainly responsible for a coor
1 CONC dination of preexisting law. There are biographies by J.E. Lloyd (1928) and J.G. Edwards (1929). [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD, 1997, Howel Dda]
1 CONT John Davies, in "A History of Wales," Allen Lane - The Penguin Press,London, 1993, states, "About 904, Llywarch ap Hyfaidd, king of Dyfed,died; his kingdom came into the posession of Hywel ap Cadell ap Rhodri, the ruler of Seisyll
1 CONC wg and the husband of Elen, Llywarch's sister. It would appear that Hywel also took possesion of Brycheiniog, for itsroyal line ends with Tewdwr ap Griffri, who died about 930. The enlarged kingdom came to be known as Deheubarth, 
1 CONC a unit of central importance in the history of Wales during the following four centuries. Deheubarth was united with the territories of Idwal ab Anarawd ap Rhodri -- Gwynedd and Powys -- in 942, and Hywel died in 950 the ruler of 
1 CONC a kingdom which extended from Prestatyn to Pembroke."
0 @N6000000001355643324@ NOTE Howel DDA (Welsh), English HYWEL THE GOOD (d. AD 950), chieftain called in the prologues to the Welsh lawbooks "king of all Wales." This epithet was indeed appropriate for Howel, particularly during the last years of his reign.
1 CONT He became ruler of Seisyllwg (roughly the area of Dyfed and the Towy Valley) jointly with his brother Clydog after the death of their father, Cadell (c. 910), but after Clydog's death in 920 he ruled alone. Sovereignty over Dyfed 
1 CONC in southwest Wales came to him through his wife, Elen, daughter of Llywarch ap Hyfaidd (d. 904), the last king of its dynasty; he acquired Gwynedd, in northwest Wales, and probably Powis, in northeast Wales, on the death of his co
1 CONC usin Idwal Foel ap Anarawd, in 942. Howel's reign was remarkable for its peacefulness, the result of his consistent policy of subservience to England. Howel's first recorded act is his homage to Edward the Elder in 918. Thereafter
1 CONC , he often attended the English court, and his name is found as a witness to 12 charters of Athelstan and Edred between 928 and 949.
1 CONT Howel was the only Welsh ruler to issue his own coins. He is remembered chiefly for the codification of Welsh law attributed to him. Although there is no contemporary record of this work, Howel was certainly responsible for a coor
1 CONC dination of preexisting law. There are biographies by J.E. Lloyd (1928) and J.G. Edwards (1929). [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD, 1997, Howel Dda]
1 CONT John Davies, in "A History of Wales," Allen Lane - The Penguin Press,London, 1993, states, "About 904, Llywarch ap Hyfaidd, king of Dyfed,died; his kingdom came into the posession of Hywel ap Cadell ap Rhodri, the ruler of Seisyll
1 CONC wg and the husband of Elen, Llywarch's sister. It would appear that Hywel also took possesion of Brycheiniog, for itsroyal line ends with Tewdwr ap Griffri, who died about 930. The enlarged kingdom came to be known as Deheubarth, 
1 CONC a unit of central importance in the history of Wales during the following four centuries. Deheubarth was united with the territories of Idwal ab Anarawd ap Rhodri -- Gwynedd and Powys -- in 942, and Hywel died in 950 the ruler of 
1 CONC a kingdom which extended from Prestatyn to Pembroke."
0 @N6000000003098031310@ NOTE By 950 A.D., Dinefwr  was the principal court from which Hywel Dda, "The Good,"  ruled a large part of Wales including the southwest area known as Deheubarth. His great achievement was to create the country's first uniform legal s
1 CONC ystem. Hywel shared with his brothers lands in Ceredigon and Ystrad Tywi after the death of their father, Cadell, about 909. He united their inheritance in 920, and acquired Gwynedd after the death of Idwal Foel in 942. He married
1 CONC  Elen, daughter of Llywarch of Dyfed, and on Llywarch's death in 904 he took over the southern kingdom. In the perspective of the Dark Ages he was a powerful prince, and it may be that later generations borrowed his personal autho
1 CONC rity to buttress their own power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like his grandfather, Rhodri the Great , Hywel was given an epithet by a later generation. He became known as Hywel Dda (Hywel the Good), although it would be wrong to consider that goodness to be innocent and unblemished. In the 
1 CONC age of Hywel, the essential attribute of a state builder was ruthlessness, an attribute which Hywel possessed, if it is true that it was he who ordered the killing of Llywarch of Dyfed, as some have claimed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although contemporary evidence is lacking, there is no reason to reject the tradition that Hywel was responsible for some of the consolidation of the Laws of Wales. Among Hywel's contemporaries there were rulers who won fame as la
1 CONC w-givers. The law was Hywel's law, cyfraith Hywel; his name gave to the law an authority comparable with that given to the laws of Mercia by King Offa or the laws of Wessex (and a larger area of England) by King Alfred. He almost 
1 CONC certainly knew of them; he was a regular visitor to the English court and in 928, when in the flower of his manhood, he went on pilgrimage to Rome. In later centuries it was claimed that he took copies of his laws to Rome, where t
1 CONC hey were blessed by the Pope. Tradition also provided details of the circumstances under which the laws were compiled and promulgated.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was probably the need to give cohesion to his different territories that prompted Hywel to codify the law. He was also successful in defending his territories, for there is no record that they were ravaged by the Vikings during
1 CONC  his reign. Neither were they attacked by the English. Hywel adhered to the close relationship with England initiated by his father-in-law, Llywarch of Dyfed, yet it is unlikely that he relished the diminution in status and the he
1 CONC avy demands for tribute which resulted from his association with the kingdom of England. He recognized the facts of power - the power which in his lifetime extinguished the Brythonic kingdom of Cornwall and which brought about the
1 CONC  death of his cousin, Idwal of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hywel's creation of the kingdom of Deheubarth, survived his death. In 950 it passed to his son Owain. Gwynedd and Powys returned to the line of Idwal ap Anarawd while Glamorgan continued to be subject to its own kings. Although th
1 CONC e union between Gwynedd, Powys and Deheubarth was broken, Wales had only three kingdoms after 950, compared with over twice that number two centuries earlier.
0 @N6000000000715084498@ NOTE King of Deheubarth (Dyfed and Seisyllwg) (910-950)
1 CONT King of Gwynedd (942?-950)
1 CONT King of Powys (942?-950)
1 CONT
1 CONT Following is all from: http://www.britannia.com/history/ebk/ebktime2.html
1 CONT c.910  - Death of King Cadell of Seisyllwg. His son, King Hywel Dda unites Seisyllwg and Dyfed to form the Kingdom of Deheubarth.
1 CONT 918  - King Idwal Foel of Gwynedd and King Hywel Dda and Prince Clydog of Deheubarth submit to the overlordship of King Edward the Elder of England. The Vikings raid Anglesey.
1 CONT 927 - Kings Hywel Dda of Deheubarth and Owain of Glywysing & Gwent submit to the overlordship of King Athelstan of England at Hereford. The border between England and Wales is set at the River Wye.
1 CONT 928 - King Hywel Dda of Deheubarth, Gwynedd & Powys begins the codification of Welsh customary law.
1 CONT 929 - King Hywel Dda of Deheubarth goes on a pilgrimage to Rome.
1 CONT 934  - King Tewdr of Brycheiniog attends the court of King Athelstan of England and signs English Land Charters. Kings Hywel Dda of Deheubarth, Idwal Foel of Gwynedd and Morgan Mwynfawr of Morgannwg are compelled to accompany Athe
1 CONC lstan on his campaign against King Constantine II of the Scots.
1 CONT 942 - Kings Idwal Foel of Gwynedd openly rebels against the overlordship of the English monarchy. Both he and Llewelyn of Powys are killed fighting English armies.
1 CONT 949 - King Hywel Dda of Deheubarth, Gwynedd & Powys attends the court of King Eadred of England.
