0 @N6000000002248215018@ NOTE Hywel Dda (the Good) ap Cadell, Prince of Deheubarth, acquired the Kingdom of Dyfed on marrying Elen, the daughter of the King of Dyfed. He may have ordered the killing of his brother-in-law, Llywarch of Dyfed in order to secure t
1 CONC he kingship of Dyfed for himself (John Davies: A History of Wales, London, 1993, pg. 87). He is remembered as "Hywel the Good" and was responsible for sponsoring a compilation of Welsh Law, making St. Davids in Dyfed the ecclesias
1 CONC tical centre of Wales, and for issuing the first Royal coinage of Wales. However, there is no evidence that coinage was used in any scale in Wales before the time of Hywel's grandson Maredudd ap Owain. 
1 CONT Disorder reigned after Hywel's death, with Viking raids and English incursions spreading havoc, which the rivalries of competing petty kings and princes did nothing to mitigate. No fewer than 35 violent deaths of rulers are record
1 CONC ed in the Brut y Tywsogion (Chronicle of the Princes) between 950 and the Norman Conquest. 
1 CONT Died in 950. 
1 CONT Hywel married Elen, the daughter of the King of Dyfed, and they had the following children: 
1 CONT Rhodri ap Hywel Dda 
1 CONT Edwin ap Hywel Dda 
1 CONT Owain ap Hywel Dha, King of Deheubarth
