1 CONT Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (c. 1000 – August 5, 1063) was the ruler of all Wales from 1055 until his death, one of very few able to make this boast. He was the son of Llywelyn ap Seisyll and a descendant of Rhodri the Great. On the deat
1 CONC h of Iago ab Idwal ap Meurig in 1039, Gruffydd unexpectedly seized control of Gwynedd, successfully waged war with Mercia, then attacked the neighbouring principality of Deheubarth. By 1044, he had conquered Deheubarth, but lost i
1 CONC t again in 1047. Allying himself with the Mercians, he proceeded to gain considerable ground along what is now the English border. In 1055, he invaded Herefordshire and defeated its earl, Ralph the Timid, and the Anglo-Norman chiv
1 CONC alry near Hereford on October 24. He then sacked the city and destroyed its Norman castle. Deheubarth came back within his power in the same year, and he claimed sovereignty over the whole of Wales - a claim which was recognised b
1 CONC y the English. Gruffydd reached an agreement with Edward the Confessor, but was less successful in holding off the incursions of Harold Godwinson. His own men deserted him, and he was murdered by them and his territory broken up a
1 CONC mong several successors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Iago ab Idwal ap Meurig Kings of Gwynedd Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bleddyn ap Cynfyn 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Iago ab Idwal ap Meurig Kings of Powys Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bleddyn ap Cynfyn 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Meurig ap Hywel Kings of Gwent Succeeded by:
