0 @N6000000002187757565@ NOTE Ealdgyth Swan-neck
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Ealdgyth Swan-neck, also known as Edith the Fair, was the mistress (or common-law wife) of King Harold II of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT She bore him several illegitimate children and was the one who located his body after the Battle of Hastings.
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Battle of Hastings
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Battle of Hastings 
1 CONT Part of the Norman Conquest 
1 CONT
1 CONT A section of the Bayeux Tapestry, chronicling the English/Norman battle in 1066 which led to the Norman Conquest. 
1 CONT Date: October 14, 1066 
1 CONT Location: Hastings, England 
1 CONT Result: Decisive Norman victory 
1 CONT Casus belli:  
1 CONT Territory changes:  
1 CONT
1 CONT Combatants 
1 CONT Normans Anglo-Saxon English 
1 CONT Commanders 
1 CONT William of Normandy, Bishop Odo Harold Godwinson† 
1 CONT Strength 
1 CONT 7,000-8,000 7,000-8,000 
1 CONT Casualties 
1 CONT Unknown, thought to be around 2000 killed and wounded Unknown, but significantly more than the Normans 
1 CONT The Battle of Hastings was the most decisive Norman victory in the Norman conquest of England in 1066.
1 CONT
