0 @N6000000003299316075@ NOTE One of the more intriguing facts revealed by the Domesday Book is that William Fitz Osbern, the earl of Hereford between the years 1067 and 1071, made a series of grants to a certain "Mariadoth" styled as rex or 'king'.
1 CONT
1 CONT This "Mariadoth" can be no other than Maredudd ab Owain ab Edwin, king of Deheubarth which raises the question as to why a Norman earl was handing out land to a Welsh king particularly one that he had according to Orderic Vitalis,
1 CONC  just defeated in battle in the previous year.
0 @N6000000003796432052@ NOTE ==================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT FAIRBAIRN Genealogy
1 CONT URL:  http://home.austarnet.com.au/dfgoonan/FAIRBAIRNpg.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT  Fairbairn was supposed to derive from Fair (white, pale, handsome) and bairn (child), but this seems unlikely as surnames weren't granted to children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another more likely theory has the surname derive from a Viking Prince named Siward “the Fair” BEORN (bear). He was also known as Siward Fairbairn “of the Strong Arm”. His father was Siward "Digira" whose father was Hringo, King o
1 CONC f Upland who was also known as Earl Beorn (bear). The tales about Siward and his father were called “Fay Bairn” (fairy bear) and relate to Nordic legends.
1 CONT
1 CONT Siward "Digira" lived in England from about 995 until his death in 1056. King Edward “the Confessor” conferred the title of Earl of Northumbria (Northumberland) on the giant man.
1 CONT
1 CONT An enduring story tells of Siward’s encounter with Tostig, Earl of Huntington. Tostig apparently insulted Siward by throwing dirt on him when they met on a bridge. Siward had just left a meeting with King Edward and took no offenc
1 CONC e. They met again on the same bridge and this time Siward decapitated Tostig and carried his head to King Edward. Edward was so impressed that he awarded Siward the Earldom of Huntington in addition to Northumbria. (If Siward kill
1 CONC ed Tostig prior to 1056, then it would discredit sources that say that Tostig was defeated by his brother Harold at the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Siward’s sister married Duncan, King of Scots. Siward’s eldest son was Osberne “Bulax” who was killed in battle with “Macbeth” on 27th July 1054. Osberne was said to have married the daughter of Lady Godiva. Siward’s second son Wa
1 CONC ltheof married Juditha, a niece of William “the Conqueror” who helped Waltheof regain the title of Earl of Northumbria in 1069. Their friendship didn’t last long though as King William had Waltheof beheaded outside Westminster in 
1 CONC 1076.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osberne “Bulax” had two sons named Siward Barn “the Red” and Siward Barn “the White” (Fairbarn). Siward “the White” became a refugee and fled to Scotland with Edgar “the Atheling” where he was greeted kindly by his cousin Malcolm 
1 CONC III, King of Scots. Siward and Malcolm III set about driving the Normans out of Northumbria. During battle Malcolm III had his horse killed under him and was partially crippled. Siward Fairbairn fought his way to the armoured King
1 CONC , took him under his arm and fought his way to safety. For this bravery he was knighted Sir Armstrange and given lands and a castle on the Scottish border. It is from this Siward Fairbairn that the Armstrongs of Mangerton claim de
1 CONC cent.
