1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernard de Neufmarche[1] (born Le Neuf-Marche-en-Lions, c.1050-?) was a Norman marcher lord active in Wales. He was from the Aufay family, son of Geoffroy de Neufmarche who was at one point close to William, Duke of Normandy.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT He had lands in Herefordshire, presumed to be dowry from his marriage to Agnes, daughter of Osbern Fitz-Richard, as well as lands previously held by Gilbert Fitz-Turold and Alfred of Marlborough[3].
1 CONT
1 CONT He carried out the conquest of Brycheiniog, in a campaign from 1088[4], defeating in 1093 Rhys ap Tewdwr, prince of Deheubarth.[5] He then built Brecon Castle. He also founded a priory.[6], dependent on Battle Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT The operations in Wales started shortly after the end of the rebellion of 1088, in which Bernard was active against the English king, William Rufus.[7]
