0 @N6000000001783169785@ NOTE Rhys ap Gruffydd
1 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=a23145ad-1d5b-44ad-ab34-3d755d753a78&tid=929940&pid=-2023991699
1 CONT
1 CONT Dinefwr Castle-Wales
1 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=3e92a6aa-469a-45f5-ab14-8192af7382d1&tid=929940&pid=-2023991699
0 @N6000000002577100063@ NOTE Prince of South Wales.
0 @N6000000006086102874@ NOTE LABEL1:Prince of South Wales
0 @N6000000004533239649@ NOTE Notes for Rhys II ap Gruffydd:
1 CONT
1 CONT The Lord Rhys Deheubarth 1155-1197, Rhys 'Fychan' ap Gruffydd
1 CONT
1 CONT Had nine children with Gwenllian, and at least 9 illegitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the eighteen years since the death of Gruffydd ap Rhys, his sonshad progressively reclaimed and rebuilt much of the old kingdom ofDeheubarth, recovering lands from the Normans and from the rivalkingdom of Gwynedd. Rhys had been
1 CONC  only four when his father died, butby the age of thirteen he was involved with his brothers in variousengagements so that he was already battle hardened when the earlydeath of his brother Maredudd catapulted Rhys into power in 11
1 CONC 55.
1 CONT
1 CONT He came to power at the same time as Henry II in England, who felt heneeded to repair the damage caused in both England and Wales duringthe last twenty years. He soon tested Welsh resistance by a campaigninto North Wales in 1157 t
1 CONC hat saw the submission of Owain Gwynedd.Rhys held out for a year with a display of bravado, but when Henry putit to the test in 1158, Rhys was forced to submit. His parley withHenry was costly. Rhys was forced to hand back all of 
1 CONC the capturedcastles to the Norman families and was left in control of only CantrefMawr and a few outlying territories. Deheubarth was again splitasunder as if the last twenty years had been for nothing. Perhaps evenmore ignominiou
1 CONC s was that Rhys agreed to drop the title of king,demonstrating his total submission to Henry's authority. Even after hewas always known as Yr Arglwydd Rhys -- 'The Lord Rhys'. Such adecision was not easily accepted. Rhys's nephew,
1 CONC  Einion ap Anarawd,rebelled and slew the garrison at Castell Hywel. Henry returned with ashow of force and Rhys had to capitulate. However, soon after Henryreturned to France, Rhys attacked several castles in Dyfed, mostnotably Ca
1 CONC rmarthen, which resulted in a major Norman force beingdispatched by Henry's illegitimate son, Reginald, earl of Cornwall.The scale of opposition drove Rhys back into his stronghold of CantrefMawr from which the combined forces of 
1 CONC five Norman earls, plus theassistance of the rulers of Gwynedd, were unable to displace him. Atruce was finally called and, for the next three years, Rhys remainedquiet.
1 CONT
1 CONT Then, in 1162, Rhys begain again on a series of raids, taking thecastle at Llandovery. When Henry returned to England in early 1163 hisfirst mission was to penetrate into South Wales. Henry caught up withRhys in Ceredigion. Quite 
1 CONC how or why Rhys submitted is not clear, butthere was no struggle, and Henry took Rhys away as a prisoner. It is ademonstration of Henry's belief in his own strength and authoritythat, after a few months, he released Rhys after he 
1 CONC had paid homage toHenry at Woodstock in July, along with Malcolm IV or Scotland andOwain Gwynedd. Rhys remained obdurate. While he had been prisoner, hisnephew Einion had been murdered. The murderer was being sheltered byRoger de 
1 CONC Clare, earl of Hertford. Once back in Wales, Rhys immediatelyled an army in Ceredigion destroying any Norman stronghold he found ashe progressed. This was the last straw for Henry. He planned to raisea major army and subdue Wales 
1 CONC once and for all. The threat of this atlast brought the various factions in Wales together into a majoruprising. At the same time Henry had his own domestic problems overThomas Becket, and his campaign of 1165 failed dramatically.
1 CONC  Rhysseized this as an opportunity to regain all of his lost lands andwithin a year had rebuilt much of Deheubarth. He never was able toregain much of the land in Dyfed or along the southern coast, but mostof Ceredigion and northe
1 CONC rn Ystrad Tywi was under his direct rule, andmany surrounding territories were ruled by client kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT For a period the Norman lords despaired, many of them turning theirattention to Ireland to expand their power. This further annoyed Henrywhose next expedition to Wales in 1170 was not to subjugate the Welshbut to bring his Norman 
1 CONC lords back to heel. It seems that during the1160s both sides had learned their lessons. In 1171 and 1172 Henry andRhys held a series of meetings out of which Rhys emerged as Henry'srecognized "justiciar" in Deheubarth, a role whic
1 CONC h demonstrated thatRhys acted with the authority of Henry and had power over theadministration of th elaw, and that meant power over the lesser rulersin South Wales. Rhys actually styled himself "Prince of South Wales".It was a tu
1 CONC rning point in Welsh affairs. From then on, for the secondhalf of his reign, Rhys put aside his rebellious past and assumed therole of a governor, negotiator, peace-maker, and king's man. He evensent a force to support Henry durin
1 CONC g the revolt of his sons in 1173.Rhys saw the benefits of Norman England and even began to imitate theNorman way of life and administration. But he did not neglect Welshculture. He rebuilt the castle at Cardigan and, upon its comp
1 CONC letion in1176, inaugurated there the first official eisteddfod. He also foundedor rebuilt a number of abbeys, of which Strata Florida was the moststriking example.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unfortunately most of Rhys's achievements were built on shiftingsands. They relied primarily on his own strength and stamina and onthe relationship that he developed with Henry II. After Henry's deathin 1189, Rhys found it difficu
1 CONC lt to receive any cooperation fromRichard's government, and the protection he was allowed from theambitions of the marcher lords also evaporated. Tensions again beganto mount, intensified by unrest between Rhys's own sons. The res
1 CONC ultwas that Rhys once again had to take to arms to defend himself. Themain Anglo-Norman opponent was William de Breos, lord of Radnor andBuilth. Initially Rhys showed his strength, but by 1194 thein-fighting within the family bega
1 CONC n to take its toll. The maintrouble-maker was one of Rhys's younger sons, Maelgwyn, who wasimprisoned and later handed over to Breos by his own brother Gruffyd.When Rhys secured Maelgwyn's release, it only led to more in-fightingb
1 CONC etween Maelgwyn and Gruffydd. Rhys was caught in the middle and in1194 found himself captured and imprisoned by Maelgwyn. He was soonreleased, but found that other sons, Rhys Grug and Maredudd, hadcaptured Deheubarth's royal stron
1 CONC ghold at Dinefwr. Rhys was incensed.Showing something of his old strength and verve he regained hiscastles and, in 1196, led a major campaign against the Normans. Duringthe last few years William de Breos had succeeded in regainin
1 CONC g some ofhis old territories, but in a remarkable campaign Rhys stormed andcaptured many of de Breos's castles and razed Norman towns to theground. Rhys went out in a blaze of glory. He died the followingspring. He was the last gr
1 CONC eat ruler of southern Wales, and certainlyone of Wales's greatest rulers. From out of almost nothing he rebuiltDeheubarth, and held it against overwhelming odds for over fortyyears. Unfortunately, as was ever the case with a stron
1 CONC g king whoruled for so long, the rivalry between his sons soon pulled thekingdom apart.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mike Ashley, 'British Monarchs'
0 @N6000000002795135270@ NOTE Rhys ap Gruffydd
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffydd  
1 CONT Reign 1155 - 28 April 1197 
1 CONT Predecessor Maredudd ap Gruffydd 
1 CONT Successor Gruffydd ap Rhys II 
1 CONT Spouse Gwenllian ferch Madog 
1 CONT
1 CONT Royal House Dinefwr 
1 CONT Father Gruffydd ap Rhys 
1 CONT Mother Gwenllian ferch Gruffydd 
1 CONT Born 1132 
1 CONT Died 28 April 1197 
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffydd (1132–28 April 1197) was the ruler of the kingdom of Deheubarth in south Wales. He is commonly known as The Lord Rhys, in Welsh Yr Arglwydd Rhys, but this title may not have been used in his lifetime.[1] He usuall
1 CONC y used the title "Prince of Deheubarth" or "Prince of South Wales", but two documents have been preserved in which he uses the title "Prince of Wales".[2] Rhys was one of the most successful and powerful Welsh princes, and after t
1 CONC he death of Owain Gwynedd of Gwynedd in 1170 was the dominant power in Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys's grandfather, Rhys ap Tewdwr, was king of Deheubarth, and was killed in 1093 while resisting Norman encroachment. Following his death, most of Deheubarth was taken over by the Normans. Rhys's father, Gruffydd ap Rhys, was ev
1 CONC entually able to become ruler of a small portion, and more territory was won back by Rhys's older brothers after Gruffydd's death. Rhys became ruler of Deheubarth in 1155. He was forced to submit to King Henry II of England in 115
1 CONC 8. Henry invaded Deheubarth in 1163, stripped Rhys of all his lands and took him prisoner. A few weeks later he was released and given back a small part of his holdings. Rhys made an alliance with Owain Gwynedd and after the failu
1 CONC re of another invasion of Wales by Henry in 1165 was able to win back most of his lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1171 Rhys made peace with King Henry and was confirmed in possession of his recent conquests as well as being named Justiciar of South Wales. He maintained good relations with King Henry until the latter's death in 1189. Follow
1 CONC ing Henry's death Rhys revolted against Richard I and attacked the Norman lordships surrounding his territory, capturing a number of castles. In his later years Rhys had trouble keeping control of his sons, particularly Maelgwn an
1 CONC d Gruffydd, who maintained a feud with each other. Rhys launched his last campaign against the Normans in 1196 and captured a number of castles. The following year he died unexpectedly and was buried in St David's Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT Genealogy and early life
1 CONT Rhys was the second son of Gruffydd ap Rhys, ruler of part of Deheubarth, and Gwenllian ferch Gruffydd, daughter of Gruffydd ap Cynan, king of Gwynedd. His elder brother was Maredudd ap Gruffydd, and there were two younger brother
1 CONC s, Morgan and Maelgwn.[3] He also had two older half-brothers, Anarawd and Cadell, and at least two sisters, Gwladus and Nest.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Deheubarth was one of the traditional kingdoms of Wales, shown here as they were in 1093 when Rhys ap Tewdwr died.His grandfather, Rhys ap Tewdwr, had been king of all Deheubarth until his death in 1093. Rhys ap Tewdwr was killed
1 CONC  in Brycheiniog while resisting Norman encroachment, and most of his kingdom was taken over by Norman lords. Gruffydd ap Rhys was forced to flee to Ireland.[4] He later returned to Deheubarth and ruled a portion of the kingdom, bu
1 CONC t was forced to flee to Ireland again in 1127. When Rhys was born in 1132, his father held only the commote of Caeo in Cantref Mawr.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of King Henry I of England and the ensuing rivalry between Stephen and Matilda gave the Welsh the opportunity to rise against the Normans. A revolt spread through south Wales in 1136, and Gruffydd ap Rhys, aided by his t
1 CONC wo eldest sons, Anarawd and Cadell, defeated the Normans in a battle near Loughor, killing over five hundred. After driving Walter de Clifford out of Cantref Bychan, Gruffydd set off to Gwynedd to enlist the help of his father-in-
1 CONC law, Gruffydd ap Cynan.[6] In the absence of her husband, Gwenllian led an army against the Norman lordship of Cydweli (Kidwelly), taking along her two youngest sons, Morgan and Maelgwn. She was defeated and killed by an army comm
1 CONC anded by Maurice de Londres. Morgan was also killed and Maelgwn captured.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT Gruffydd formed an alliance with Gwynedd, and later in 1136 the sons of Gruffydd ap Cynan, Owain Gwynedd and Cadwaladr ap Gruffydd, led an army to Ceredigion. Their combined forces won a decisive victory over the Normans at the Ba
1 CONC ttle of Crug Mawr. Ceredigion was reclaimed from the Normans, but was annexed by Gwynedd as the senior partner in the alliance. Gruffydd ap Rhys continued his campaign against the Normans in 1137, but died later that year. The lea
1 CONC dership of the family now passed to Rhys's half-brother Anarawd ap Gruffydd. In 1143, when Rhys was eleven, Anarawd was murdered by the bodyguard of Cadwaladr ap Gruffydd, brother of Owain Gwynedd, king of Gwynedd. Owain punished 
1 CONC Cadwaladr by depriving him of his lands in Ceredigion.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT First battles (1146–1155)
1 CONT  Rhys gained his first recorded military experience at the age of fourteen when he participated in the storming of Llansteffan Castle in 1146. Photo by Rogue Soul.Anarawd's brother, Cadell ap Gruffydd, now took over as head of the
1 CONC  family. Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Pembroke, rebuilt Carmarthen castle in 1145 then began a campaign to reclaim Ceredigion. He built a castle in the commote of Mabudryd, but Cadell, aided by Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd who held Ceredig
1 CONC ion for Gwynedd, destroyed it in 1146. Rhys appears in the annals for the first time in 1146, fighting alongside his brothers Cadell and Maredudd in the capture by assault of Llansteffan Castle.[9] This was followed by the capture
1 CONC  of Wiston in 1147, Carmarthen in 1150 and Loughor in 1151. In 1151 Cadell was attacked while out hunting by a group of Norman and Flemish knights from Tenby, and left for dead. He survived, but suffered injuries which left him un
1 CONC able to play an active role, and in 1153 he left on a pilgrimage to Rome.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT Maredudd became ruler of Deheubarth and continued a campaign, begun in 1150, aimed at recovering Ceredigion, which had been held by Gwynedd since 1136. Maredudd and Rhys were able to drive Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd from Ceredigion by
1 CONC  1153. The same year Rhys is recorded as an independent commander for the first time, leading an army to capture the Norman castle of St Clears.[11]. Maredudd and Rhys also destroyed the castles at Tenby and Aberafan that year. Ma
1 CONC redudd died in 1155 at the age of twenty-five and left Rhys as ruler of Deheubarth. Around this time he married Gwenllian ferch Madog, daughter of Madog ap Maredudd, prince of Powys.[12]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Early reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Loss of territory (1155–1163)
1 CONT Shortly after becoming ruler of Deheubarth, Rhys heard rumours that Owain Gwynedd was planning to invade Ceredigion in order to reclaim it for Gwynedd. Rhys responded by building a castle at Aberdyfi in 1156.[13] The threatened in
1 CONC vasion did not take place, and Turvey suggests that Owain's intention may have been to test the resolve of the new ruler.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT King Stephen had died in October 1154, bringing to an end the long dispute with the Empress Matilda which had helped Anarawd, Cadell and Maredudd to extend their rule in Deheubarth. With disunity within the realm no longer a probl
1 CONC em, the new king of England, Henry II, soon turned his attention to Wales. He began with an invasion of Gwynedd in 1157. This invasion was not entirely successful, but Owain Gwynedd was induced to seek terms and to give up some te
1 CONC rritory in the north-east of Wales.[15]
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1158 King Henry stripped Rhys of all his territories apart from Cantref Mawr. The areas in Deheubarth held by various Norman lords are shown in grey.The following year, Henry prepared an invasion of Deheubarth. Rhys made plans
1 CONC  to resist, but was persuaded by his council to meet the king to discuss peace terms. The terms were much harsher than those offered to Owain: Rhys was stripped of all his possessions apart from Cantref Mawr, though he was promise
1 CONC d one other cantref. The other territories were returned to their Norman lords.[16]
1 CONT
1 CONT Among the Normans who returned to their holdings was Walter de Clifford, who reclaimed Cantref Bychan, then invaded Rhys's lands in Cantref Mawr. An appeal to the king produced no response, and Rhys resorted to arms, first capturi
1 CONC ng Clifford's castle at Llandovery then seizing Ceredigion. King Henry responded by preparing another invasion, and Rhys submitted without resistance. He was obliged to give hostages, probably including his son Hywel.[17]
1 CONT
1 CONT The king was absent in France in 1159, and Rhys took the opportunity to attack Dyfed and then to lay siege to Carmarthen, which was saved by a relief force led by Earl Reginald of Cornwall. Rhys retreated to Cantref Mawr, where an
1 CONC  army led by five earls, the Earls of Cornwall, Gloucester, Hertford, Pembroke and Salisbury, marched against him. The earls were assisted by Cadwaladr, brother of Owain Gwynedd, and Owain's sons, Hywel and Cynan. However they wer
1 CONC e forced to withdraw and a truce was arranged.[18] In 1162, Rhys again attempted to recover some of his lost lands, and captured Llandovery castle. The following year Henry II returned to England after an absence of four years and
1 CONC  prepared for another invasion of Deheubarth. Rhys met the king to discuss terms and was obliged to give more hostages, including another son, Maredudd. He was then seized and taken to England as a prisoner.[19] Henry appears to h
1 CONC ave been uncertain what to do with Rhys, but after a few weeks decided to free him and allow him to rule Cantref Mawr. Rhys was summoned to appear before Henry at Woodstock to do homage together with Owain Gwynedd and Malcolm IV o
1 CONC f Scotland.[20]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Welsh uprising (1164–1170)
1 CONT In 1164 all the Welsh princes united in an uprising. Warren suggests that when Rhys and Owain were obliged to do homage to Henry in 1163 they were forced to accept a status of dependent vassalage instead of their previous client s
1 CONC tatus, and that this led to the revolt.[21] Rhys had other reasons for rebellion, for he had returned to Deheubarth from England to find that the neighbouring Norman lords were threatening Cantref Mawr. His nephew, Einion ab Anara
1 CONC wd, who was the captain of his bodyguard, had been murdered at the instigation of Roger de Clare, Earl of Hertford. The murderer had been given the protection of the Clares in Ceredigion.[22] Rhys first appealed to the king to int
1 CONC ercede, then when this failed invaded Ceredigion and recaptured all of it apart from the town and castle of Cardigan. The Welsh revolt led to another invasion of Wales by King Henry in 1165. Henry attacked Gwynedd first, but inste
1 CONC ad of following the usual invasion route along the north coast he attacked from the south, following a route over the Berwyn hills. He was met by the united forces of the Welsh princes, led by Owain Gwynedd and including Rhys. Acc
1 CONC ording to Brut y Tywysogion:
1 CONT
1 CONT  The arms of Rhys ap Gruffydd feature three ravens... [King Henry] gathered an innumerable host of the selected warriors of England and Normandy and Flanders and Gascony and Anjou ... and against him came Owain and Cadwaladr the s
1 CONC ons of Gruffydd with all the host of Gwynedd, and Rhys ap Gruffydd with all the host of Deheubarth and Iorwerth the Red son of Maredudd and the sons of Madog ap Maredudd with all the host of Powys.[23]
1 CONT
1 CONT Torrential rain forced Henry's army to retreat in disorder without fighting a major battle, and Henry vented his spleen on the hostages, having Rhys's son Maredudd blinded. Rhys's other son, Hywel, was not among the victims. Rhys 
1 CONC returned to Deheubarth where he captured and burned Cardigan Castle. He allowed the garrison to depart, but held the castellan, Robert Fitz-Stephen, as a prisoner. Shortly afterwards Rhys captured Cilgerran castle.[24]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1167 he joined Owain Gwynedd in an attack on Owain Cyfeiliog of southern Powys, and spent three weeks helping Owain besiege the Norman castle of Rhuddlan.[25] In 1168 he attacked the Normans at Builth, destroying its castle. Rh
1 CONC ys benefited from the Norman invasion of Ireland in 1169 and 1170, which was largely led by the Cambro-Norman lords of south Wales. In 1167 the King of Leinster, Diarmait Mac Murchada, who had been driven out of his kingdom, had a
1 CONC sked Rhys to release Robert Fitz-Stephen from captivity to take part in an expedition to Ireland. Rhys did not oblige at the time, but released him the following year and in 1169 Fitz-Stephen led the vanguard of a Norman army whic
1 CONC h landed in Wexford. The leader of the Norman forces, Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, known as "Strongbow", followed in 1170. According to Warren:
1 CONT
1 CONT They were prompted to go by a growing suspicion that King Henry did not intend to renew his offensive against the Welsh, but was instead seeking an accommodation with the Welsh leaders.[26]
1 CONT
1 CONT The departure of the Norman lords enabled Rhys to strengthen his position, and the death of Owain Gwynedd in late 1170 left him as the acknowledged leader of the Welsh princes.[27]
1 CONT
1 CONT Later reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Peace with King Henry (1171–1188)
1 CONT In 1171 King Henry II arrived in England from France, on his way to Ireland. Henry wished to ensure that Richard de Clare, who had married Diarmait's daughter and become heir to Leinster, did not establish an independent Norman ki
1 CONC ngdom in Ireland. [28] His decision to try a different approach in his dealings with the Welsh was influenced by the events in Ireland, although Warren suggests that "it seems likely that Henry began rethinking his attitude to the
1 CONC  Welsh soon after the débâcle of 1165".[29] Henry now wished to make peace with Rhys, who came to Newnham to meet him. Rhys was to pay a tribute of 300 horses and 4,000 head of cattle, but was confirmed in possession of all the la
1 CONC nds he had taken from Norman lords, including the Clares. They met again in October that year at Pembroke as Henry waited to cross to Ireland. Rhys had collected 86 of the 300 horses, but Henry agreed to take only 36 of them and r
1 CONC emitted the remainder of the tribute until after his return from Ireland. Rhys's son, Hywel, who had been held as a hostage for many years, was returned to him. Henry and Rhys met once more at Laugharne as Henry returned from Irel
1 CONC and in 1172, and shortly afterwards Henry appointed Rhys "justice on his behalf in all Deheubarth".[30] According to A.D. Carr:
1 CONT
1 CONT This meant the delegation to him of any authority which the king might have claimed over his fellow Welsh rulers; it might also have involved some authority over the king's Anglo-Norman subjects ... Rhys was more than a native Wel
1 CONC sh ruler; he was one of the great feudatories of the Angevin empire.[31]
1 CONT
1 CONT  Dinefwr Castle was the chief seat of the Dinefwr dynasty. The earliest surviving part of the present castle may have been built by Rhys or by his son, Rhys Gryg.[32]The agreement between Henry and Rhys was to last until Henry's d
1 CONC eath in 1189. When Henry's sons rebelled against him in 1173 Rhys sent his son Hywel Sais to Normandy to aid the king, then in 1174 personally led an army to Tutbury in Staffordshire to assist at the siege of the stronghold of the
1 CONC  rebel Earl William de Ferrers.[33] When Rhys returned to Wales after the fall of Tutbury, he left a thousand men with the king for service in Normandy. King Henry held a council at Gloucester in 1175 which was attended by a large
1 CONC  gathering of Welsh princes, led by Rhys. It appears to have concluded with the swearing of a mutual assistance pact for the preservation of peace and order in Wales.[34] In 1177 Rhys and Dafydd ab Owain, who had emerged as the ma
1 CONC in power in Gwynedd, swore fealty and liege homage to Henry at a council held at Oxford.[35] At this council the king gave Meirionnydd, part of the kingdom of Gwynedd, to Rhys. There was some fighting in Meirionnydd the following 
1 CONC year, but Rhys apparently made no serious attempt to annex it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys built a number of stone castles, starting with Cardigan castle, which was the earliest recorded native-built stone castle in Wales.[36] He held a festival of poetry and song at his court at Cardigan over Christmas 1176. This 
1 CONC is generally regarded as the first recorded Eisteddfod.[37] The festival was announced a year in advance throughout Wales and in England, Scotland, Ireland and possibly France. Two chairs were awarded as prizes, one for the best p
1 CONC oem and the other for the best musical performance. J. E. Caerwyn Williams suggests that this event may be an adaptation of the similar French puys.[38] R.R. Davies suggests that the texts of Welsh law, traditionally codified by H
1 CONC ywel Dda at Whitland, were first assembled in book form under the aegis of Rhys.[39]
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys founded two religious houses during this period. Talley Abbey was the first Premonstratensian abbey in Wales, while Llanllyr was a Cistercian nunnery, only the second nunnery to be founded in Wales and the first to prosper.[4
1 CONC 0] He became the patron of the abbeys of Whitland and Strata Florida and made large grants to both houses.[41] Giraldus Cambrensis, who was related to Rhys, gives an account of his meetings with Rhys in 1188 when Giraldus accompan
1 CONC ied Archbishop Baldwin around Wales to raise men for the Third Crusade. Some Welsh clerics were not happy about this visit, but Rhys was enthusiastic and gave the Archbishop a great deal of assistance. Giraldus says that Rhys deci
1 CONC ded to go on crusade himself and spent several weeks making preparations, but was eventually persuaded to change his mind by his wife Gwenllian, "by female artifices".[42]
1 CONT
1 CONT Final campaigns (1189–1196)
1 CONT Henry II died in 1189 and was succeeded by Richard I. Rhys considered that he was no longer bound by the agreement with King Henry and attacked the Norman lordships surrounding his territory. He ravaged Pembroke, Haverfordwest and
1 CONC  Gower and captured the castles of St. Clear's, Laugharne and Llansteffan. Richard's brother, Prince John (later King John), came to Wales in September and tried to make peace. He persuaded Rhys to raise the siege of Carmarthen an
1 CONC d accompany him to Oxford to meet Richard. Rhys arrived at Oxford to discover that Richard was not prepared to travel there to meet him, and hostilities continued.[43]
1 CONT
1 CONT  By 1196 Rhys ruled almost all of Deheubarth, as well as controlling much of the remainder of south Wales through client princes. The remaining Norman-held areas in Deheubarth are shown in grey.In his later years Rhys had trouble 
1 CONC keeping control of his sons, particularly Maelgwn and Gruffydd. In 1189 Gruffydd persuaded Rhys to imprison Maelgwn, and he was given into Gruffydd's keeping at Dinefwr. Gruffydd handed him over to his father-in-law, William de Br
1 CONC aose. Gruffydd is also said to have persuaded his father to annex the lordship of Cemais and its chief castle of Nevern, held by William fitzMartin, in 1191. This action was criticized by Giraldus Cambrensis, who describes Gruffyd
1 CONC d as "a cunning and artful man". William fitzMartin was married to Rhys's daughter Angharad, and, according to Giraldus, Rhys "had solemnly sworn, by the most precious relics, that his indemnity and security should be faithfully m
1 CONC aintained".[44] Rhys had also annexed the Norman lordships of Cydweli and Carnwyllion in 1190.[45] In 1192 Rhys secured Maelgwn's release, but by now Maelgwn and Gruffydd were bitter enemies. In 1194 Rhys was defeated in battle by
1 CONC  Maelgwn and Hywel, who imprisoned him in Nevern castle, though Hywel later released his father without Maelgwn's consent. Giraldus suggests that Rhys's incarceration in Nevern castle was divine vengeance for the dispossession of 
1 CONC William fitzMartin.[46] In 1195 two other sons, Rhys Gryg and Maredudd, seized Llanymddyfri and Dinefwr, and Rhys responded by imprisoning them.[47] Rhys launched his last campaign against the Normans in 1196. He captured a number
1 CONC  of castles, including Carmarthen, Colwyn, Radnor and Painscastle, and defeated an army led by Roger de Mortimer and Hugh de Sai near Radnor, with forty knights among the dead.[48] William de Braose offered terms, and Painscastle 
1 CONC was returned to him.[49]
1 CONT
1 CONT Death and aftermath (1197)
1 CONT In April 1197 Rhys died unexpectedly and was buried in St David's Cathedral. The chronicler of Brut y Tywysogion records for 1197:
1 CONT
1 CONT  Rhys was buried in St David's Cathedral, where an effigy said to be of him can still be seen.... there was a great pestilence throughout the island of Britain ... and that tempest killed innumerable people and many of the nobilit
1 CONC y and many princes, and spared none. That year, four days before May Day, died Rhys ap Gruffydd, Prince of Deheubarth and unconquered head of all Wales.[50]
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys died excommunicate, having quarreled with the Bishop of St. David's, Peter de Leia, over the theft of some of the bishop's horses some years previously. Before he could be buried in the cathedral, the bishop had his corpse sc
1 CONC ourged in posthumous penance.[51]
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys had nominated his eldest legitimate son, Gruffydd ap Rhys, as his successor, and soon after his father's death Gruffydd met the Justiciar, Archbishop Hubert Walter, on the border and was confirmed as heir. Maelgwn, the eldest
1 CONC  son but illegitimate, refused to accept this and was given military assistance by Gwenwynwyn ab Owain of Powys. Maelgwn took the town and castle of Aberystwyth and captured Gruffydd, whom he handed over to the custody of Gwenwynw
1 CONC yn. Gwenwynwyn later handed him over to the king, who imprisoned him at Corfe Castle.[52] Gruffydd was set free the following year and regained most of Ceredigion. In 1201 Gruffydd died, but this did not end the fighting between r
1 CONC ival claimants. In 1216 Llywelyn the Great of Gwynedd held a council at Aberdyfi where he allocated parts of Deheubarth to several sons and grandsons of Rhys.
1 CONT
1 CONT Character and historical assessment
1 CONT Giraldus Cambrensis frequently mentions Rhys in his writings and describes him as "a man of excellent wit and quick in repartee".[53] Gerald tells the story of a banquet at Hereford in 1186 where Rhys sat between two members of th
1 CONC e Clare family. What could have been a tense affair, since Rhys had seized lands in Ceredigion previously held by the Clare family, passed off with an exchange of courteous compliments, followed by some good-natured banter between
1 CONC  Rhys and Gerald about their family connections.[54] Rhys gave Gerald and Archbishop Baldwin a great deal of assistance when they visited Wales to raise troops for the crusade in 1188, and Gerald several times refers to his "kindn
1 CONC ess" and says that Rhys accompanied them all the way from Cardigan to the northern border of Ceredigion "with a liberality peculiarly praiseworthy in so illustrious a prince".[55]
1 CONT
1 CONT Another contemporary writer also wrote of Rhys if Roger Turvey is correct in stating that Walter Map's piece Of the King Appollonides deals with Rhys under a pseudonym.[56] Map was less favourably disposed towards Rhys, describing
1 CONC  him as "This king I have seen and know, and hate", but goes on to say "I would not have my hatred blacken his worth; it is not my wish ever to suppress any man's excellence through envy". He tells the following story about Apollo
1 CONC nides/Rhys:
1 CONT
1 CONT This same man gave provisions to his enemies when besieged and driven by risk of famine to capitulate; he wished them to be overcome by his own strength and not by want of bread; and though he deferred victory, he increased the re
1 CONC nown of it.[57]
1 CONT
1 CONT The historian R.R. Davies provides the following assessment of Rhys:
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys's career was indeed a remarkable one. Its very length was a tribute to his stamina and skill: he had occupied the stage of Welsh politics for over fifty years, from his first appearance in his early 'teens, at the capture of 
1 CONC Llansteffan castle in 1146, to his death in 1197. But it was his achievement which was astounding: he had reconstituted the kingdom of Deheubarth and made it the premier Welsh kingdom. For once, the poet's compliment was well-dese
1 CONC rved: Rhys had restored "the majesty of the South".[58]
1 CONT
1 CONT Davies also notes two flaws in Rhys's achievement. One was the personal nature of his accord with Henry II, which meant that it did not survive Henry's death. The other was his inability to control his sons and to force the other 
1 CONC sons to accept Gruffydd as his successor.[59]
1 CONT
1 CONT Children
1 CONT  Several of Rhys's children, including Gruffydd and Maelgwn, were buried at Strata Florida Abbey.Rhys had at least nine sons and eight daughters.[60] Confusingly, three of the sons were named Maredudd and two of the daughters were
1 CONC  named Gwenllian. Gruffydd ap Rhys (died 1201) was the eldest legitimate son and was nominated by Rhys as his successor. He married Matilda de Braose.[61] Maelgwn ap Rhys (died 1231), who was the eldest son but illegitimate, refus
1 CONC ed to accept Gruffydd as his father's successor. A bitter feud developed between the two, with several of Rhys's other sons becoming involved. Rhys Gryg (died 1233) married Joan de Clare [62] and eventually became the main power i
1 CONC n Deheubarth, but never ruled more than a portion of his father's realm and was a client prince of Llywelyn the Great of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hywel ap Rhys (died 1231) spent many years as a hostage at the court of Henry II and on his return became known as Hywel Sais (Hywel the Englishman). Maredudd ap Rhys (died 1239) was also given as a hostage, but was less fortunate
1 CONC  than Hywel. He was blinded by King Henry after the failure of the invasion of Wales in 1165, and became known as Maredudd Ddall (Maredudd the Blind). He ended his days as a monk at Whitland Abbey. Another Maredudd (died 1227) bec
1 CONC ame Archdeacon of Cardigan.[63]
1 CONT
1 CONT His daughter Gwenllian ferch Rhys married Rhodri ab Owain, prince of the western part of Gwynedd. Another Gwenllian (died 1236) married Ednyfed Fychan, seneschal of Gwynedd under Llywelyn the Great, and through her Rhys became an 
1 CONC ancestor of the Tudor dynasty. When Henry Tudor landed in Pembrokeshire in 1485 to make a bid for the throne, his descent from Rhys was one of the factors which enabled him to attract Welsh support.[64] Angharad ferch Rhys married
1 CONC  William fitz Martin, lord of Cemais, and another daughter married another member of the fitz Martin family. Other daughters married the Welsh rulers of Gwrtheyrnion and Elfael.[65]
1 CONT
