0 @N6000000003051219502@ NOTE Lord High Constable of England
0 @N6000000003615512212@ NOTE http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=swilliams10&id=I37071
0 @N6000000003949681524@ NOTE In 1141, during the struggle between the Empress Maud and Stephen I for the throne the former conferred the Earldom of Hereford on Miles of Gloucester, so-called from his father being hereditary Constable of the shire. Only two ye
1 CONC ars earlier the new Earl had supported Stephen, but it has been suggested that he went over to the Empress's in part because his overlord, the Earl of Gloucester, was one of Henry I's many bastard sons, hence Maud's half brother. 
1 CONC (Her generous gifts to Miles of land, houses, and castles, in addition to the Earldom, may have helped win him over.) [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT Killed by arrow, deer hunting.  Heriditary Lord High Constable  He succeeded his father as Sheriff of Gloucester. On King Stephen's accession he supported the King's party, however in 1139 he defected to Matilda, by whom he was cr
1 CONC eated Earl of Hereford, 1143. He was excommunicated by the Bishop of Hereford as the result of a land dispute.
0 @N6000000000337400218@ NOTE Source #1:  Weis, p. 165:  Complete Peerage Vol I, 22;  IV, 669 chart - created Earl of Hereford 1143
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:   George Edward Cokayne, "The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant or Dormant," New Edition, Revised and Much Enlarged, Edited by The Hon. Vicary Gibbs and H. A. D
1 CONC oubleday (London:  The St. Catherine Press, 1926), Vol IV, pp. 192-194.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3:  Jim Bradbury, "Stephen and Matilda:  The Civil War of 1139 - 53" (Alan Sutton Publishing, Lt, Stroud, Glucestershire, 1996), pp. 43-45;  73-75;  
1 CONT
1 CONT The famous supporter of Matilda's claim to the throne, Miles of Gloucester, Earl of Hereford.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first he seemed to support Stephen.  "He continued, as his father had done, as sheriff of Gloucester."  In 1138 he welcomed Stephen to Gloucester and gave him military assistance at Shrewsbury.  He had been, along with Robert o
1 CONC f Gloucester and Brian fitz Count, one of the men chosen by Henry I to escort Matilda to her first wedding.  "Miles was perhaps the most able military leader of the trio, all of whom were good commanders.  He was intelligent and d
1 CONC ecisive in his acts."  "All three of these supporters held considerable lands in Wales, which gave another strong motive for acting together."
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #4:  Donald Matthew, "King Stephen" (London:  Hambledon and London, 2002), p. 121
1 CONT
1 CONT He was killed in a hunting accident in December 1143.
0 @N6000000006348209350@ NOTE Miles is said to have died in a hunting accident.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry [Henry I], king of the English, to his archbishops, bishops, abbots, earls, and sheriffs, to all his barons, French and English, and to all his faithful men of all England and Wales, greeting. Know that to Miles of Glouceste
1 CONC r I have given and firmly granted Sibyl, daughter of Bernard de Neufmarché, with all the land of her father Bernard and of her mother after their death, or earlier — that is to say, during their lifetime, if they wish — and with t
1 CONC his marriage portion:[8] namely, Talgarth, the forest of Ystradyw, the castle of Hay,[9] and the whole land of Bryn as far as the boundaries of the land of Richard Fitz-Pons — that is to say, as far as Cantref Bychan and Cowarne, 
1 CONC a certain vill in England — also the fee and service of Roger de Baskerville, the fee and service of William Réveil, the fee and service of Robert de Turberville, and the fee and service of Picard. And I will and enjoin that all t
1 CONC he tenants of the aforesaid [land given as] marriage portion shall perform liege homage to [the said Miles] as to their lord, saving my fealty. And all the tenants of all the aforesaid land of Bernard shall likewise perform liege 
1 CONC homage to him as to their lord, saving my fealty and [saving the rights of] Bernard so long as he wishes to hold the land. And this I give and grant to [the said Miles] as the purchase of Bernard which he has given to me[10] — and
1 CONC  this at the request of the said Bernard and of his wife and of his barons.[11 ]And I will and firmly enjoin that [the said Miles] shall hold it as well and as honourably, as quietly and as freely, as ever Bernard best and most ho
1 CONC nourably held it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Witnesses: Roger, bishop of Salisbury; Robert, bishop of Lincoln; Ralph the Chancellor, Robert the King's Son, William de Tancarville, Nigel d'Aubigny, Payn Fitz-John, Geoffrey Fitz-Payn, Geoffrey de Clinton, Ralph Basset, William
1 CONC  Brito d'Aubigny. In the same year that the king took in marriage the daughter of the duke of Louvain, between Easter and Pentecost.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first Llanthony Priory, near Abergavenny in Wales, was seized by Welsh rebels in 1136. Llanthony Secunda (outside Gloucester) was founded in the same year to house the fugitive prior and about 20 canons. Miles of Gloucester su
1 CONC pplied the land, which was attached to his Gloucester Castle. He led the civil war against King Stephen. Miles and his descendants the Bohuns, who were the hereditary Constables of England, were buried for ten generations in the p
1 CONC riory church and chapter house.
