1 CONC rd de Kingsley, Chief Forester of Delamere. He died before 1259,leaving a son:
1 CONT William Gerrard of Kingsley, died before 1316 having by his wife, Margaret, his son and heir:
1 CONT presumed to have been of the house of Montalt, barons of Hawarden, as his descendents bore the arms of Montalt with a band for difference
1 CONT Generation No. 2 
1 CONT William Fitz Gerald, justice in Eyre Co. Chester, presumed to have been of the house of Montalt, barons of Hawarden, as his descendents bore the arms of Montalt with a band for difference. 
1 CONT The forests had an army of staff to look after them: SENESCHALS, JUSTICIARS, REGARDERS and VERDERES administered the forest laws (of these, only the verderers now survive as a titled office, and that only in one place - the New Fo
1 CONC rest, one of William the Conqueror's original first forests). The courts that heard offences were either COURTS OF EYRE (travelling courts to hear serious offences, from the Latin iterare, 'to travel', which also gives us words li
1 CONC ke 'ITERATION'), or of SWAINMOTE (a court held three times a year principally to control the pasturage of pigs in the forest; this word comes from Old English and literally means 'a meeting of swineherds' - The LARDINERS (sometime
1 CONC s important magnates) stored the carcasses of the deer; foresters cared for the animals and vegetation.
0 @N6000000004969829430@ NOTE William FitzWilliam FitzGerald did not inherit Carew Castle, etc. and did not go to Ireland. He left home (Carew Castle in Wales) and moved to Cheshire County where his mother’s family was from. Cheshire County adjoins Wales to th
1 CONC e northeast. There, he became the Justice of Eyre for the County of Chester where Kingsley was located. As near as I can tell, “Justice of Eyre” means that he was “sheriff” of the forests in the county where he was to prevent poac
1 CONC hing, etc. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Before William FitzWilliam FitzGerald moved to Cheshire County, he may have married Alina De Clare who was also from Pembroke, Wales. On the other hand, there is strong evidence that she married William FitzWilliam FitzGerald’s co
1 CONC usin William FitzMaurice FitzGerald. She was the daughter of Richard Strongbow FitzGilbert De Clare. This is the same Richard Strongbow, who with William’s uncles, were prominent in the Invasion of Ireland. There is a tremendous a
1 CONC mount of history on the De Clare ancestors. They were from France and before that they were Vikings. There are a few records that say that William also married Ela (Adela) Plantagenet, but I doubt it. 
0 @N6000000004969826898@ NOTE Griffin FitzWilliam de Carew also went to Ireland like his brother Raymond. He became Baron of Knocktopher, Kilkenny, Ireland. Griffin had four sons, Gilbert, Matthew, Raymond and Griffin. Gilbert FitzGriffin is cited as the 2nd B
1 CONC aron of Knocktopher, likely the eldest (surviving) son of Griffin, and the first owner of Knocktopher manor of whom there is certainty. Griffin was sheriff of Dungarvan, where he and Thomas FitzAnthony seemed to have worked togeth
1 CONC er in a high handed way, for between them they relieved the Bishop of Waterford of his property at Lismore, Ardmore, and Ardfinan. They parceled out a great deal of land among their friends -- not forgetting themselves. Gilbert di
1 CONC ed about 1203. The Knocktopher land stayed in the hands of Griffin’s descendants for many years.
