1 CONT MINOR, WAITE, FOSTER LINE - 21st ggrandfather
1 CONT
1 CONT 1200 - Llywelyn the Great seizes Anglesey
1 CONT 1257 - Assumes the title Prince of Wales, and establishes (1259) peace between England and Wa les
1 CONT [Timetables of History]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Last of the Welsh princes; slain while fighting for his dominions against Edward I. [The Rol l of Battle Abbey]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Gwynedd, 1205 -- Llywelyn is recognized as ruler by King John and marries Joan, the king's i llegitimate daughter.
1 CONT Wales, Aug 1211 -- King John forces the Welsh prince, Llywelyn of Gwynedd, to swear that hi s lands will revert to the crown of England should he die without a legitimate heir.
1 CONT Wales, 1215 -- Llywelyn of Gwynedd ousts Gwenwynwyn of Powys, a Welsh ally of King John, rega ining much land from English control. When Llywelyn was informed of the details of a secret v isit by Gwenwynwyn to the court of King Jo
1 CONC hn in England to discuss a joint expedition agains t Gwynedd, Llywelyn was incensed by this double-dealing of his father-in-law.  He openly disc ussed the matter with his wife Joan and publicly concluded that Gwenwynwyn was chiefl
1 CONC y to bla me.  This was not strictly true, sin the initial overture,
1 CONT financial and military preparatons were primarily coming from John. Nonetheless, Llywelyn ha d Gwenwynwyn tried in his absence for treachery.  He then summoned a host of Welsh lords an d marched south into Powys.  Returning hurrie
1 CONC dly from England, Gwenwynwyn was seriously hur t in a vain attempt to regain his kingdom.  He was forced to flee back to England, taking ref uge in Cheshire where he lay mortally wounded. [Chronicle of the Royal Family, p. 52-4]
1 CONT
1 CONT !South Wales, 1223 -- Llywelyn of Gwynedd is defeated by William Marshal, the Earl of Pembrok e.
1 CONT Gwynedd, 1230 -- Llywelyn of Gwynedd hangs William de Briouze, the lord of Brecon, after disc overing him in the chamber of his wife Joan.
1 CONT Gwynedd, 11 Apr 1240 -- Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, the ruler of Gwynedd, dies.
1 CONT [Chronicle of the Royal Family, p. 56]
1 CONT
1 CONT Built Castel y Bere. [Realm, No. 59, p. 48]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Iorwerth Drwyndwy and according to the rules of primogentiture, the true heir of Owai n Gwynedd. In 1194, when he was 22 years old, he defeated his uncle, Dafydd, near the estuar y of the Conwy. Llywelyn took possession of 
1 CONC the Perfeddwlad. He became ruler of the rest of G wynedd on the death of his cousin,
1 CONT Gruffudd, in 1200, and in England it was considered judicious to come to terms with him a s a counter-weight to the ambitions of Gwenwynwyn. He swore an oath of allegiance to John i n 1201 and m. Joan, the king's illegitimate dau.
1 CONC , in 1205. [History of Wales, p. 135]
1 CONT
1 CONT While Gwenwynwyn sought to take advantage of William de Breos' disgrace and thereby attracte d the King John's enmity, Llywelyn seized the opportunity to take possession of Powys Wenwynw yn and at the same time he drove Maelgwn, G
1 CONC wenwynwyn's ally, from northern Ceredition. This w as a bold demonstration of
1 CONT the determination of the ruler of Gwynedd to be master of Pura Wallia. Yet it was not withou t precedent, for Llywelyn could appeal to the story of the sons of Cunedda, to the fame of Ma elgwn Fawr, to his position as the heir of 
1 CONC the senior branch of the line of Rhodri and to TH E LIFE OF GRUFFUDD AP CYNAN. Owain
1 CONT Gwynedd had absorbed commotes on the borders of Powys, Ceredigion had been temporarily unde r his control and his forces had crossed the Teifi and had captured Gwyddgrug Castle, but Lly welyn's dealings with Gwenwynwyn and Maelgwn
1 CONC  prove that he was prepared to be considerably mo re forceful than his grandfather. In extending his authority, Llywelyn was careful not to ant agonize John, his overlord and father-in-law. He accompanied the king on his invasion 
1 CONC of Scot land in 1209; this was the repayment of an old debt, for Alexander I had joined Henry I in hi s attack upon Gruffudd ap Cynan in 1114. Nevertheless, by
1 CONT 1211, John had come to the conclusion that Llywelyn was among the most dangerous of his enemi es, he invaded Gwynedd and for the first time for a century the forces of the king of Englan d were encamped upon the banks of the Menai
1 CONC . Llywelyn had no choice but to yield; his authroi ty was restricted to Gwynedd Usch Conwy and he was obliged to recognize the king as his hei r should his marriage to Joan not produce a son. The lesser Welsh rulers abandoned Llyw
1 CONC elyn , thinking John was the lesser evil since he would be far away. They
1 CONT quickly realized they had underestimated King John and regrouped around Llywelyn. John, in hi s frustration with the Welsh, hanged a number of Welsh hostages, Maelgwn's 7-year-old son amo ng them; he offered a shilling apiece for 
1 CONC the heads of the Welsh insurgents and the king's ac counts for 1212 record a payment of six shilings for the heads of the men of Cadwallon ab Ifo r Bach of Senghennydd. [History, pp. 135-7]
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn was eager to exploit the divisions within the English kingdom, and the barons were a nxious for his support. Llywelyn's contributions to their campaign -- in particular his seizu re of Shrewsbury in May 1215 -- was one of
1 CONC  the factors which persuaded King John to seal th e MAGNA CARTA in June 1215. Between
1 CONT 1215-18, he and his allies captured a number of castles, Carmarthen and Cardigan among them ; they threatened such long-established centers of Marcher power as Haverfordwest, Swansea an d Brecon. Llwelyn aimed at a status superior
1 CONC  to that of mere war leader and he used the struc tures of feudalism to strengthen his authority. Gwenwynwyn had sworn him an oath of allegia n and had done homage to him, and, when he broke his oath in 1216, Powys Wenwynwyn was s
1 CONC eize d by Llywelyn in accordance with the rights of a feudal overlord. [History, p. 138]
1 CONT
1 CONT King John d. 1216 and was succeeded by his 9-year-old son, Henry III. Henry's advisors signe d a treaty with Llewellyn that permitted him to retain the castles of Carmarthen and Cardiga n until the king came of age, and as Gwenwyn
1 CONC wyn had died in 1216 it was agreed that Llywely n should hold Powys Wenwynwyn until Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn reached manhood. Within 5 years o f the Treaty, the officials of the English crown again sought to restrict Llywelyn to Gwy
1 CONC nedd . In 1223, the castles of Carmarthen and Cardigan were seized by
1 CONT William, earl of Pembroke and rector of the kingdom of England. At the same time, Hubert de B urgh, justiciar of England, ordered a castle to be built at Montgomery on a site more defensi ble than that of the old castle of the Mon
1 CONC tgomery family. But when Hubert sought to extend hi s authority over the commotes of Powys, he was utterly trounced by Llywelyn at Ceri in 1228 . Hubert won control of a number of Marcher Lordships. Llywelyn reacted in 1231 with a
1 CONC  campai gn in which he led his forces to regions where a Welsh army had not been seen for a century o r more. The army burned Brecon, marched through Glamorgan and destroyed Neath. When the confl ict came to an end in 1234 he succ
1 CONC eeded through the Peace of Middle in regaining the positio n he had won during the reign of John. He continued to rule Powys Wenwynwyn; he subjected th e lords of Powys Fadog and Deheubarth to his authroity through bonds of allegi
1 CONC ance and he obt ained possession of Gwrtheyrnion, Builth and Maelienydd. Perhaps in order to stress that he w as not merely ruler of Gwynedd, he adopted the title of Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowd onia, a style which emphas
1 CONC ized his unique position among the rulers of Wales. In 1220, King H enry III supported Llywelyn in his efforts to ensure that his son by Joan, Henry's nephew Daf ydd, should be heir to Gwynedd. That dispensation involved disinheri
1 CONC ting Llywelyn's eldest so n, Gruffudd, who had been born to Tangwystl before the marriage with Joan. [History, pp. 138- 9]
1 CONT
1 CONT Except for Gruffudd, all of Llywelyn's offspring married members of the Francigenae. So grea t was the desire of the de Breos family to ally with the prince that the uncle, the brother a nd the daughter of William de Breos m. memb
1 CONC ers of the house of Aberffraw. Yet the relationshi p between the two families did not prevent Llywelyn from hanging William de Breos in 1230 fo r excessive familiarity with Joan. The hanging was an audacious act and the lack of re
1 CONC actio n to it is proof of the power of Llywelyn and of the intensity of the desire of William's son s-in-law to obtain their share of his possessions, for William was the last of his branch o f the de Breoses. [History, pp. 140-41
1 CONC ]
1 CONT
1 CONT !The Genealogical Society of Utah; Gareth Rice;
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince of North Wales; b. 1173, d. Aberconway, 11 Apr 1240; m. Joan; father of Margaret. [Anc estral Roots, p. 31]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Iorwerth "Drwyndwn" ap Owain Gwynedd and Margaret ferch Madog; m. Tangwystl ferch Llyw arch. [GRS 3.03, Automated Archives, CD#100]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sometimes resided at Caernarfon as did his grandson, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd later in the 13th c entury. We know that around this dwelling of the princes there lay a typical Welsh maenor, wh ose territory included most of Llanbeblig,
1 CONC  with other neighboring hamlets, and whose townsmen , with agricultural holdings in the
1 CONT surrounding fields, were the predecessors of the English burgesses settled on their lands und er charter from King Edward I. [Caernarfon Castle, p. 5]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1218 Llywelyn invaded Powys and Gwenwynwyn ap Owain, his kinsman, was forced to flee to En gland where he died soon afterwards. On Llywelyn's death in 1240, Gwenwynwyn's son Gruffydd w as restored to Powys. [Powis Castle, p. 53
1 CONC ]
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn ab Iorwerth - Llywelyn the Great (d. 1240) - was the grandson of Owain Gwynedd. HI s rise to power was meteoric: like 'the swirl of a great windstorm', as one of his court poet s put it. Llywelyn swept aside uncles and co
1 CONC usins and secured recognition of his position fro m King John (1199-1216) in 1201. Indeed,
1 CONT in 1205, he m. John's illegitimate dau., Joan. But relations between Llywelyn and his father- in-law were strained more than once, and in 1211 the king -- outraged by Llywelyn's insubordi nation -- led two invasion forces into nor
1 CONC th Wales, penetrating with devastating effect dee p into Gwynedd. The following year, John assembled a huge army and seemed intent on complet e conquest. At the last moment, the king abandoned the campaign, with rumours of a threa
1 CONC t aga inst his life playing no small part in the decision. [Flint Castle/Ewloe Castle, p. 5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Criccieth Castle was probably built by the Normans at the end of the 11th century, but the ca stle soon fell into Welsh hands and may have continued in occupation until the 1230s when Lly welyn ab Iorwerth (Llywelyn the Great) tra
1 CONC nsferred the commotal centre to Criccieth, where h e built the present castle. [Criccieth Castle, p. 2]
1 CONT
1 CONT The earliest part of Criccieth Castle consists of the inner ward built on the highest point o f the hill by Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, probably sometime between 1230 and his death in 1240. Th e natural sea-cliff defences were supplemen
1 CONC ted by the construction of earthwork defences on t he mosre vulnerable landward side.
1 CONT    By the end of the second decade of the 13th century Llywelyn had become the premier nativ e lord in Wales, a position reflected in the marriage alliances he contracted with leading En glish lords of the Welsh March. Notable amo
1 CONC ng these were the marriages of his daus Gwladus t o Reginald de Braose and Helen to John
1 CONT the Scot (nephew and heir of Ranulf de Blundeville, earl of Chester). [Criccieth Castle, p. 1 1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince of Gwynedd, styled from 1230, Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowden; b. 1173, prob . at Dolwyddelan; defeated his uncle Dafydd I in 1194 and by 1203, regained all Gwynedd, beco ming overlord of all Welsh princes; m. 1205,
1 CONC  Joan, natural dau. of King John Lackland; d. 124 0 and was succeeded by his only
1 CONT legitimate son, Dafydd II. [Charlemagne & Others, Chart 2936]
1 CONT
1 CONT Although the Marcher lords had joined the general confederacy against King John, a great poli tical change began to make the support of the crown necessary to them even before his death . This was the revival of the Welsh power un
1 CONC der Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, largely in consequenc e of dissensions in England. After gaining complete possession of North Wales by reducing th e royal castles, Llywelyn, in 1215, when the barons were marching from Stamford on London
1 CONC , to ok Shrewsbury in conjunction with the bishop of Hereford, Giles de Braose, head of his grea t house since the death of his father William de Braose in exile. Llywelyn was then in allian ce with the barons, and clauses in his 
1 CONC interest were inserted in the Articles of the Barons a nd in Magna Carta (arts. 56-8), but in reality nothing could be less pleasing to the Marcher s than the establishment of a strong Welsh state. By the end of the year Llywelyn 
1 CONC had capture d all the great castles of South Wales from the borders of Pembrokeshire to the confines of t he earl of Gloucester's lordship of Glamorgan, and in the
1 CONT following year he occupied Upper Powys. Appalled by the prospect of a united Wales, Reginal d de Braose, who had succeeded to the family possessions on 26 May 1216, reconciled himself w ith the central government. In the meantime 
1 CONC towards the end of July John himself retreated be fore Louis of France from
1 CONT Corfe to the neighbourhood of Hereford, making the city his headquarters from 24 to 31 July a nd remaining in the valleys of the Severn and Wye for about a month, ravaging and destroying , and endeavouring without success to form 
1 CONC a league of Welsh princes. He exercised no permane nt influence on the situation, and in 1217 Llywelyn attacked Brecknock and forced De Braose t o make his submission. But when later in the same year the Treaty of Lambeth put an e
1 CONC nd for t he time to the civil war in England, Llywelyn was wise enough to desist
1 CONT from aggression. In March 1218 he and his chief nobles submitted themselves to their overlor d at Worcester.
1 CONT    The next 12 years were passed in fiftul attacks by Llywelyn varied by pacifications, but i n 1231 he renewed his inroads on a greater scale. After ravaging Montgomery and Brecon he ove rran Gwent, the modern Monmouthshire. Henr
1 CONC y III carried on a campaign in person without muc h success, making Hereford
1 CONT his headquarters. In 1233 the Earl Marshal, Richard Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, whose family h ad held Goodrich Castle in Herefordshire from within a century of the Conquest, quarrelled wi th Henry III and allied himself with Llywe
1 CONC lyn. He was supported by  Herefordshire magnate, Wa lter de Clifford, the lord of
1 CONT Clifford Castle. In November Henry came to Hereford, which he again made his headquarters fo r a campaign. He drove Clifford into exile and confiscated his lands, but in January 1233-4 , the Earl Marshal defeated the royal army in
1 CONC  Wales, and then in company with Llywelyn burne d Shrewsbury. This terminated
1 CONT operations for the time. Clifford obtained restitution of his lands in 1235. He was a man o f violence, and in 1250 was fined 1000 marks for compelling the king's messenger to eat the r oyal letters including the wax of the seal. 
1 CONC In 1235 also the Earl Marshal fell in Ireland, wh ile Llywelyn, perhaps fromadvancing age, wsa less aggressive during the six remaining years o f his reign.
1 CONT    In the meantime a new cause of strife arose between the Welsh and the Marcher lords. In 12 30 William de Braose, only son and heir of Reginald, contemplated alliance with Llywelyn. T o further the union he visited that prince i
1 CONC n order to arrange a marriage between Llywelyn' s osn Davydd and his own daughter Isabella. During the visit Llywelyn either detected or inve nted an intrigue between his wife Joan and De Braose, and caused the Marcher lord to be 
1 CONC hange d. Notwithstanding the execution of her father, Isabella, who probably was also in Llywelyn' s hands at the time, was married to Davydd. [The Victoria History of England's Counties: Here fordshire, p. 360-4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Research suggests that Margaret was the daughter of Llywelyn's second wife who was the daught er of the Lord of the Isles. This marriage was eventually voided due to Llywelyn's earlier ma rriage to a sister of Ranulph, Earl of Che
1 CONC ster. Llywelyn's first two marriages have been pret ty much overlooked in the literature. There were no surviving children born to the first marr iage.
1 CONT    Eventually Prince Llywelyn m. Joan, illegitimate daughter of King John of England. By tha t marriage, Llywelyn had four identifiable children:
1 CONT 1. David his heir
1 CONT 2. Ellen/Helen
1 CONT 3. Gladys Dhu
1 CONT 4. Susanna
1 CONT The daughter Gladys is often thought to have been illegitimate. However, her descendants, th e Mortimers, claimed to be the lineal heirs of Prince Llywelyn, following the failure of he r brother, David, to leave legitimate issue. 
1 CONC As such, one can only conclude that Gladys was l egitimate, being the oldest surviving daughter of Llywelyn and Joan to leave issue. [Dougla s Richardson <dcrdcr4@aol.com]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Iorwerth Drwyndwy ab Owain Gwynedd and Marared verch Madog ap Meredudd; m. 1205 Joan o f England; associated with tangwystyl Goch; father of:
1 CONT 1. Grufydd ap Llewelyn, d. 1244, son of Tangwystyl
1 CONT 2. Dafydd, Prince of Gwynedd, son of Joan
1 CONT 3. Helen who m. John, Earl of Chester
1 CONT 4. Gwladus Ddu who m. Reginald de Braose
1 CONT 5. Marared/Margaret who m.1 John de Braose and m.2 Walter Clifford
1 CONT 6. Gwenllian, d. 1281, m. William de Lacy
1 CONT    It was not until Louis of France was defeated and Henry III's reign was established in 121 7 that Reginald de Braose swore homage to the crown. Llywelyn was incensed by this seeming be trayal and laid siege to
1 CONT Reginald's castle at Brecon. Rhys and Owain, princes of Deheubarth, allies of Llywelyn and ne phws of Reginald, took the town of Builth. Reginals was pursued into Swansea were he surrende red the castle to
1 CONT Llywelyn and peace was restored
1 CONT    He built on the renewed confidence and emerging national consciousness inspired by the Lor d Rhys and became prince of Wales in all but name. His grandson Llywelyn ap gruffydd almost m irrored his achievements but died having l
1 CONC ost all to King Edward I.
1 CONT    King John offered conditional tolerance of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth's expansion and gave the p rince his illegitimate daughter Joan in marriage. Like Rhys, Llywelyn could play the suprem e diplomat and he introduced Norman ways to 
1 CONC his people when it suited the purposes of surviva l.
1 CONT    The power vacuum created by the fall of William de Braose was the making of Llywelyn. He s kilfully manipulated the rebellions against King John and welded six successive de Braose hei rs to his interest. William and Giles foug
1 CONC ht alongside him as allies. Reginald and John de Br aose both married daughters of Llywelyn. Reginald's son William pledged his daughter Isabell a to Dafydd ap Llywelyn and she in turn became a de Braose heiress.
1 CONT    After the hanging of William de Braose, Llywelyn wrote to Eva de Braose and her powerful b rother William Marshal. He warned Eva that he could not restrain his men because of the scand al and outrage (of finding William in his 
1 CONC wife's bedchamber). Lest any worse harm might happe n, would she still be willing for the marriage of Dafydd and Isabel to proceed? The little gi rl was apparently at his court, his son wanted Builth and his men were in the mood t
1 CONC o take it , and more, if the betrothal was not honoured. Amid this appalling
1 CONT hostility, the marriage of Dafydd and Isabel went ahead.
1 CONT    Llywelyn's letter to William Marshal, his "friend and dearest brother", said: "Your grac e will know that the magnates of our land might in no way control what they will do regardin g William de Braose, in view of the judgment 
1 CONC which they will have made as to how he engineere d our deceit by entering our chamber, bringing shame to us in the highest way. Whereby we as k your grace resolutely, concerning which we show the greatest trust, in so far as you m
1 CONC ay wi sh to show to us your will, whether you might have wished the alliance made between your niec e and our son David, whch if you might have wished it, on our part that alliance steadfastl y and persistently stands firm."
1 CONT    Llywelyn's ascendancy in his last years owed much to a brief alliance with Richard Marshal , Eva de Braose's younger brother. Richard inherited fantastic wealth and power. His hostilit y to Henry III had major repercussions and
1 CONC  Llywelyn benefited from a peace treaty negotiate d with the king at this time. As always, Llywelyn took advantage of English disunity, Richar d died in Ireland in 1234 and the king was suspected of treachery. ["Barons de Braose" 
1 CONC <http: //freespace.virgin.net/doug.thompson/BraoseWeb/stage.htm]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1231 Llywelyn ap Iorwerth attacked both Hay and Brecon, burning the towns and destroying t he castles. In a second campaign, two years later, he completely overran Brycheiniog, ruthles sly destroyed the castles, and 'plundering
1 CONC  and pillaging every place'. Brecon Castle was besi eged with catapults and engines for a month, while at the same time he 'turned the whole tow n into ashes'. [The Castles of Wales, p. 41]
1 CONT
1 CONT Castell y Bere, Gwynedd was the castle of the Welsh prince, Llywelyn ap Iorwerth. Like two o f his other castles, Cricieth and Dolbadarn, it was later adapted to suit the needs of Edwar d I, but what remains of Castell y Bere date
1 CONC s from Llywelyn's building of 1221. This, accordi ng to BRUT, was the result of his discontent with his son, Gruffudd, from whom he took away t he cantref of Meirionnydd and the commote of Ardudwy, and 'began to build a castle the
1 CONC re fo r himself'.
1 CONT    Castell ye Bere was no Welsh attempt to offset the English onslaught; it was rather a chal lenge to any compatriot bold enough to defy Llywelyn's territorial supremacy. The plan of th e castle was determined by the contours of 
1 CONC the narrow rock on which it was built, utilizing e very possible naturally defensive vantage point. [The Castles of Wales, p. 76-77][3210710.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, (Llywelyn Fawr or Llywelyn the Great: 1173-1240) c an justly be called the greatest of the medieval Welsh kings. By 120 2, he had taken advantage of the quarrels of his uncles and had become rul er of the kin
1 CONC gdom of Gwynedd. Further successes in Deheubarth, after the d eath of the Lord Rhys, and in Powys made him the most powerful of all t he Welsh rulers, though it was Gruffudd ap Llwyelyn who is remembered as t he only native monarc
1 CONC h to rule over all of Wales. In 1205 he married Ki ng John of England's daughter, Joan, and helped his father-in-law in a cam paign against King William of Scotland. By 1210 the situation had chang ed dramatically. John invaded Gw
1 CONC ynedd, Llywelyn being forced to retre at to the mountainous areas to the West.
1 CONT
1 CONT When John found himself embroiled in struggles with his Barons and the Pop e, Llywelyn was able to reassert his authority in North Wales. In 121 6, he presided over a Welsh Parliament at which he was acknowledged as ove rlord of a
1 CONC ll other native Welsh rulers, a position confirmed at the Pea ce of Worcester in 1218, by Henry III. The achievements of Llywelyn were m any. Before he died in 1240 as a monk at Aberconwy monastery, he had inspi red a revision of 
1 CONC the Laws of Hywel Dda. His reorganization of the adminis trative machhinery of Wales, his maintenance of cordial relations with t he Pope and the English Church, and the bringing of an era of peace and pr osperity to the territori
1 CONC es under his control were all testaments to his r emarkable diplomatic and military skills. It is no wonder that his achieve ments on behalf of his people were widely celebrated by Welsh poets.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Owain Gwynedd in 1170, southern Wales, under Rhys ap Gr uffydd (The Lord Rhys) in Dyfed, became dominant, but at the end of the ce ntury Gwynedd once again had a powerful prince. Llywelyn ap Iorwerth emerg ed as
1 CONC  ruler in 1200, after a fierce family struggle. At his death in 12 40 he was undisputed ruler of all pura Wallia, corresponded on equal ter ms with Phillip Augustus of France, and had hanged one of the most powerf ul Marcher lords
1 CONC  for undue familiarity with his wife, the daughter of Ki ng John of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn's reign saw the construction of the first sophisticated stone cas tles in Wales built by her native rulers. Sited at strategic points periph eral to Snowdonia, often on his summer grazing lands, they guarded the sto re-cu
1 CONC pboard of his kingdom. Simple towers like that in Dinas Emrys, may be long to this program, but his best castles are much more sophisticated, a nd incorporate details of construction and advanced design being used by s ome of his 
1 CONC friends - and enemies - among the Marcher lords. Dolwyddelan (b elow right) Dolbadarn, Criccieth, and Castell y Bere in south Meirionny dd still stand as witness to the energy and resources put into the defen ce of his kingdom aft
1 CONC er the disastrous attack which it suffered from his f ather-in-law, King John, in 1211. These castles were probably built betwe en 1220 and 1230. The plans vary: towers are round, square or D-shape d, a ground plan especially char
1 CONC acteristic of this series. The well-sit ed castle at Criccieth is undoubtedly the best design, but it might have b een surpassed by Degannwy had this crucial site not been destroyed by h is son Daffydd as part of a scorched earth 
1 CONC policy.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Llywelyn's death the kingdom and Gwynedd's pre-eminence were again thre atened by a disputed inheritance and renewed interest from England. Only w hen his grandson, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, overcame family rivals in 1255 cou ld th
1 CONC e political legacy of Llywelyn the Great begin to be rebuilt by a com bination of raids and alliances. Llywelyn's position as effective rul er of all Wales as far south as Caerphilly was eventually recognized by He nry III, in the
1 CONC  Treaty of Montgomery (1267). This acknowledged the title " Prince of Wales," and the concept of Wales as a unified state, never expli citly recognized before. The Prince of Wales was a vassal of the king of E ngland but was, in e
1 CONC ffect, an independent political power. His grandfather 's castles still provided the main defence of the kingdom; Llywelyn reinfo rced and enlarged them, but founded only one new castle at Dolforwyn ne ar Welshpool, far outside hi
1 CONC s patrimony, begun in 1273 when clouds were al ready gathering on the horizon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Additional Note: The attribution of some of the children to the two mothe rs is uncertain but Grufydd is known to be Tangwystyl's son and Dafydd, Gw ladus and Helen are Joan's children.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the course of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, the Welsh we re ruled by many ruthless and able men: Tywysogion, or Princes. The most f orceful and intelligent of these rulers was surely the prince of Gwyned d, Llywelyn
1 CONC  ab Iorwerth (d. 1240), whose castle, Dolwyddelyn, is pictur ed here. Llywelyn was the only prince of the age to earn from posterity t he cognomen Fawr: the Great. Like his predecessors and contemporaries, Lly welyn had to simulta
1 CONC neously find a way to coexist with his vastly more wea lthy and powerful neighbor to the east, and at the same time extend his he gemony over the lesser princes of Wales. The Angevin Kings of England a nd their marcher barons were
1 CONC  powerful and dangerous, and the princes of Wa les were quarrelsome and resistant to any of their number who would attem pt to impose his authority on the rest. In spite of this volatile politic al atmosphere, Llywelyn Fawr came c
1 CONC loser than any of his predecessors to c reating a Welsh polity which could withstand the encroachments of the marc her lords and the impositions of the English King. But it was not an ea sy task and even Llywelyn the Great had his
1 CONC  setbacks.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the spring of 1230, Llywelyn's wife Joan, the illegitimate daught er of King John of England, was discovered "in the prince's chamber" wi th William de Braose, a powerful Anglo-Norman marcher lord. The two were i mprisoned and 
1 CONC soon William was executed. The affair and the subsequent exe cution created tensions between Llywelyn and the princes of southern Wale s, as well as jeopardizing his good relations with both the royal governme nt and the Braose fa
1 CONC mily with whom Llywelyn had been negotiating a marria ge for his son Dafydd. These unresolved tensions and royal inattention ult imately led to the outbreak of war in south Wales in 1231.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn the Great
1 CONT Source: Wikipedia, http://www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llywelyn_the_Great
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince of Gwynedd and effectively Prince of Wales. Also known as "Llywel yn Fawr" (Llywelyn the Great) and "Llywelyn ap Iorwerth" (patronymic).
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn was born in 1173, the grandson of Owain Gwynedd. His father havi ng been disposed of by Owain's other sons after Owain's death, Llywelyn w as left to fend for himself until, as a youth, his natural superiority ma de itsel
1 CONC f felt and he defeated his uncles to take Gwynedd for himsel f. He consolidated this conquest in 1205 by marrying Joan, illegitimate da ughter of King John I of England. Despite occasional disputes with John a nd his successor, He
1 CONC nry III of England, Llywelyn succeeded in maintaini ng Welsh independence. After a long struggle, he gained the ascendancy ov er his main rival, Gwenwynwyn of Powys so as to unite the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage to Joan has an unusual history. Following the birth of a legi timate heir, Dafydd ap Llywelyn, and a daughter, Elen (who was married o ff to the Norman Earl of Chester), Joan committed adultery with Willi am de Braose
1 CONC  or Breos, a Norman noble of south Wales who had allied himse lf with Llywelyn by the marriage of his daughter, Isabella, to Llywelyn 's son, Dafydd. On learning of the affair in 1230, Llywelyn executed de Br aose and Joan was imp
1 CONC risoned. Some time later, she was forgiven and restor ed to her position as princess, dying in 1237.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn died in 1240 and a power struggle arose between his legitimate so n, Dafydd, and his older, illegitimate son, Gruffydd, who according to Wel sh law had equal rights of inheritance. Llywelyn had departed from traditi on by
1 CONC  naming Dafydd as heir, 
